WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.590
- The following is a conversation
with Andrew Scull, a historian of

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.630
psychiatry and mental health. He
has authored many books that I

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.520
highly recommend including
Madness in Civilization:

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.840
A Cultural History of Insanity
from the Bible to Freud, from

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.770
the Madhouse to Modern Medicine
and Desperate Remedies:

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.690
Psychiatry's Turbulent Quest
to Cure Mental Illness.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.920
Andrew Scull has spent decades
studying how societies have understood

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.780
madness, how psychiatry rose
to authority, and how often

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.580
that authority was used with
false confidence and catastrophic

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.500
consequences. In this conversation
we'll trace the long arc from the

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.260
asylum era to eugenics, from
lobotomy and insulin coma

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.220
therapy to electroconvulsive
therapy, psychoanalysis,

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.400
antipsychotics, antidepressants
and the modern crisis

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.360
of mental health. It is
in part a story about the

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.200
terrifying history of bad
ideas in medicine but it is

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.070
also about the fascinating
mystery of the human mind and

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.460
about the difficult
journey to understand it.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.960
This is a Lex Fridman podcast. To support
it please check out our sponsors in the

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.560
description where you can also
find links to contact me, ask

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.380
questions, give feedback and so on. And
now dear friends here's Andrew Scull.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.360
Is it fair to characterize your view
on psychiatry and mental illness

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.600
as that there's a crisis in modern
psychiatry? We have made some progress-

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:30.960
- Yes

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.960
- ... over the past century but mostly
we still are not good at treating

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:42.660
mental illness either via the drugs or
talk therapy meaning psychopharmacology or

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.980
psychotherapy or as you put it
the brain or the mind route.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:52.020
So let's start at the end of our
story. Let's start at where we

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.540
stand before we go into the rich history
that you so eloquently write about.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.600
- So psychiatry is a
profession that tries to

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.060
deal with an enormously
complicated thing. The

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.620
human mind, the human emotions, the human
ability to attempt to understand the world

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.260
and in particular obviously
it focuses on people where

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.900
our common sense approach to
the world seems to break down.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.130
People whose emotional life
is filled with turmoil,

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.600
people whose ability to
relate to others is badly

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.580
damaged, people who see things
in the world that the rest of

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.340
us simply think aren't
there. They're illusions,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:47.260
they're hallucinations, they're
delusions and this is a subject that has

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.440
occupied some very clever
minds over the years

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:58.280
and there's no question that in the course
of at least the last three quarters of a

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.120
century there has been some limited

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:09.130
progress in dealing with the problems that
mental illness creates. Some of that is

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.380
confined to the milder
forms of mental distress;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.609
the more serious forms of psychosis and

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.680
breakdown of emotional control
those are areas where I think

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.200
again there's been some progress
but it's easy to overstate how much

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.080
of that there's been. As we'll see probably
later in our conversation today the

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.100
advent of modern psychopharmacology which

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.920
occurred in the early nineteen
fifties was a serendipitous

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.840
event. It wasn't planned for.
It happened almost by accident

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.600
and it did mark in some
ways an advance over some

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.950
of the things that psychiatry
had engaged in before that

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:59.049
and no question for some people that
revolution and a parallel revolution

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.000
in the psychotherapeutic realm have

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.480
created some advance for
patients and we should not

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.520
minimize that. What we
have available to us

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.390
are symptomatic treatments
not cures. We don't have

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.450
a psychiatric penicillin for
any of the conditions we're

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.280
gonna be talking about. That doesn't
mean we can't do some things that

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:28.820
help but the help is quite limited
and it's important to understand

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:32.400
both the ways in which we
have progressed and the

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.730
limits of that progress and also to
understand that when we treat something

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.160
sometimes we create new
problems what we call

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.260
iatrogenic problems, things caused
by the interventions that we use.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.240
That's true of some
psychotherapeutic interventions and

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.360
most certainly true of the drugs we use
to treat mental illness these days.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:06.720
So for example in treating PTSD
we often get patients to confront

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:14.220
the episode -- the trauma which
provoked their distress and that is

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.800
often a very, very fraught
process and for many

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:22.840
patients it actually makes things worse.
For some patients it makes them better.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.420
So that's a situation where
you could see problems

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.320
With both antipsychotics and
antidepressants, the two

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:39.240
main classes of psychotropic drugs that
we use, um, they're at best partially

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.060
effective and they don't work for a

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.820
significant fraction of patients who
are given them and one of the big

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.600
problems psychiatry faces is that

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.220
psychiatrists don't know in advance
who's going to respond well

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.040
to the drugs, who's
going to respond badly,

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.250
for which group of patients in
the middle the side effects and

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.140
the main effects, if that's what we want
to call them, the therapeutic effect

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.960
are finely balanced and making those
judgment calls about what to do

00:06:12.864 --> 00:06:16.034
are very, very difficult.
Now in terms of the

00:06:16.064 --> 00:06:21.614
crisis psychiatry faces at the
moment as I see it there are

00:06:23.064 --> 00:06:28.823
a number of strands that point to
this. Psychiatry's diagnostic system,

00:06:29.704 --> 00:06:33.574
that is still used -- I mean,
the fundamental basis of,

00:06:33.574 --> 00:06:40.604
of psychiatric diagnosis today was really
first formulated in 1980 with the third

00:06:40.644 --> 00:06:44.684
edition of The Diagnostic and
Statistical Manual of a profession --

00:06:44.964 --> 00:06:45.744
- DSM-III

00:06:45.944 --> 00:06:53.184
- ... DSM-III and then there's been
DSM-III-R, DSM-IV, DSM-IV-TR, and now DSM

00:06:53.344 --> 00:06:56.904
5. Finally not with a
Roman numeral but with

00:06:57.324 --> 00:07:01.284
with an Arabic numeral.
So they thought with DSM

00:07:01.344 --> 00:07:04.784
5 when they needed to modify
it it would be like a piece of

00:07:04.864 --> 00:07:08.814
software. You have Windows
10, Windows 11, and

00:07:08.944 --> 00:07:12.544
so on right? Now that diagnostic system

00:07:13.044 --> 00:07:16.684
came into being because
psychiatrists had a very hard time

00:07:16.724 --> 00:07:20.664
agreeing with one another about what
was wrong with somebody, and that

00:07:20.724 --> 00:07:24.694
became embarrassingly clear in
-- first in the professional

00:07:24.704 --> 00:07:28.574
literature which outsiders
didn't read and then via a

00:07:28.644 --> 00:07:32.364
very famous study that's a
scientific fraud by David

00:07:32.404 --> 00:07:36.164
Rosenhan called On Being Sane
in Insane Places where he

00:07:36.224 --> 00:07:39.244
claimed to have sent in pseudo
patients to the hospital

00:07:40.084 --> 00:07:44.054
and they all were diagnosed as schizo.
All but one of them diagnosed as

00:07:44.084 --> 00:07:47.964
schizophrenic the other as somebody
with bipolar disorder and they

00:07:47.984 --> 00:07:51.694
were fake patients. So
almost in a panic after

00:07:51.784 --> 00:07:55.524
that study appeared in Science and because

00:07:55.744 --> 00:07:58.764
there was an abundant
professional literature from the

00:07:58.784 --> 00:08:04.464
1960s showing the same thing that
diagnosis was a very erratic process.

00:08:05.324 --> 00:08:09.884
The DSM-III task force led by Robert
Spitzer who was then at Columbia

00:08:10.684 --> 00:08:14.564
was explicitly set up to try
to create a sort of tick the

00:08:14.584 --> 00:08:18.244
boxes approach to deciding which box a

00:08:18.284 --> 00:08:22.204
patient belonged in. Were you
schizophrenic? Were you this type of

00:08:22.224 --> 00:08:24.604
schizophrenic or that type
of schizophrenic? Were you

00:08:25.524 --> 00:08:30.364
manic depressive or bipolar? Did you
have various forms of depression?

00:08:32.004 --> 00:08:39.864
And to construct those boxes what they
relied upon was a list of symptoms and if

00:08:39.924 --> 00:08:43.364
you had more than a certain
number If you had more than

00:08:43.664 --> 00:08:48.444
six of ten Six or ten or more
symptoms of a certain sort

00:08:49.464 --> 00:08:53.244
you could be diagnosed with major
depression for example. So that

00:08:53.304 --> 00:08:58.664
system came into being.
It was partially embedded

00:08:58.744 --> 00:09:02.414
because it appealed to drug
companies who were developing

00:09:02.424 --> 00:09:05.104
drugs to treat these various disorders.

00:09:06.024 --> 00:09:10.364
It appealed to insurance companies 'cause
it gave them a stable base to look at.

00:09:11.364 --> 00:09:17.264
It appealed to, as diagnosis always does
to patients and their families who are

00:09:18.464 --> 00:09:25.254
scrambling to deal with these enormous
upsets in the mental life of either

00:09:25.384 --> 00:09:27.834
themselves or a family member.

00:09:28.764 --> 00:09:32.224
So provided some sense of
certainty around diagnosis.

00:09:33.044 --> 00:09:36.894
But that was always based simply on
symptoms in the way an eighteenth

00:09:36.984 --> 00:09:40.454
century doctor might
diagnose dropsy or diagnose,

00:09:41.244 --> 00:09:45.064
Some other kind of disor---
Fever for example well

00:09:46.064 --> 00:09:50.044
fever and other diseases they
talked about in the eighteenth

00:09:50.104 --> 00:09:54.564
century are really a constellation of
very different things under one label.

00:09:55.504 --> 00:09:59.464
And I think that's what is
turning out to be true of the

00:09:59.504 --> 00:10:02.904
DSM labels. But that approach

00:10:03.404 --> 00:10:07.264
was really all that psychiatry
could come up with if it

00:10:07.384 --> 00:10:11.304
wanted to make sure whether you
were in Walla Walla or New York or

00:10:11.724 --> 00:10:15.644
San Francisco or Atlanta you
reached the same conclusion faced

00:10:15.724 --> 00:10:20.344
by the same patient. That you had
a reliable diagnostic system.

00:10:21.284 --> 00:10:24.884
That didn't mean necessarily it was
a valid diagnostic system if you

00:10:24.964 --> 00:10:28.204
understand the distinction
between validity and

00:10:28.244 --> 00:10:32.163
reliability. Reliability means
you and I faced with the

00:10:32.204 --> 00:10:35.964
same sets of facts reach
the same conclusion but

00:10:36.004 --> 00:10:39.764
that conclusion may or may not reflect the

00:10:39.824 --> 00:10:43.804
underlying reality of things
right? So you had this

00:10:43.844 --> 00:10:47.394
diagnostic system and
it went through various

00:10:47.464 --> 00:10:51.444
iterations each time it went
through an iteration the number

00:10:51.524 --> 00:10:59.284
of possible psychiatric disorders you
could get grew and grew and grew and it

00:10:59.364 --> 00:11:03.194
became difficult to believe in
some of those categories shall

00:11:03.224 --> 00:11:06.484
we say. That they were
really illnesses rather

00:11:07.124 --> 00:11:11.864
th-than some sorta social
construct. But beyond that um,

00:11:13.004 --> 00:11:18.844
psychiatrists wanted to be more
like other medical doctors to root

00:11:20.344 --> 00:11:23.784
their diagnoses in an
understanding of the underlying

00:11:24.064 --> 00:11:27.724
pathology of the disorder.
What was it that caused

00:11:27.824 --> 00:11:31.804
people to become
schizophrenic? The sense was

00:11:31.884 --> 00:11:35.844
if we could grasp that we'd have a
better handle on how to attack it How

00:11:35.924 --> 00:11:45.314
To treat it. And when uh, DSM-5 was mooted
the fifth edition in the early 2000s

00:11:46.084 --> 00:11:49.934
NIMH then under the leadership
of Steven Hyman who's at

00:11:49.964 --> 00:11:53.844
Harvard now and succeeded by Thomas Insel

00:11:54.284 --> 00:11:58.144
who ruled NIMH for about
thirteen years. The

00:11:58.204 --> 00:12:02.064
two of them had pushed
psychiatry and psychiatric

00:12:02.084 --> 00:12:04.934
research in a very particular direction.

00:12:06.244 --> 00:12:11.244
On the one hand, towards understanding
the genetics of mental illness,

00:12:12.084 --> 00:12:18.680
and on the other, to looking at what the
new discipline of neuroscience could

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:22.640
contribute to understanding things.
Both of them were heavily invested

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:28.000
in the idea that mental
illness was brain disease.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.000
And if it was brain disease, then
the question was what was making the

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:35.620
mechanism here go awry? What was

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:39.660
causing people's emotions or
their cognitive skills or

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:44.000
their sense of the world
to become so disordered?

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:48.820
And they invested a lot of money in
that approach. When Insel stepped

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:51.860
down, he gave an interview
actually to somebody at MIT

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:56.260
and he's repeated it since in a
book he wrote about his experiences

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:00.280
where he said, "Well, you know,
as I look back on my 13 years,

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:05.540
I funded an enormous amount of scientific...
really cool scientific research.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:10.160
I funded geneticists and I
funded neuroscientists and

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:12.640
we-- they did a lot of
really interesting science."

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.840
And after spending 20 billion
dollars, the lot of the

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:22.400
mentally ill has improved
not one bit right?"

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.380
Which was a pretty devastating
statement, I thought.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.680
- And NIMH is National
Institute of Mental Health.

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:30.730
- National Institute of Mental Health,
yes. I shouldn't resort to jargon.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:36.560
But the National Institute of Mental
Health had been founded in the late

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:43.200
1940s with the goal of improving, doing
basic research training people in the field

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:47.520
advancing the care of the mentally
ill and producing obviously

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:54.390
cures and advances. Uh, and
its mission has varied widely

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:58.060
over the years since but starting in the

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:02.320
in the-- in the 1990s—the period
when the first George Bush

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:06.220
declared the decade of the brain—uh, NIMH

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:10.280
increasingly focused on the idea
that mental illness was purely a

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.880
brain disease and there's something
to that and there's also some

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:15.920
mistake in thinking about it that way.

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.590
- This is, by the way, as we will
talk about this distinction

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:24.070
between seeing mental health,
the maladies of the human mind,

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.040
as a problem of the brain, like
neurobiology, neuroscience, versus

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:34.020
the problem of the mind, which is more in
the cognitive, psychotherapy, these more,

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:38.700
less amenable to scientific
rigor. So I think what's

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:42.340
appealing about studying the brain and
neurobiology, neurosciences, there's

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.520
data. Yes. It's more
rigorous. You can do science.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:50.440
- It’s the kind of thing that really
appealed to medical school deans because

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:57.830
once NIH and the drug companies
too were funding basic research on

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:05.080
neurobiology and basic research on
genetics, the money flooded in. Um, and

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:09.040
psychiatry, which had been something of
an orphan, became much more popular,

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:13.260
- and we should say that this is something you
write a lot about. There are all these factors to

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:19.830
consider. So there's cultural elements,
political, public policy elements. Then at

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:24.960
a certain point, drug companies,
insurance companies come in, of course.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:29.480
all human beings around somebody who's

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:35.760
suffering with a mental health issue, so family
factors, that's connected, cultural stuff.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:42.420
- it's a very complicated area and
oversimplification is a real problem I think.

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:49.960
Uh, and I think even that dichotomy
you just drew between, um, the

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.529
brain and the social or psychological,
some mix of those things,

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:59.340
I think in some ways that's a category mistake,
that's a mistaken way of looking at the

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:07.430
world, because the brain you have
today, you're not born with. Human

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:11.220
brains are remarkably plastic things.
They develop in response to the

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:18.440
environment. Social, psychological
are embedded in our brains.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:26.410
Rigid separation, saying one or the
other—no. Abundant evidence from

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:33.380
epidemiology that social factors play an
important role in the development of illness.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:39.100
- Nevertheless, history of psychotherapy,
for example, subfields...

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:46.400
human mind is complicated, all
these factors. To say anything

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:52.990
helpful, you have to simplify. Freud famously
simplified a lot towards a particular view of the human

00:16:53.080 --> 00:17:00.720
mind. That simplification, actually,
we'll talk about if it's correct

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.560
in a deeper sense, was productive.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:12.100
- Yes. In fact, whenever you're trying to
deal with a very complicated set of issues,

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:16.680
you have to simplify. You
have to make heuristic

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:20.280
decisions about what you're going
to neglect and what you're gonna

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:27.500
emphasize. But what I'm trying to say, well,
you asked me about the crisis in psychiatry,

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:33.650
and the simplest way for me to
encapsulate the issue and the

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:40.260
problem as I see it that's arisen—one
of the problems—is a quote by Leon

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:46.180
Eisenberg, who had a very long
career at Harvard, and Eisenberg

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:50.020
said towards the end of his
career, he said, "When I entered

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:56.980
psychiatry, it was brainless psychiatry,
leaving it mindless psychiatry." Yeah,

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:03.480
sums up the way the field to
a large degree has moved.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:12.400
In the process, insights are lost, though
in other ways, things are being gained. Um,

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:17.860
Freudians tended to neglect the
biological, to suggest the brain

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:22.520
as a biological organism was
not something we needed to

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:26.480
worry about. We worried at the level of a

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:32.770
psychological model of how our
minds work. And all too often,

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:38.040
not exclusively because some of
the psychiatrists I'm friends with

00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:45.540
are very sophisticated men and women and
do grasp that you can't move in these

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:50.539
bipolar directions. You've got
to meet somewhere in the middle.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:57.840
But that sort of got lost, I
think, in all the enthusiasm for

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:02.640
neuroscience. I mean, when
antipsychotic drugs were discovered

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:08.580
in the early 1950s, we had no clue of
how they worked. And one of the things

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:13.480
that they helped us spawn as
people began to say, "Well,

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.660
why did these drugs
work? What did they do?"

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:20.480
Was to begin to understand that
the brain wasn't just a set of

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:24.200
electrical signals, as had been
thought in the first half of the 20th

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:28.120
century and before. But
rather there was this very

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:32.110
interesting chemical soup running
around in our brains with

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:35.780
neurotransmitters that help
the brain work the way it did

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:39.020
and perhaps explained why it went awry.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:43.940
So it was one of the many factors
that gave rise to neuroscience. The

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:47.920
first neuroscience conventions were
in the 1970s, and they attracted a

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:51.380
few hundred people. Now
it's tens of thousands of

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:55.360
neuroscientists. It's
a huge enterprise. So

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:59.260
diagnosis has come under
threat. The categories that

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:03.020
we have all become familiar
with, that we're told are real

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:07.540
diseases like bipolar disorder,
schizophrenia, major depression,

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:12.600
are beginning to falter,
and we're beginning to

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.340
worry about whether
those diagnoses are real

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:19.940
ways of thinking about the world
and may in fact mislead us.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.480
Because if we think there's something
called schizophrenia or the

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:27.300
schizophrenias, as the inventor
of that term put it, if we think

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:31.230
there's something like that and
we try to research where it

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:37.630
comes from, but that's not really what's
going on, obviously we're probably

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:41.460
gonna not make the progress we
could be making if we had a better

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:43.790
diagnostic system. And that's what

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:47.720
DSM-V thought it was gonna
be able to do and discovered

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:51.420
it couldn't. So it stayed basically
with the symptomatic approach.

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.520
- If we can just really brief -

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:57.320
I would love to talk a little bit more
about the DSM, but just to give a history

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:04.960
here, looking it up on Perplexity, DSM
One in 1952 was a 32 page pamphlet-

00:21:04.969 --> 00:21:09.720
with 106 diagnoses heavily influenced
by psychodynamic concepts-

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:14.080
and etiology using broad, often
vague categories like reactions.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:21.460
DSM Two in 1968 expanded to 185
diagnoses still psychodynamically

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:25.400
flavored and reliability poor,
but added more attention

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:29.340
to childhood disorders and later
removed homosexuality as a disorder in

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:33.220
1974 printing. Then to
the revolution, as you

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:37.000
mentioned, the DSM Three in
1980 introduced explicit

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:40.600
symptom-based diagnostic criteria, a multi

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:43.960
axial assessment system, and an officially

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:49.890
atheoretical stance about causes aiming to
improve reliability and research utility.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:53.520
And then DSM Three R in
1987 revised criterion

00:21:53.560 --> 00:22:01.280
expanded to around two 97 diagnoses.
DSM Four in '94 and DSM Four TR in

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:05.910
2000 focused on literature
driven empirical revisions,-

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:10.260
added and deleted some disorders
increased coordination with

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:17.340
ICD 10 and goes on. And then
finally in 2013 DSM Five came

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.320
out which eliminated the multiaxial system

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:24.840
integrated most information
into a single nonaxial

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:28.790
diagnostic list than separate notations
for psychological and medical

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:32.740
factors. It reorganized chapters e.g.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:36.660
Neurodevelopmental, obsessive-compulsive
related, trauma and

00:22:36.819 --> 00:22:40.760
stress related disorders introduce
new and refined entities and so

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:48.950
on. But big picture trajectory conceptually
the DSM moved from cause focused and

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:55.060
psychoanalytical to symptom-based,
theoretical, and reliability driven

00:22:56.380 --> 00:23:00.260
over time. There has been growth in the
number of granularity of categories

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:04.100
closer alignment with
ICD ongoing controversy

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:07.470
over medicalization, validity,
and the balance between

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:11.380
categorical and dimensional
approaches. Yes. It

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:15.220
feels like that description doesn't
necessarily fully get to the

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:21.860
core of why the DSM five doesn't quite
get the full scope of the problem

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:26.240
- You invest a ton of money. You invest-

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:30.180
...$20 billion-- More than $20
billion in a particular approach.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:34.500
And at the time you're constructing
this, you say, "Finally,

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:38.420
because of all the work that's
being done in genetics, we now have

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:40.320
decoded the human genome.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:48.040
We have PCR so we can chop it up and we
can look at bits of it. We have a lot more

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:52.680
understanding of the role of
neurotransmission in the brain.

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:59.320
Surely we're going to be able
to recast our diagnostic system

00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:04.139
based on underlying pathology
so that we won't be

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:08.150
worrying about symptoms anymore.
We'll be looking at the actual

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:11.570
underlying pathological
changes that have taken place.

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:16.300
But in the event, it turned out
they couldn't do that. By about

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:20.400
2008, they had thrown up their
hands and said, "No, we're going

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:23.460
to have to continue to refine,

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:30.660
rely upon the same basic approach
that we developed in 1980." And

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:37.340
when that document came out, and there'd
been a lot of criticism, some of it from

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:45.460
Robert Spitzer, who had been largely in
control of DSM-III and DSM-III-R, and then

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:49.480
his successor Allen
Frances, who'd run DSM-IV,

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:54.370
they both were fiercely critical about
what was going on and said it was

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:58.130
being done in the dark and it was done
in secrecy and it wasn't scientific.

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:00.690
And when it was about to come out,

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.900
Thomas Insel and Steven Hyman,
the two then existing and the

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:08.810
preceding director of the National
Institute of Mental Health, denounced the

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:11.920
document as unscientific and useless.

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:16.540
So if you're talking about a crisis,
that's a crisis for the field. If its

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:21.460
diagnostic system is being dissed
by leading figures like that,

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:28.780
What else was a problem? The
difficulties with the drug existing

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:33.500
therapies, particularly, had
become more and more manifest.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:41.540
To make matters worse, partly because
Big Pharma had sometimes behaved,

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:49.800
shall we say, rather unethically sometimes
with these drugs, it had hidden things

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:58.460
that undercut their claims. It had
manufactured studies. It had manipulated data.

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:02.780
And it got caught out, and it paid
billions of dollars in damages

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:06.140
for some of the tricks it had got up to.

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:10.240
Not just in psychiatry—I
mean Vioxx, for example, the

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:13.820
painkiller, was another huge
scandal, and it was a $5 billion

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:17.840
settlement. But the drug companies had

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:21.780
suffered some reputational
damage. Beyond that, they didn't

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:25.500
have any real clues. Unfortunately,
neuroscience hadn't thrown up new

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:28.900
targets for different
forms of drug development

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:33.500
And they decided they could make more
money with spending their money on

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:36.490
research on other diseases,
not mental illness

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:41.340
A crisis was you were stuck with
a set of drugs that Steve Hyman

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:44.900
says, "Aren't really any
advance on the ones by

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:48.520
accident we found in the
fifties. Mm-hmm. And

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:52.420
there isn't new research unless
it's small startups going

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:57.140
on to develop new ones, so
you have an increasing sense

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:07.089
Your diagnostic process is falling apart, your
drug treatments' limitations are becoming

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:14.100
more manifest and, um, as well for people

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:18.520
with serious mental illness. Public
policy has screwed things up badly.

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:26.040
So if you, a person with serious
mental illness, you will

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:31.720
die on average fifteen to twenty-five
years before the rest of us. That

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:36.340
gap has been growing rather than
diminishing. So, that's not a good

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:38.900
thing. It's not something I want to

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:42.830
lay entirely at the door of psychiatry.
So don't get me wrong, I think a

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:50.420
lot of this is public policy that has really
abandoned treatment for the seriously

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:54.440
mentally ill. And you see it on
the streets of our cities. You

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:57.100
see the sidewalk psychotics.
You see people who are

00:27:57.220 --> 00:28:04.120
cycling between brief periods of inpatient
care, uh, the gutter, or the flop

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:07.700
house and the jail. You so the three

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:11.860
largest centers of inpatient
psychiatric care, if you can call it

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:15.580
that in the United States
today are the Los Angeles

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:20.040
County Jail, Cook County Jail in
Chicago, and Rikers Island in New

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:26.700
York, that's shockingly, because by their very
nature, of course, prisons aren't equipped to

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:29.580
deal with serious mental illness

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:32.840
- Some dark ways to return
to the asylum era.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:39.730
- Yeah, well, you know, the asylums had acquired a
very bad reputation, and I've written about some

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:42.730
of the reasons why that happened, but when

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:49.580
asylums were founded, it was a period of enormous
optimism that we were going to be able to cure these

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:56.000
people that we would rescue them from attics
and from jail cells, and through providing a

00:28:56.060 --> 00:28:58.780
therapeutic environment, coaxing them to

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:06.420
work, creating a system where they learn to
control themselves, not necessarily to drive

00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:12.000
their demons out, but to keep them under some
sort of wraps. The expectation was when the

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:14.230
asylums came along is that they'd not

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:18.120
only rescue people from horrendous
conditions in the prisons and

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:22.070
the jails, but they'd
actively cure them and

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:28.500
the earliest alienists, as they
called themselves then, thought that

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:35.800
they could cure 60, 70, 80 percent, maybe even
more of patients, as long as they came in early.

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:39.380
And really, asylums were
built out of that sense.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:45.380
of optimism, that sense that we, you know,
that so much was environmental and so much was

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:52.480
Not exacerbating the condition by treating
people like animals or treating them, you know.

00:29:52.520 --> 00:30:00.320
Beating them and in other ways horrifically
maltreating them, that would create, um, a sense of

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:06.020
Cure and really that underlay the
construction of the asylums and

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:16.180
Now having abandoned the asylums, which we did starting a
little bit in the 1950s but really the late 1960s onwards, uh.

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:20.960
That was because there was going to be
something miraculous called community care, but

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:25.300
Community care is a shell game without
a pee. It's like one o' those.

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:32.984
confidence tricks. There were no substitute
community facilities for dealing with

00:30:33.024 --> 00:30:34.924
the really serious mentally ill.

00:30:35.424 --> 00:30:38.774
- So now we're in this
truly, in, in this crisis-

00:30:38.774 --> 00:30:42.434
... in this essentially
dark ages. And in part

00:30:42.964 --> 00:30:47.314
I think our conversation, our journey
through the history of psychiatry-

00:30:47.314 --> 00:30:51.004
... is an exploration of some

00:30:51.044 --> 00:30:55.004
gigantic mistakes but also an
exploration of where lay some

00:30:55.084 --> 00:30:58.984
hope for the future. So we'll
talk quite a bit about this. I

00:30:59.004 --> 00:31:02.464
was wondering if you can also just lay out

00:31:02.584 --> 00:31:06.214
what are the big categories of mental
illness that we're referring to. You've

00:31:06.264 --> 00:31:11.024
already hinted at them, but
like levels of seriousness-

00:31:11.044 --> 00:31:13.134
... and the categories of illness-

00:31:13.184 --> 00:31:15.764
... like with psychosis
and depression and so on.

00:31:16.224 --> 00:31:23.824
- So going back to the early 20th century
was when the German psychiatrist Emil

00:31:23.984 --> 00:31:28.264
Kraepelin working with thousands
of records in German asylums

00:31:29.064 --> 00:31:32.664
inductively developed a
distinction between two very

00:31:32.684 --> 00:31:35.224
broad categories of mental illness.

00:31:36.204 --> 00:31:39.084
One he called dementia
praecox or early dementia,

00:31:40.024 --> 00:31:43.984
and the other he called manic depressive
illness which was a more remitting

00:31:44.084 --> 00:31:47.454
illness that sometimes went away entirely,

00:31:47.944 --> 00:31:54.604
other times went back and forth whereas
dementia praecox was a one way ticket down.

00:31:55.984 --> 00:32:01.444
Now that label was transformed
by a Swiss psychiatrist Bleuler

00:32:02.584 --> 00:32:06.384
into the term we use today
schizophrenia although

00:32:06.824 --> 00:32:10.764
Bleuler talked about the
schizophrenias because he thought

00:32:10.784 --> 00:32:15.974
under that broad label there were a
diverse group of things running around.

00:32:16.904 --> 00:32:20.344
And I think that was an important
insight that tends to get lost

00:32:20.424 --> 00:32:24.324
sometimes. This was
jamming together people

00:32:24.364 --> 00:32:28.244
with very serious psychosis,
that is people who'd lost touch

00:32:28.304 --> 00:32:31.184
with we liked-- what we like
to think of as reality,

00:32:32.184 --> 00:32:36.904
whose emotional and cognitive
lives were in total turmoil,

00:32:37.764 --> 00:32:41.664
Who were -- who lost
the ability to connect

00:32:41.764 --> 00:32:44.924
with other human beings.
So their social skills

00:32:45.144 --> 00:32:48.844
atrophied, their-- Well, this
is something contemporary

00:32:48.884 --> 00:32:53.604
psychiatrists would refer to as the positive
and negative symptoms of schizophrenia.

00:32:53.944 --> 00:32:57.544
- But underlying the definition
of schizophrenia here is a

00:32:57.664 --> 00:33:01.333
detachment from reality. So you're hearing
voices- ... you're seeing visions.

00:33:01.644 --> 00:33:05.544
- You're thinking people are plotting
against you. You think the

00:33:05.584 --> 00:33:08.144
television is talking directly to you.

00:33:08.184 --> 00:33:11.864
- And because of that it has these
consequences of how you're

00:33:11.904 --> 00:33:15.893
connected to the rest of the world and what your
emotional life is like, all that kind of stuff.

00:33:15.924 --> 00:33:19.324
- Yes. Your emotional life flattens
out. Your language capacity

00:33:19.724 --> 00:33:23.384
deteriorates. Your ability
to relate to other people

00:33:24.004 --> 00:33:27.844
either vanishes or becomes
caught up in the web of

00:33:27.854 --> 00:33:31.324
delusions where you think
people around you are plotting

00:33:31.444 --> 00:33:33.744
against you or doing
terrible things to you.

00:33:33.784 --> 00:33:37.264
- And is that a different world than
the world of bipolar and the world of

00:33:37.324 --> 00:33:37.824
depression?

00:33:38.224 --> 00:33:42.184
- Yes. The Greeks recognized and
the Romans, ancient Greece and

00:33:42.244 --> 00:33:46.544
ancient Rome recognized different
forms of insanity as they called it.

00:33:47.464 --> 00:33:48.964
So melancholia

00:33:49.904 --> 00:33:53.724
would have been the term the Greeks
and the Romans would have used and it

00:33:53.764 --> 00:34:01.464
survived and was very much around.
And I think that is a form of

00:34:01.524 --> 00:34:07.844
depression. What's happened now is major
depression has become a catchall category.

00:34:07.964 --> 00:34:14.704
So it embraces both what we might think
of as milder forms of emotional distress

00:34:15.784 --> 00:34:19.744
along with what melancholia
referred to which was really

00:34:20.804 --> 00:34:24.524
a kind of depression that had
psychotic features. This loss of

00:34:24.584 --> 00:34:28.184
contact as it were with everyday reality.

00:34:28.464 --> 00:34:32.044
- Something you would talk about maybe like
a clinical depression. And by the way, we

00:34:32.084 --> 00:34:37.004
should mention that this field in the
21st century is like a minefield.

00:34:37.064 --> 00:34:43.934
- Yes, very much so. So here's a very strange
bit of historical record. The other

00:34:43.984 --> 00:34:47.784
distinction and the distinction
we're grappling with so we

00:34:47.844 --> 00:34:51.734
have people who are-- whose
depression is of such a scale they're

00:34:51.744 --> 00:34:55.444
threatening to do away with
themselves. They've retreated

00:34:55.504 --> 00:34:59.144
into complete almost immobility. They're

00:34:59.284 --> 00:35:02.844
overwhelmed by senses
of sadness and loss and

00:35:02.944 --> 00:35:06.904
they're-- If they're religious then
they're damned to hell and all those

00:35:06.984 --> 00:35:10.564
kinds of things. So we have this psychosis

00:35:11.184 --> 00:35:14.704
that we've been talking about.
We have something also that the

00:35:14.744 --> 00:35:18.704
ancients recognize: dementia;
the loss really the loss

00:35:18.764 --> 00:35:24.094
of our mind as it were.
But then we also have

00:35:24.924 --> 00:35:31.264
other kinds of disturbance of our
mental faculties that generally

00:35:31.344 --> 00:35:35.084
we think of as more minor but I
don't want to make light of them

00:35:35.144 --> 00:35:38.924
because often people suffer
from these things genuinely

00:35:38.934 --> 00:35:42.844
suffer pretty badly. But
those things-- we in

00:35:42.884 --> 00:35:46.504
the 20th and 21st centuries
tend to talk about as neuroses

00:35:47.104 --> 00:35:53.644
neurotic diseases, psychotic diseases.
In the 19th century neurosis was a

00:35:53.704 --> 00:35:57.604
term that meant things rooted
in the brain as you can

00:35:57.704 --> 00:36:01.664
see from the root of the word
and psychosis was stuff that

00:36:02.224 --> 00:36:03.744
came from the mind, the psyche.

00:36:04.544 --> 00:36:08.304
And yet sometime in the late 19th
century those things crossed over

00:36:09.084 --> 00:36:16.364
and so when we talk in the present we do
tend to distinguish between the core,

00:36:17.404 --> 00:36:20.714
really the most severe
forms of mental disorder,

00:36:20.714 --> 00:36:24.584
...which would include things
like Alzheimer's disease

00:36:24.604 --> 00:36:28.144
and other forms of dementia, would include

00:36:28.484 --> 00:36:32.384
very serious depression,
would include bipolar

00:36:32.444 --> 00:36:36.504
disorder where people oscillate
either have extreme mania so they're

00:36:37.304 --> 00:36:41.124
they're not getting any sleep. They're
talking at an extraordinary rate

00:36:41.264 --> 00:36:44.764
Their behavior is very hard to
tolerate, and they're exhausting

00:36:44.784 --> 00:36:48.724
themselves, and they may even die from
exhaustion if it's not controlled.

00:36:48.784 --> 00:36:52.364
And very often that
alternates with periods of

00:36:52.384 --> 00:36:59.354
depression. So that was a category
that was captured initially as,

00:36:59.864 --> 00:37:03.524
Manic depressive illness
and later on evolved into

00:37:03.564 --> 00:37:07.544
bipolar disorder and then
separating out depression

00:37:07.584 --> 00:37:11.364
and major depression separately.
But then there are a whole bunch of

00:37:11.404 --> 00:37:15.064
other things like, for example, phobias.

00:37:15.564 --> 00:37:19.224
People can't go outside because
they find it too frightening.

00:37:19.994 --> 00:37:23.784
Milder phobias: "I can't get in an
airplane because it's gonna crash,"

00:37:24.514 --> 00:37:28.284
"I can't go to school because it's
overwhelming for me." So school phobia

00:37:28.424 --> 00:37:35.044
emerges as a diagnosis. A disorder that
at first attracted Freud, hysteria.

00:37:35.524 --> 00:37:38.264
- And now we should say you
wrote a book on hysteria.

00:37:38.304 --> 00:37:38.994
- Oh, I did indeed, yes.

00:37:38.994 --> 00:37:42.634
- You mentioned ... I mean, hysteria,
every classification we're

00:37:42.663 --> 00:37:46.404
talking about has been
used and abused by every

00:37:46.484 --> 00:37:48.484
layer of society including institutions-

00:37:49.084 --> 00:37:49.094
- Yes

00:37:49.094 --> 00:37:55.704
- ...including just culturally the word
hysteria applied to different races

00:37:55.804 --> 00:37:58.324
disproportionately, to different, um-

00:37:58.684 --> 00:37:59.134
- Genders particularly, yes, yes

00:37:59.134 --> 00:38:00.344
- ...genders disproportionate-

00:38:00.424 --> 00:38:04.244
So we're in this crisis of trying to
figure out what to do with this super

00:38:04.324 --> 00:38:05.404
complicated human mind-

00:38:05.784 --> 00:38:05.854
- Yes

00:38:05.854 --> 00:38:09.664
- ...and everybody is dogmatically creating

00:38:09.784 --> 00:38:15.424
narratives that hold, take hold, and
in so doing can lead to some abuses-

00:38:15.504 --> 00:38:16.094
- Yes, they do. Yes, they do

00:38:16.124 --> 00:38:17.204
- ...as you document.

00:38:17.864 --> 00:38:21.564
- If you look back at the asylum era
born in this period of intense

00:38:21.744 --> 00:38:25.584
optimism, and then the claims
to be able to cure these vast

00:38:25.664 --> 00:38:27.604
numbers of patients were overblown.

00:38:28.664 --> 00:38:32.544
Uh, I do believe the early asylums
actually did good work and that some

00:38:32.604 --> 00:38:38.044
patients did very well and recovered as a
result of their stays. But what happened,

00:38:39.064 --> 00:38:42.564
they weren't discharging 80% of their
patients. They were discharging

00:38:42.764 --> 00:38:48.684
35 or 40% of their patients, and what
that meant is every year left behind

00:38:49.544 --> 00:38:53.454
were a batch of chronic patients, and then
the next year you repeated it. And over

00:38:53.584 --> 00:38:57.384
time, what that means is the ratio of new

00:38:57.424 --> 00:39:01.094
patients to chronic patients
gets worse and worse.

00:39:02.104 --> 00:39:05.784
And more and more the image of
the asylum is defined by the

00:39:05.804 --> 00:39:09.413
chronic patient who hasn't
recovered and maybe spends years or

00:39:09.484 --> 00:39:18.294
decades there and only leaves in a pine
box. So the image of the asylum declined

00:39:18.344 --> 00:39:21.804
drastically. As they became
more and more overcrowded,

00:39:21.904 --> 00:39:25.764
conditions in them deteriorated.
Patients were often

00:39:25.824 --> 00:39:29.744
abused. Psychiatrists didn't know
what to do with them, and they

00:39:29.804 --> 00:39:35.544
faced a problem in the late 19th century.
You'd promised 70% or 80% cures,

00:39:36.384 --> 00:39:40.124
but we're not seeing that. In fact, when
we calculate cures and the numbers of

00:39:40.184 --> 00:39:44.094
people in the asylum, it's
more like 10% or 12%. That's

00:39:44.124 --> 00:39:48.064
the way you could play with statistics
'cause that's all the old patients

00:39:48.104 --> 00:39:55.884
mixed with the new. But still, it looks
very bad. How do you explain this doctor?

00:39:56.224 --> 00:40:00.104
You've promised us one thing, and you
delivered something quite different. The

00:40:00.144 --> 00:40:04.084
answer came in a way of
blaming the victim, in a way

00:40:04.144 --> 00:40:06.984
of saying, "Well, you know,
what we didn't understand

00:40:07.784 --> 00:40:11.484
was that mental illness is
a fundamentally biological

00:40:11.584 --> 00:40:15.284
condition. These people are evolutionary

00:40:15.364 --> 00:40:18.904
throwbacks." Evolution was
generally thought of as a

00:40:18.964 --> 00:40:22.924
progressive onwards and upwards,
but these people had fallen back

00:40:22.984 --> 00:40:26.544
into a lesser form of

00:40:26.624 --> 00:40:32.024
existence. They'd lost their essential
humanity because their brains were defective.

00:40:32.824 --> 00:40:38.144
So what emerged then was the idea
of degeneration, the idea that

00:40:39.004 --> 00:40:42.484
these patients were degenerates.
They were people with an inferior

00:40:42.564 --> 00:40:46.404
biology. You couldn't release them because
they breed like rabbits. They didn't

00:40:46.444 --> 00:40:50.684
have any self-control because
of their diminished humanity.

00:40:50.984 --> 00:40:52.504
- This is the narrative.

00:40:52.904 --> 00:40:56.804
- This is the narrative. And
so what it did was provide a

00:40:56.864 --> 00:41:00.744
justification for locking up people
in asylums that wasn't therapeutic

00:41:00.804 --> 00:41:02.724
at all. It was just keeping
them out of the way.

00:41:02.964 --> 00:41:05.604
- And then it led to the
justification of sterilization-

00:41:05.884 --> 00:41:06.224
- Exactly.

00:41:06.234 --> 00:41:07.144
- ...based on the same argument.

00:41:07.214 --> 00:41:10.784
- So maybe we can release them if
we make sure they can't breed.

00:41:10.794 --> 00:41:12.434
- This is the beginning of the darkness.

00:41:12.544 --> 00:41:16.424
- It is the beginning of the
darkness. My own state was

00:41:16.484 --> 00:41:20.224
one of the pioneers in
this process, and it

00:41:20.284 --> 00:41:24.024
continued to sterilize mental
patients up until about 1960.

00:41:24.624 --> 00:41:32.334
- By the 1960s, over 60,000 sterilizations
have been performed in the US with

00:41:32.384 --> 00:41:36.524
California performing a
disproportionately high number.

00:41:36.564 --> 00:41:39.784
- That's correct. But even more serious

00:41:39.844 --> 00:41:43.794
consequences could flow
from these set of issues.

00:41:44.424 --> 00:41:51.664
When you start talking, one British
psychiatrist said that if his patients that

00:41:51.804 --> 00:41:55.524
were coming into the
asylum had been puppies,

00:41:57.044 --> 00:42:00.964
we'd have tied them up in a sack because
they were some horrible mongrel, not

00:42:01.024 --> 00:42:04.744
a purebred dog, tied them up in a sack with
some lead weights, and thrown them in a

00:42:04.884 --> 00:42:05.744
pond and drowned them.

00:42:05.964 --> 00:42:05.974
- Man.

00:42:06.924 --> 00:42:10.444
- That kind of language is
very, very dangerous.

00:42:11.164 --> 00:42:14.534
And what happened,
California's law surrounding

00:42:14.604 --> 00:42:18.444
sterilization was advocated
for in the West. By that I

00:42:18.504 --> 00:42:22.554
mean North America and
Britain and much of Europe.

00:42:23.424 --> 00:42:27.384
There were enough checks and balances
in a democratic system that even

00:42:27.424 --> 00:42:30.764
though there were enthusiasts,
the eugenicists who said,

00:42:31.214 --> 00:42:34.364
"Best get rid of these
people, put them to death,"

00:42:35.104 --> 00:42:40.452
that never really, that never
really acquired mass support.

00:42:41.292 --> 00:42:44.752
But what happened in Germany
once Nazi-- the Nazis came to

00:42:44.852 --> 00:42:48.631
power is they seized on these
notions and that the idea

00:42:48.732 --> 00:42:52.462
that the mentally ill were, as
they put it, useless eaters,

00:42:52.552 --> 00:42:56.512
people consuming resources but never
gonna get better, just a burden on

00:42:56.552 --> 00:43:00.422
the state. Their lives weren't worth
living because after all, they had this

00:43:00.472 --> 00:43:03.692
serious mental illness. So first you

00:43:03.772 --> 00:43:07.552
sterilize, and then you go, "But
we're still supporting all these

00:43:07.572 --> 00:43:11.332
people." And so Hitler starts
something called the T4 program

00:43:11.432 --> 00:43:17.152
after the street name of the house where
this was concocted, Tiergartenstraße 4.

00:43:18.332 --> 00:43:24.672
And the mentally ill were the first
people to suffer from the Final Solution.

00:43:25.472 --> 00:43:29.352
It was in the mass killing of the mentally
ill, which may have been as many as a

00:43:29.412 --> 00:43:35.472
quarter million people, that the technology
of the gas chamber was developed,

00:43:35.812 --> 00:43:39.612
and the technology of disguising the

00:43:39.652 --> 00:43:46.502
gas chamber as showers was developed.
And so patients were taken away

00:43:47.532 --> 00:43:51.332
to a number of psychiatric
centers and systematically

00:43:51.412 --> 00:43:55.192
put to death, and they had the
crematoriums and the black

00:43:55.272 --> 00:43:59.052
smoke, and local people talked about
the buses that were bringing them in as

00:43:59.112 --> 00:44:02.452
killing crates. So they were
aware of what was going on.

00:44:02.572 --> 00:44:05.712
- Were they influenced by the narratives
that were born in the United States?

00:44:05.792 --> 00:44:06.542
- Yes, absolutely. They-

00:44:06.572 --> 00:44:07.652
- About this kind of-

00:44:07.792 --> 00:44:11.022
- About that sort of thing, and
lacking the check, the checks and

00:44:11.072 --> 00:44:15.652
balances that at least until
recently this country had, um,

00:44:16.512 --> 00:44:18.852
it was relatively easy for Hitler

00:44:19.692 --> 00:44:23.552
to do that. And particularly
with the war looming, the idea

00:44:23.592 --> 00:44:27.432
that we're gonna support all
these useless people, let's kill

00:44:27.472 --> 00:44:33.772
them. And German psychiatry for the most
part collaborated with that process.

00:44:34.212 --> 00:44:38.932
- I wonder how many people throughout that
whole journey in the psychiatry profession

00:44:39.872 --> 00:44:43.852
sort of were brave enough to
speak up like, "Hey, maybe the

00:44:43.952 --> 00:44:46.502
sack of puppies kinda language-"

00:44:46.512 --> 00:44:46.762
- Oh, yes. Oh, yes.

00:44:46.762 --> 00:44:47.552
- ... is a problem?"

00:44:48.311 --> 00:44:52.732
- That's the really nasty direction
that that language could lead to.

00:44:53.692 --> 00:44:57.112
And it was symptomatic of the kind of

00:44:57.612 --> 00:45:01.242
stigma that tends to attach
itself to mental illness and this

00:45:01.312 --> 00:45:05.142
sense of hopelessness. So but
if you're a healing profession,

00:45:05.632 --> 00:45:09.092
if, if you enter psychiatry thinking,

00:45:09.612 --> 00:45:13.532
"I'm gonna do this to help people, to
cure people, to make their lives better,"

00:45:15.292 --> 00:45:20.492
to just become a glorified boarding
housekeeper keeping them under lock and key

00:45:21.092 --> 00:45:24.752
or in the alternative to collaborate
in this, in the sorts of

00:45:25.132 --> 00:45:28.262
awfulness that the Nazis
perpetrated, that's

00:45:28.332 --> 00:45:32.161
something from which decent human beings

00:45:32.212 --> 00:45:37.592
tend to recoil and decent
psychiatrists tended to recoil. And so

00:45:38.652 --> 00:45:41.892
still thinking as they did
by the end of the 19th

00:45:41.932 --> 00:45:47.852
century that mental illness was
predominantly a biological problem,

00:45:49.632 --> 00:45:53.532
some of them began to say, "Well,
maybe biology as well as being the

00:45:53.572 --> 00:45:57.552
problem could provide the
solution. Maybe we should look for

00:45:57.732 --> 00:46:03.192
ways to intervene in the biological systems
of these people and make them better."

00:46:04.032 --> 00:46:08.722
The same logic that applies
I would say in the present-

00:46:08.732 --> 00:46:10.412
... for, for many working in the field.

00:46:10.652 --> 00:46:11.682
- But in a cruder form.

00:46:11.732 --> 00:46:14.032
- But in a very different form, exactly.

00:46:14.112 --> 00:46:18.012
- Yeah. If, if we can just speak
about the Nazis a bit more, you

00:46:18.052 --> 00:46:21.792
highlight that America financially
supported the German psychiatric

00:46:21.872 --> 00:46:25.792
researchers with deep Nazi
ties like Ernst Rudin who was

00:46:25.832 --> 00:46:29.612
the key architect of Hitler's mass
sterilization and extermination

00:46:29.712 --> 00:46:33.672
laws. So it seems like the Nazis borrowed

00:46:33.712 --> 00:46:37.072
the American narratives of the
psychiatrist that these are lesser

00:46:37.092 --> 00:46:42.332
biological beings and then this financial
support and the ties continued.

00:46:42.452 --> 00:46:46.242
- Yeah. So one of the organizations that

00:46:46.312 --> 00:46:52.822
recognized that mental illness was an acute
social problem, very costly to the state

00:46:54.752 --> 00:46:59.532
inflicting all kinds of suffering on
people was The Rockefeller Foundation.

00:47:00.512 --> 00:47:04.032
What we don't realize today is that the

00:47:04.072 --> 00:47:07.952
involvement of the federal
government in medical research

00:47:08.092 --> 00:47:11.892
and indeed scientific
research is a World War II

00:47:11.902 --> 00:47:15.792
and post-war development partly
the Cold War and Sputnik and

00:47:15.872 --> 00:47:21.002
all of that. But that's when big
science and big medicine got funded-

00:47:21.132 --> 00:47:24.252
... in extravagant ways. Before the war,

00:47:25.172 --> 00:47:29.052
science was an orphan. It didn't get
money from the government much and

00:47:29.092 --> 00:47:38.352
medicine even more so. So to the extent
medical training was reformed, that was the

00:47:38.752 --> 00:47:42.632
product of investment by The Rockefeller
Foundation enormously wealthy

00:47:42.832 --> 00:47:47.422
by the standards of the
time. And come about 1930,

00:47:48.792 --> 00:47:52.632
The Rockefeller Foundation
decided it needed

00:47:52.692 --> 00:47:59.252
to concentrate its resources and pick
priorities for the money it was investing.

00:48:00.412 --> 00:48:03.792
And it may seem a rather
strange thing within the

00:48:04.092 --> 00:48:07.592
whole range of medical
areas that it could choose,

00:48:08.232 --> 00:48:12.112
it chose psychiatry as the one that
it was going to invest in. And

00:48:12.192 --> 00:48:15.212
I think it did so in part for

00:48:16.592 --> 00:48:20.672
precisely because scientific research
in psychiatry was so backward,

00:48:22.312 --> 00:48:26.692
partly because it was such
a pressing public problem.

00:48:26.732 --> 00:48:30.592
And partly, and this was less
public but nonetheless I

00:48:30.612 --> 00:48:34.472
think played an important role,
several of the trustees of The

00:48:34.532 --> 00:48:38.232
Rockefeller Foundation had
direct experience of mental

00:48:38.272 --> 00:48:41.212
illness in their families.
Wives who'd been

00:48:41.252 --> 00:48:44.992
institutionalized as
schizophrenic. In one case, a

00:48:45.032 --> 00:48:47.952
wife who murdered the
children and killed herself

00:48:48.712 --> 00:48:52.112
leaving her husband as
a major actor in The

00:48:52.312 --> 00:48:55.892
Rockefeller Foundation bereft
and of course inclined

00:48:55.952 --> 00:49:01.920
then to support research in this area. And
Rockefeller spread its money very widely,

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:06.520
Precisely 'cause it didn't
know where to spend most of

00:49:06.560 --> 00:49:10.080
its money. So it did some support of

00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:14.060
Psychotherapeutics. It
supported a number of the then

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:17.600
extant therapeutic experiments going on,

00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:22.270
and it supported work in genetics.
And one of the geneticists it

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:26.190
supported, Ernst Rüdin in
Germany, who was the leading

00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:29.510
German researcher in genetics
and mental disorder.

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:34.120
And Rüdin, because he's absorbed
the lessons from California

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:38.240
about sterilization, became a very
enthusiastic proponent of that,

00:49:38.980 --> 00:49:42.230
and then a supporter of
murdering mental patients.

00:49:42.700 --> 00:49:46.840
- And we should say, I mean, we'll probably
talk about the complicated nature of science-

00:49:46.920 --> 00:49:50.420
...that it sometimes can be
captured by certain ideologies and-

00:49:50.430 --> 00:49:54.150
...in so doing do a lot of
damage to humanity. But

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:58.180
ultimately, the beacon of hope for
the future of humanity lays in the

00:49:58.220 --> 00:50:02.150
scientific method, as flawed as it is.
So everything we're talking about,

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:07.500
we get to see how you F up-

00:50:09.740 --> 00:50:14.430
In a major dark disturbing ways
throughout the 20th century-

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:17.820
...on the-- in the psychiatric
profession. But that

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:21.540
should be instructive lessons
of how we proceed forward to do

00:50:21.580 --> 00:50:23.100
better and better and better.

00:50:24.120 --> 00:50:31.360
- We can discuss a series of therapeutic
experiments on people who were shut up in a

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:35.260
double sense. They were locked away,
and their voices were not heeded

00:50:35.300 --> 00:50:39.140
because it was the product of their
madness. And so what we see in

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:42.720
the first five decades really of the 20th

00:50:42.780 --> 00:50:46.730
century is people with a
variety of motivations

00:50:46.800 --> 00:50:50.100
including the desire to improve
the lot of the mentally ill

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:54.800
engaging in uncontrolled experiments
that had terrible results.

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:59.540
And the science behind it
was shaky but nonetheless

00:50:59.800 --> 00:51:03.260
it existed. It wasn't just
plucked out of the sky.

00:51:04.350 --> 00:51:08.279
And yes, it eventually those things
break down. I mean, the clearest case

00:51:08.340 --> 00:51:12.180
of that because it's the most
extreme of these at least in the

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:15.780
public imagination is lobotomy. The

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:19.800
idea that you are going to solve
psychotic breakdowns in people

00:51:20.600 --> 00:51:23.940
by excising part of their brain

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:28.380
initially by drilling holes in
the skull and injecting alcohol

00:51:28.480 --> 00:51:32.100
or using what looks like a butter
knife to break connections

00:51:32.180 --> 00:51:36.080
between the brain. And then
later when that process

00:51:36.140 --> 00:51:40.009
seems to be too slow, the
Henry Ford of lobotomy--

00:51:40.320 --> 00:51:44.180
his daughter said Walter Freeman aspired
to be the Henry Ford of lobotomy, the

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:47.650
one who could mechanize the
production of it and get it done

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:51.420
fast. He invented the ice
pick lobotomy where you

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:54.849
used an ice pick in the orbit of the eye

00:51:55.660 --> 00:51:59.540
having rendered somebody unconscious
after two or three electric shocks

00:52:00.460 --> 00:52:04.320
and you banged it through the
bone and wiggled it about and

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:08.460
severed -- I mean, it's
just hard to even describe.

00:52:08.720 --> 00:52:12.340
- So this is from the 1930s to the 1970s?

00:52:12.680 --> 00:52:16.600
- Yes. Freeman starts his work in 1936

00:52:16.780 --> 00:52:20.760
borrowing from the work of the
preceding year of Portuguese

00:52:20.840 --> 00:52:24.780
neurologist named Egas Moniz.
And Moniz wins the Nobel

00:52:24.860 --> 00:52:28.240
Prize in medicine in 1949 for lobotomy.

00:52:28.780 --> 00:52:32.720
So it's important to see
that. That was 14 years

00:52:32.800 --> 00:52:36.780
of experience and yet that
won a Nobel Prize. I'm

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:38.680
sure that's one they'd like to retract.

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.520
- So he popularized Walter
Freeman, the ice pick.

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:46.220
- And his particular specialty after
the war became this ice pick

00:52:46.280 --> 00:52:50.220
lobotomy because there were
over half million patients in

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:54.240
America's mental hospitals. Freeman
was convinced this operation was

00:52:54.320 --> 00:53:00.490
a cure all. And so he traveled
around in a camper truck

00:53:01.300 --> 00:53:04.420
which he called the Lobotomobile
and he would descend in the

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:10.740
summer on state hospitals and he would teach
them how to do this ice pick lobotomy.

00:53:11.020 --> 00:53:14.440
- This is by the way an image of the tool.

00:53:15.300 --> 00:53:19.199
- Yes, those are the tools he used. Originally,
he used an actual ice pick and then

00:53:19.259 --> 00:53:20.240
he developed this-

00:53:20.480 --> 00:53:23.240
- This is what he developed. This is
the state-of-the-art technology.

00:53:23.280 --> 00:53:25.720
- This is the state of the art
with a hammer or a mallet.

00:53:25.860 --> 00:53:26.560
- Oh no!

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:32.400
- Yes, I'm sorry. It's very
distressing. I, I don't know-

00:53:32.680 --> 00:53:34.150
- You having to write
about this by the way--

00:53:34.180 --> 00:53:35.660
- Well, it's really-

00:53:36.120 --> 00:53:36.130
- Is a lot

00:53:36.140 --> 00:53:39.520
- ...very, very, very, very
difficult. I came across, for

00:53:39.560 --> 00:53:43.280
example, picture of a woman, naked woman

00:53:43.320 --> 00:53:46.680
being dragged away by attendants
to be lobotomized and she's

00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:53.280
resisting with all her might and to
no avail. You have a picture there of

00:53:53.360 --> 00:53:57.680
Walter Freeman lobotomizing a
patient in Washington State.

00:53:58.520 --> 00:54:06.000
Freeman was ambidextrous and when he taught
neurology, he would draw simultaneously

00:54:06.020 --> 00:54:09.820
with his left and right hand
and he could do it perfectly.

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:13.810
When he was performing lobotomy, when
his right hand got tired, he switched to

00:54:13.820 --> 00:54:19.140
his left hand and he sometimes would do
20 or 30 lobotomies in an afternoon.

00:54:20.080 --> 00:54:23.700
And he boasted, he said, "You
know I could teach any damn

00:54:23.800 --> 00:54:27.340
fool to perform a lobotomy in twenty
minutes even a psychiatrist."

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:33.840
Because Freeman was a neurologist and he
had a lot of contempt for psychiatrists. So

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:40.520
So this was a very ugly episode.
How did it die away? It really took

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:47.780
generational change. Some of these
lobotomists continued to operate into the

00:54:47.820 --> 00:54:49.790
sixties even to the early seventies,

00:54:50.700 --> 00:54:53.620
but the younger generation
who became acquainted

00:54:53.760 --> 00:54:57.500
with the really worst failures of that

00:54:57.540 --> 00:55:02.180
regime; the people on the back wards
who were incontinent, who were

00:55:03.340 --> 00:55:07.209
basically zombies had
lost all mental power.

00:55:07.932 --> 00:55:11.092
They rebelled against
this and by then they had

00:55:11.932 --> 00:55:15.642
a different treatment in the
form of antipsychotic drugs-

00:55:16.072 --> 00:55:19.322
... which looked much more like
what regular medicine was doing,

00:55:20.312 --> 00:55:22.842
And didn't have these
horrible overtones. And of

00:55:22.872 --> 00:55:30.392
course in the popular mind I think
the, probably the most famous,

00:55:31.672 --> 00:55:37.952
instance of telling the public about
some of these interventions was the

00:55:37.992 --> 00:55:41.232
film of Ken Kesey's novel One
Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest

00:55:41.892 --> 00:55:45.072
where you see Jack Nicholson giving
I think the performance of a

00:55:45.112 --> 00:55:51.812
lifetime who is given ECT,
electroconvulsive therapy,

00:55:52.672 --> 00:55:56.552
at--in a very dramatic rendition
of what that was not, not really

00:55:57.772 --> 00:56:01.752
what was going on by, by the 1970s

00:56:01.832 --> 00:56:05.732
with ECT but nonetheless certainly
what had been going on back in the

00:56:05.772 --> 00:56:10.812
past. And then finally when
ECT doesn't smash him to bits

00:56:11.772 --> 00:56:15.652
they lobotomize him and, and the
film ends obviously with him being

00:56:15.692 --> 00:56:21.232
smothered to death 'cause Chief can't bear
to see him in, in the state that he's in.

00:56:22.212 --> 00:56:25.212
So that fixed in the public mind

00:56:26.232 --> 00:56:30.032
some of the images of these things.
It is one of the things that gave

00:56:31.212 --> 00:56:35.152
Electroconvulsive therapy such a bad name.

00:56:35.472 --> 00:56:38.912
- So we will actually not to
fast forward too quickly,-

00:56:38.952 --> 00:56:41.662
-... let's talk about uh, the full journey

00:56:42.472 --> 00:56:46.122
of everything we've been talking about. So we
mentioned the asylum era that began in the

00:56:46.692 --> 00:56:51.312
mid nineteenth century going into twentieth
and we talked about the narratives-

00:56:51.532 --> 00:56:53.092
- And we talked about sterilization

00:56:53.212 --> 00:56:58.612
- Sterilization. And let's look at the
insulin shock therapy of uh, nineteen

00:56:58.732 --> 00:57:02.612
thirty three to the nineteen sixties
where you were putting patients

00:57:02.652 --> 00:57:06.692
in deep hypoglycemic comas
using large doses of insulin.

00:57:07.072 --> 00:57:10.892
- Yeah. So as I mentioned
for psychiatrists who

00:57:10.932 --> 00:57:14.692
went into the field and were
ambitious but who also wanted to

00:57:14.772 --> 00:57:18.522
think of themselves as therapeutic agents-

00:57:18.522 --> 00:57:22.432
....uh, to just sit there
passively and contain the patients

00:57:22.532 --> 00:57:28.232
was very unattractive. And so they
looked around for ways in which perhaps

00:57:29.212 --> 00:57:31.672
biological interventions could be used

00:57:32.472 --> 00:57:37.052
to ameliorate this condition that they
still saw in largely biological terms.

00:57:38.292 --> 00:57:46.512
Now in some ways one of the crucial
early ways in which this thinking went

00:57:47.372 --> 00:57:51.352
and which affected a large
number of patients was this:

00:57:52.032 --> 00:57:55.942
one of the few diagnostic triumphs of
psychiatry in the early nineteenth

00:57:55.992 --> 00:57:58.372
century as the profession began to emerge

00:57:59.372 --> 00:58:03.292
Was that it began to distinguish a
group of patients who were deemed

00:58:03.352 --> 00:58:07.012
to be suffering from something called
general paralysis of the insane.

00:58:08.352 --> 00:58:13.881
That encapsulates two things about
what was going on. First of all,

00:58:14.752 --> 00:58:18.872
paralysis; the gradual
loss of motor control,

00:58:19.692 --> 00:58:24.812
ability to walk, ability to
swallow, ability to, to talk.

00:58:26.712 --> 00:58:30.202
So those are primarily what we'd
think of these days as neurological

00:58:30.592 --> 00:58:34.312
issues. But those were
accompanied by bizarre

00:58:35.172 --> 00:58:38.852
psychiatric symptomatology. These
were people who thought they were

00:58:38.892 --> 00:58:44.672
Napoleon or Jesus Christ or the
richest and sexiest man in the world-

00:58:44.692 --> 00:58:48.642
...or Mary the mother of
God. They were primarily men

00:58:48.712 --> 00:58:50.402
but there were also female victims.

00:58:51.752 --> 00:58:55.332
At the turn of the twentieth
century as many as twenty

00:58:55.452 --> 00:58:58.812
five percent of the people
being admitted to asylums

00:58:59.332 --> 00:59:03.212
were suffering from General Paralysis
of the Insane or GPI for short.

00:59:04.832 --> 00:59:08.352
There have been a lot of suspicions
about this being connected somehow to

00:59:08.492 --> 00:59:13.312
sex and to moral dissolution and so forth

00:59:14.132 --> 00:59:19.652
but what evolved in the early
twentieth century was the

00:59:19.692 --> 00:59:25.722
discovery of the actual origins of this

00:59:25.752 --> 00:59:29.332
disorder. So I've said psychiatry
has been looking for the

00:59:29.412 --> 00:59:32.472
underlying pathology
that lies behind mental

00:59:32.492 --> 00:59:36.892
diseases. This was one that
at Rockefeller Institute

00:59:37.872 --> 00:59:45.882
they discovered that the organism that causes
syphilis was residing in the brains of

00:59:45.912 --> 00:59:49.552
the people who were suffering
from GPI. This was in

00:59:49.612 --> 00:59:53.312
fact tertiary stage of
syphilis. Syphilis is a

00:59:53.572 --> 00:59:57.412
still a real public health problem. It
was like AIDS in the late nineteenth

00:59:57.422 --> 00:59:58.452
century. It was everywhere.

00:59:59.852 --> 01:00:03.852
And when you first contract syphilis
in the primary phase you have pain

01:00:05.052 --> 01:00:08.332
but then it goes underground
and you think it's gone

01:00:09.392 --> 01:00:14.732
and it lurks the way chicken
pox virus lurks and can

01:00:14.792 --> 01:00:21.552
surface years later right? It lurks, it lurks
and it's insidiously damaging sometimes

01:00:21.612 --> 01:00:30.152
it attacks the heart and people drop dead
of a heart attack in their forties ohh

01:00:30.392 --> 01:00:34.212
he died of a-- You know it's natural heart
attack but in fact it was the syphilis

01:00:35.172 --> 01:00:38.392
Or it attacks the central nervous
system the spinal column in the brain

01:00:39.132 --> 01:00:42.512
and then you get the paralysis -
Mm-hmm ...and then you get also the

01:00:42.532 --> 01:00:49.232
psychiatric symptomatology. So when that
was discovered that sort of suggested

01:00:49.241 --> 01:00:51.972
that mental illness might
have an infectious origin.

01:00:52.832 --> 01:00:56.692
Tertiary syphilis GPI went on to win for

01:00:56.712 --> 01:01:00.632
somebody who developed a treatment
for it a Nobel Prize one of only

01:01:00.772 --> 01:01:04.432
two awarded for psychiatric
innovations. One was a lobotomy

01:01:04.932 --> 01:01:08.492
the other was giving people
malaria to cure their syphilis.

01:01:08.652 --> 01:01:15.192
- In case people didn't hear
that- Giving people malaria-

01:01:15.332 --> 01:01:17.876
.. In order to cure syphilis

01:01:18.196 --> 01:01:26.116
- So there was an Austrian doctor, Wagner
Jauregg, who had long thought that

01:01:26.196 --> 01:01:30.016
fever could be used to cure
mental illness. And he tried rat

01:01:30.056 --> 01:01:33.656
bite fever. He tried giving people
typhoid vaccine that creates a

01:01:33.696 --> 01:01:37.476
fever to no avail. And towards
the end of World War I,

01:01:38.036 --> 01:01:41.976
the Italians were fighting in
World War I on the side of the

01:01:41.996 --> 01:01:43.716
British and the Americans and the French.

01:01:44.616 --> 01:01:48.036
They captured an Italian
soldier who had malaria.

01:01:48.436 --> 01:01:51.256
Malaria was endemic in
those years in Italy.

01:01:51.916 --> 01:01:55.365
And they brought him to him, and he
extracted the malarial blood and

01:01:55.436 --> 01:01:59.296
injected it into a series
of patients with GPI, with

01:01:59.426 --> 01:02:02.496
general paralysis of the insane,
and claimed it cured them.

01:02:03.756 --> 01:02:07.356
We know from later on
when he fessed up that

01:02:07.516 --> 01:02:11.356
those claims were wildly
exaggerated, but they were widely

01:02:11.365 --> 01:02:14.596
accepted, and malarial treatment

01:02:14.716 --> 01:02:19.796
spread to Britain, it spread to Germany,
it spread to the United States.

01:02:21.015 --> 01:02:26.396
Sometimes it was vials of malarial
blood, but very often mental

01:02:26.406 --> 01:02:29.686
hospitals had actual colonies
of malarial mosquitoes.

01:02:30.386 --> 01:02:34.336
So imagine you're a mental patient, and
you're put in a straitjacket, and you're put

01:02:34.376 --> 01:02:38.366
in a room, and you can't move, and
mosquitoes are buzzing around,

01:02:38.396 --> 01:02:40.956
and they bite you, and
then you develop malaria.

01:02:40.986 --> 01:02:42.736
- And he got a Nobel Prize for this?

01:02:42.816 --> 01:02:46.406
- Yes, in 1937, he got a Nobel
Prize. 'Cause this was a

01:02:46.436 --> 01:02:53.536
condition that was invariably fatal.
And the claim was that somehow

01:02:53.596 --> 01:02:57.326
the malarial fever worked.
Now, there are two possible

01:02:57.416 --> 01:03:01.116
ways. Von Jauregg thought
it stimulated the

01:03:01.136 --> 01:03:04.916
immune system to attack whatever
it was, was causing the insanity.

01:03:05.306 --> 01:03:08.816
But the other possibility
was the following: When

01:03:08.896 --> 01:03:12.416
you have the malarial parasite in a test

01:03:12.516 --> 01:03:20.196
tube and you heat the test tube to about
105, 106 degrees, it dies. So the idea

01:03:20.216 --> 01:03:23.576
potentially was you were sort of burning
the parasites out of the brain with

01:03:23.636 --> 01:03:29.636
this agent. And because people
were pretty unsophisticated about

01:03:29.716 --> 01:03:35.366
statistics and because the idea of a
controlled trial had not yet come to pass,

01:03:36.316 --> 01:03:42.666
this treatment was used extensively
for a couple of decades. What

01:03:42.736 --> 01:03:45.976
caused it to stop, and you talk
about the progress of science,

01:03:46.956 --> 01:03:51.286
was the discovery of penicillin,
which was a real magic bullet.

01:03:51.576 --> 01:03:55.496
So once you had penicillin, you
weren't gonna continue treating people

01:03:55.536 --> 01:03:59.306
with malaria, so it died
away. But it was the first

01:03:59.336 --> 01:04:03.176
such treatment, and here's the
other way this feeds into the

01:04:03.256 --> 01:04:06.996
narrative of these desperate
remedies that develop in this

01:04:07.056 --> 01:04:10.696
period between the
mid-'teens and, say, 1950.

01:04:12.216 --> 01:04:17.336
The discovery of the syphilitic origins
of general paralysis of the insane

01:04:18.296 --> 01:04:21.716
occurred at a time when
medicine had undergone a

01:04:21.916 --> 01:04:25.736
undergone a profound
transformation. In the late 19th

01:04:25.776 --> 01:04:30.276
century, the work of Louis Pasteur,
who was a chemist, not an MD,

01:04:31.076 --> 01:04:35.846
and the work of Robert Koch
in Germany had uncovered

01:04:36.676 --> 01:04:45.096
the origins of a variety of diseases and
suggested that bacteria were the reason why

01:04:45.116 --> 01:04:51.016
people sickened. And that led, of
course, to a whole series of public

01:04:51.076 --> 01:04:57.136
health triumphs, because initially it
didn't lead to antibiotics. But for a lot

01:04:57.176 --> 01:05:04.486
of these diseases, even viral diseases
like rabies, you could develop a vaccine.

01:05:05.196 --> 01:05:10.336
And the vaccines were
phenomenally effective. And so

01:05:11.196 --> 01:05:16.096
that was one way in which the
new germ theory of disease

01:05:16.316 --> 01:05:20.986
transformed medicine and tied it into the
laboratory and into science in a new way.

01:05:22.136 --> 01:05:26.036
And the other was the adaptation
of Pasteur's theories

01:05:26.226 --> 01:05:33.776
by a British surgeon named Lister,
who previously when pus developed

01:05:33.836 --> 01:05:37.235
post-surgery, people had
thought that was a good sign.

01:05:38.976 --> 01:05:42.516
Lister said, "No, I don't think
so. This is actually these

01:05:42.636 --> 01:05:45.936
nasty germs causing this,
and so we're gonna do

01:05:46.076 --> 01:05:49.936
antiseptic surgery." So
he sprayed carbolic acid

01:05:50.016 --> 01:05:52.876
on the wounds to try to kill the microbes.

01:05:54.056 --> 01:05:57.956
Most of his colleagues thought he
was nuts. Thought he was just-- This

01:05:58.016 --> 01:06:01.916
is ridiculous, these microorganisms. You
couldn't even see them, you know? Well, you

01:06:02.036 --> 01:06:07.396
could with a... But Lister prevailed.
And eventually we moved from

01:06:07.406 --> 01:06:11.366
antiseptic surgery to aseptic
surgery, which is what we have now,

01:06:11.376 --> 01:06:15.066
where you try to have a sterilized set of

01:06:15.096 --> 01:06:18.986
instruments in a sterilized environment,
so you don't infect things, right?

01:06:19.276 --> 01:06:23.616
- By the way, these are definitive
examples of progress in medicine.

01:06:23.696 --> 01:06:24.216
- Absolutely.

01:06:24.516 --> 01:06:25.056
- Penicillin--

01:06:25.256 --> 01:06:25.776
- Yes.

01:06:25.796 --> 01:06:30.226
- ...uh, you know, making sure
there's no germs in during surgery.

01:06:30.226 --> 01:06:30.836
- Associated with wounds, yes.

01:06:30.896 --> 01:06:34.816
- So these are all just refreshingly
clear examples of progress.

01:06:35.236 --> 01:06:39.076
The reason I say it's refreshingly clear
that there's progress, there's not a

01:06:39.136 --> 01:06:44.376
refreshingly clear progress in the history
of psychiatry. Maybe, maybe a few hints.

01:06:44.386 --> 01:06:46.416
- Some bits, yes. There haven't been

01:06:47.456 --> 01:06:50.836
the dramatic breakthroughs that
I think everybody in the field

01:06:51.456 --> 01:06:55.206
would hope for. And
there's some debate about

01:06:55.256 --> 01:06:59.186
how powerful what we have done is, and I

01:06:59.216 --> 01:07:03.056
think it's reasonable to debate
that and to also acknowledge

01:07:03.076 --> 01:07:11.096
that there is important progress, limited
as it is. Now you have 25% of the admits

01:07:11.156 --> 01:07:14.836
to a mental hospital actually
suffering from an infectious

01:07:14.956 --> 01:07:19.736
disease. Medicine in general has now
tied its fortunes to the laboratory.

01:07:20.736 --> 01:07:26.644
The idea that disease is caused by
bacteria, we can't yet see viruses.

01:07:28.264 --> 01:07:35.464
It is a very powerful one and that notion
that disease is caused by bacterial

01:07:35.504 --> 01:07:39.284
infection acquires great momentum but it

01:07:39.384 --> 01:07:44.724
hasn't touched psychiatry so medicine
now has interventions that work

01:07:46.104 --> 01:07:49.364
And sometimes quite dramatically

01:07:50.084 --> 01:07:53.884
um you know the first patient
given diphtheria vaccine--

01:07:54.004 --> 01:07:57.664
Diphtheria causes a leather
like membrane to grow over your

01:07:57.674 --> 01:08:01.414
throat and you die choking
to death And if you have a

01:08:01.464 --> 01:08:07.064
child and you watch that child die
like that You will never be over it

01:08:08.204 --> 01:08:11.364
So when you had something
that warded that off

01:08:13.184 --> 01:08:17.024
That improved medicine's
image dramatically and

01:08:17.044 --> 01:08:20.994
improved its financial prospects
dramatically particularly

01:08:21.204 --> 01:08:27.844
as medical training became reformed
and more involved with science

01:08:30.264 --> 01:08:34.604
So that hasn't applied to psychiatry until

01:08:35.064 --> 01:08:38.704
syphilis comes along Now
we have the model that an

01:08:38.764 --> 01:08:46.184
infectious agent can cause
people's minds to go amok

01:08:46.484 --> 01:08:50.304
So The very person who
invents the basic distinction

01:08:50.324 --> 01:08:56.964
between kinds of psychoses we still use
today schizophrenia and bipolar disorder

01:08:57.964 --> 01:09:01.904
Emil Kraepelin begins to think "You
know there may be something infectious

01:09:02.024 --> 01:09:04.044
about the mental illnesses
we're treating."

01:09:05.064 --> 01:09:07.304
And one of the people he trains is a young

01:09:08.364 --> 01:09:12.344
psychiatrist from the United States
named Henry Cotton Who's also

01:09:12.384 --> 01:09:16.314
been trained by Adolf
Meyer who is the leading

01:09:16.314 --> 01:09:19.924
American psychiatrist of the first
forty years of the twentieth century

01:09:21.284 --> 01:09:24.364
And when he comes back from
Germany having spent a year there

01:09:25.024 --> 01:09:28.764
Meyer secures him a position as
head of the New Jersey State Mental

01:09:28.824 --> 01:09:34.724
Hospital at Trenton and Cotton
is an ambitious reforming man

01:09:35.264 --> 01:09:38.944
He wants to bring psychiatry
back to medicine He also

01:09:39.004 --> 01:09:42.724
wants to chuck out all the
old stuff He doesn't want

01:09:43.244 --> 01:09:47.214
chains in his hospital He doesn't want
If he can help it straight jackets

01:09:47.604 --> 01:09:54.313
So that goes away But nothing seems to
change fundamentally He still isn't

01:09:54.344 --> 01:09:58.144
curing patients And then he
comes across this idea of

01:09:58.184 --> 01:10:01.644
focal sepsis The idea that low grade

01:10:01.704 --> 01:10:05.284
infections can lurk in the body
and what they do is release

01:10:05.404 --> 01:10:08.724
toxins into the bloodstream
and the lymph And hey

01:10:09.184 --> 01:10:13.914
imagine if those toxins get to
the brain What's it going to do?

01:10:13.914 --> 01:10:17.704
It's gonna poison the brain and
the brain is then gonna act up

01:10:19.084 --> 01:10:23.324
so We don't have antibiotics So
what are we gonna do about this

01:10:24.484 --> 01:10:28.434
Well we can perhaps locate
the bacterial infection

01:10:29.464 --> 01:10:34.554
and then we can Get rid of it We can engage
in what he calls surgical bacteriology

01:10:35.764 --> 01:10:39.304
So the first obvious target here is teeth

01:10:40.464 --> 01:10:43.804
Your teeth look close to your
brain They're often infected

01:10:44.404 --> 01:10:50.524
That infection often goes untreated for
a time... So we pull a lot of teeth

01:10:52.204 --> 01:10:58.184
Patients don't get better Mm Maybe
the theory's wrong No Um tonsils

01:10:58.864 --> 01:11:03.704
well they're getting infected so we'll
remove them Still don't get better

01:11:05.144 --> 01:11:11.124
Well they're swallowing the bacteria so
we remove stomachs and we move spleens

01:11:11.164 --> 01:11:15.084
and we remove colons And we claim
to be curing eighty percent

01:11:15.144 --> 01:11:19.064
of our patients And rich patients
come from all over America

01:11:19.104 --> 01:11:21.364
to be treated with this novel treatment

01:11:21.824 --> 01:11:22.444
- Crazy

01:11:22.744 --> 01:11:26.604
- Cotton gives A series of lectures
at Princeton There were not

01:11:26.644 --> 01:11:30.124
some lectures which are given by
Nobel Prize winners. There's a very

01:11:30.224 --> 01:11:31.264
prestigious series.

01:11:32.504 --> 01:11:36.424
Uh, it's published by Oxford University
Press and Princeton University Press.

01:11:36.464 --> 01:11:40.984
The New York Times hails it as a
great breakthrough. And in reality,

01:11:41.924 --> 01:11:46.084
45% of the people who get the
abdominal surgery die within a year.

01:11:46.364 --> 01:11:47.564
- They're cutting out stomachs.

01:11:47.844 --> 01:11:51.784
- Yep. He's cutting out stomachs. He
says--there's this passage in one of his papers

01:11:51.824 --> 01:11:55.584
where he says, "Stomachs are like
cement mixers on a construction

01:11:55.644 --> 01:11:59.604
site and could be dispensed
with." When you think, oh my God,

01:12:00.824 --> 01:12:04.604
if you were released from
Trenton and you had no

01:12:04.644 --> 01:12:09.804
teeth, people immediately knew you were an
ex-patient because the word had spread.

01:12:10.864 --> 01:12:17.744
And this goes on for--starting
in 1916, Cotton drops dead of a

01:12:17.784 --> 01:12:24.664
heart attack in 1933, but he's succeeded
by three people he's trained. They

01:12:24.733 --> 01:12:28.624
drop the abdominal surgery. They
use colonic irrigation, but

01:12:29.024 --> 01:12:34.104
but the teeth and the tonsils keep
being pulled. I interviewed the dentist

01:12:34.904 --> 01:12:38.744
who had come to the hospital in
1916 and must have pulled several

01:12:38.804 --> 01:12:40.084
hundred thousand teeth.

01:12:41.204 --> 01:12:45.404
In nine-- and he retired in 1960,
which was when that finally stopped.

01:12:46.304 --> 01:12:51.704
And he was convinced Cotton should have
won the Nobel Prize for this. Oh, well.

01:12:51.984 --> 01:12:55.424
- Can you just give some
intuition, put ourselves in that

01:12:55.524 --> 01:12:59.164
mind space? I mean,
presumably these are smart

01:12:59.304 --> 01:13:02.784
human beings. Why were they
fraudulent in the reporting of how

01:13:02.844 --> 01:13:06.744
effective it is? Why are all the
people that are participating,

01:13:07.484 --> 01:13:10.304
both the doctors and general culture--

01:13:11.044 --> 01:13:13.404
- It's extraordinary. So obviously,

01:13:15.404 --> 01:13:19.364
many of the interventions I'm
talking about are very powerful

01:13:19.404 --> 01:13:24.404
interventions conducted by people in white
coats and stethoscopes and scalpels.

01:13:24.784 --> 01:13:28.664
- Powerful, by the way,
by the amount of impact

01:13:28.704 --> 01:13:31.784
they have on the human body, not powerful
in terms of how effective they are.

01:13:31.884 --> 01:13:35.244
- Yes, powerful as well in
terms of placebo effect.

01:13:36.144 --> 01:13:40.124
Look, what I am going to do to
you, and yes, it's gonna hurt

01:13:40.164 --> 01:13:43.264
and it's very intimate, but
it's going to make you better.

01:13:44.394 --> 01:13:47.794
So that's always-- this is
a problem that persists in

01:13:47.874 --> 01:13:50.354
contemporary psychiatry,
trying to figure out

01:13:51.174 --> 01:13:53.114
how much of the improvement we're seeing

01:13:54.094 --> 01:13:58.074
is the placebo effect and how-- how
much of it is the active effect of

01:13:58.094 --> 01:13:58.934
whatever we're doing.

01:13:59.554 --> 01:14:03.974
- But by the way, on that small tangent,
let's return to that perhaps often.

01:14:04.034 --> 01:14:04.334
- Yes.

01:14:04.654 --> 01:14:08.494
- I for one can tell you
that for me, for my mind,

01:14:10.034 --> 01:14:14.914
the placebo effect even when you
tell me it's placebo will work.

01:14:15.134 --> 01:14:15.234
- Yes!

01:14:15.274 --> 01:14:18.924
- But now if you have
combined an actual gigantic

01:14:19.034 --> 01:14:25.154
operation that is physically,
mentally, in every way life changing -

01:14:25.194 --> 01:14:29.184
... everybody around you in lab
coats, all of society's telling

01:14:29.214 --> 01:14:32.814
you this is going to be life changing.
I get it. That's like the most

01:14:32.994 --> 01:14:34.414
pure kind of placebo effect.

01:14:34.624 --> 01:14:39.234
- It's placebo effect that comes from both
the patient who wants to be better-

01:14:39.254 --> 01:14:42.734
... and wants to believe this is
gonna make them, and from the person

01:14:42.774 --> 01:14:46.114
conducting. And it's easy
to deceive yourself,

01:14:46.814 --> 01:14:52.374
to see what you want to see. So
anyway, that was one episode. Um,

01:14:53.644 --> 01:14:57.044
we talk about triumphs of medicine
so let me talk about one of

01:14:57.094 --> 01:15:01.754
the real triumphs of 20th century medicine

01:15:03.794 --> 01:15:08.474
which is interesting to refer
to because it wasn't a cure,

01:15:09.914 --> 01:15:13.414
just like psychiatric drugs
aren't a cure for mental illness.

01:15:14.094 --> 01:15:17.714
It was a symptomatic treatment
but it transformed lives

01:15:18.354 --> 01:15:22.054
and that was discovery
of insulin in the 1920s.

01:15:23.014 --> 01:15:26.554
Previously particularly
what we now call type

01:15:26.674 --> 01:15:29.574
one or juvenile diabetes
was a death sentence.

01:15:30.674 --> 01:15:34.374
You got it and whatever you
did, you tried various quack

01:15:34.394 --> 01:15:38.254
remedies, you tried diet, you
tried all sorts of things. The

01:15:38.314 --> 01:15:42.494
inevitable thing was it killed
you. And then came insulin.

01:15:43.464 --> 01:15:48.114
Now insulin doesn't mean you're cured of
dia- your diabetes but what it means is

01:15:49.014 --> 01:15:52.794
you can live a relatively normal
life and your lifespan is

01:15:52.914 --> 01:15:59.374
greatly expanded. So by any measure you
have to say that's dramatic progress.

01:16:00.294 --> 01:16:04.794
But insulin is something
our bodies produce we hope

01:16:04.874 --> 01:16:08.734
unless we're really seriously
diabetic. Those of us with Type

01:16:08.744 --> 01:16:12.714
Two diabetes, our bodies
resist insulin and we have

01:16:12.754 --> 01:16:16.654
to resort to other ways
of trying to cope. But

01:16:16.754 --> 01:16:20.734
if you get too much insulin,
it makes you unconscious

01:16:21.854 --> 01:16:27.334
and that's how another one of these
desperate remedies came along.

01:16:28.714 --> 01:16:32.334
A man named Sakel working
in a German clinic

01:16:32.874 --> 01:16:36.754
for drug addicts they were using putting

01:16:36.794 --> 01:16:40.354
people under mild comas
to help them through the

01:16:40.394 --> 01:16:44.134
withdrawal symptoms once they got
over their addiction. So he was

01:16:44.174 --> 01:16:47.814
familiar with that. And when
he moved to Austria, he

01:16:47.854 --> 01:16:51.894
decided he'd try this as a
treatment for schizophrenia.

01:16:52.874 --> 01:16:56.714
And so he put people into comas
sometimes comas that would

01:16:56.774 --> 01:17:01.774
last hours days um they would be
revived by giving them glucose

01:17:02.734 --> 01:17:06.474
usually intravenously sometimes not. Um

01:17:06.554 --> 01:17:10.244
during the time they were in
comas they often seized had

01:17:10.294 --> 01:17:14.154
seizures. He saw that
as a therapeutic sign.

01:17:14.254 --> 01:17:17.654
And he claimed that this
Insulin Coma Treatment

01:17:18.634 --> 01:17:22.514
cured eighty percent of his -- eighty
percent tends to come up again and again

01:17:22.594 --> 01:17:25.554
in these treatments as a sort
of percentage that you cure.

01:17:26.694 --> 01:17:30.554
And he was invited to New York

01:17:31.134 --> 01:17:34.534
and demonstrated this at the
Harlem Valley Mental Hospital.

01:17:35.294 --> 01:17:39.034
It spread. This is in the
thirties? This was starting in

01:17:39.074 --> 01:17:42.834
nineteen thirty-three. The visit to
America was I believe nineteen thirty-

01:17:43.074 --> 01:17:46.844
six and Sakel ended up
settling here. He had a very

01:17:46.954 --> 01:17:50.814
lucrative private practice in
New York and when he died he

01:17:50.874 --> 01:17:54.714
left his partner I think an estate
of about two million dollars which

01:17:54.774 --> 01:17:58.674
in the early sixties was
a very substantial amount

01:17:58.694 --> 01:18:02.334
of money that he'd earned
from practice, right? So

01:18:02.414 --> 01:18:06.214
insulin coma therapy was
widely adopted. What kept it

01:18:06.274 --> 01:18:10.194
from being a large scale thing
was it required an enormous

01:18:10.254 --> 01:18:14.154
amount of nursing and medical
attention 'cause people were literally

01:18:14.194 --> 01:18:17.814
hovering on the brink of life and
death. They could go into a permanent

01:18:17.894 --> 01:18:21.154
coma, they could just die. Um, so they had

01:18:21.754 --> 01:18:25.534
vital signs had to be monitored.
They had to be brought around very

01:18:25.614 --> 01:18:31.874
quickly if need be. And there's some
evidence that the treatment killed

01:18:31.934 --> 01:18:35.434
brain cells. And when Sakel was told
that, he said, "Yes, that's probably

01:18:35.474 --> 01:18:39.284
true. They're killing the schizophrenic
brain cells." That's just

01:18:39.314 --> 01:18:41.794
nonsense, of course. That's
just nonsense, but that's

01:18:41.814 --> 01:18:45.474
his rationale. Insulin comas
weren't subjected to a

01:18:45.514 --> 01:18:48.454
randomized controlled
trial until the nineteen

01:18:48.494 --> 01:18:52.214
fifties. And when they were
subjected to a controlled

01:18:52.274 --> 01:18:56.194
trial, they failed it, and so it died out.

01:18:56.234 --> 01:19:00.114
And that's, I guess, scientific
progress again in a way, but

01:19:00.124 --> 01:19:03.014
took a long time. One of the people who

01:19:03.034 --> 01:19:08.914
received insulin coma therapy—have you seen
the film A Beautiful Mind? Mm-hmm, he did.

01:19:09.014 --> 01:19:15.414
Receive insulin coma therapy. Ironically, actually at Trenton
State Hospital where Cotton had been, so John Nash received

01:19:15.424 --> 01:19:19.434
John Nash got insulin comas, and
they were going to lobotomize him,

01:19:20.714 --> 01:19:25.734
and they didn't, but he was at
risk of that. We should say

01:19:25.794 --> 01:19:48.014
that this treatment, patients would thrash, moan, and convulse before falling into a coma. The treatment required a course of up to sixty comas. It turned out
to have a mortality rate of one to five percent and caused significant brain damage and obesity. Yeah, yet was hailed as a miracle cure for schizophrenia.

01:19:48.494 --> 01:19:52.854
Yes, that's right. Performed
on John Nash, one of the great

01:19:53.390 --> 01:19:55.050
- minds of the 20th century.

01:19:55.270 --> 01:19:57.970
- Yes, and Nash clearly
did become delusional,

01:19:58.970 --> 01:20:02.770
but that was one of the
treatments he was subjected to,

01:20:03.130 --> 01:20:07.510
and well represented, actually,
in the film of A Beautiful Mind.

01:20:08.470 --> 01:20:13.290
So I mentioned seizures. Also in
Austria-Hungary in this period,

01:20:14.110 --> 01:20:20.570
another psychiatrist decided
that you couldn't be both

01:20:20.650 --> 01:20:27.150
schizophrenic and epileptic. That there
was somehow an antagonism between the two.

01:20:28.170 --> 01:20:31.110
So if you had epilepsy, you didn't
have schizophrenia. If you had

01:20:31.230 --> 01:20:33.450
schizophrenia, you couldn't have epilepsy.

01:20:34.490 --> 01:20:38.730
Uhh... I should say at the outset, that's
not true, but that's what he believed.

01:20:39.550 --> 01:20:43.510
So then, the logical next step
was, "Well, if we could create

01:20:43.570 --> 01:20:48.510
an artificial epileptic seizure, maybe
we would drive out the schizophrenia."

01:20:50.430 --> 01:20:53.950
So, what to do? He first tries injecting

01:20:54.070 --> 01:20:57.770
camphor, a natural substance. Natural
substances aren't necessarily

01:20:57.810 --> 01:21:01.740
benign substances. That
caused abscesses, and it

01:21:01.770 --> 01:21:05.590
wasn't very effective. He sought
an alternative, and he settled

01:21:05.630 --> 01:21:09.450
on something that was,
um, called Cardiazol

01:21:11.150 --> 01:21:14.350
or Metrazol, depended which side
of the Atlantic you were on.

01:21:15.390 --> 01:21:19.010
And injecting that into a
patient usually caused a

01:21:19.090 --> 01:21:22.790
seizure, a big seizure like
a grand mal seizure, where

01:21:22.850 --> 01:21:27.110
your body arcs back, your
legs contract dramatically.

01:21:27.230 --> 01:21:30.510
- You can fracture spines
and hips and bones.

01:21:30.950 --> 01:21:34.930
- And you ended up, yes, with
fractures of vertebrae, fractures

01:21:34.970 --> 01:21:38.830
of the hip socket. Because
when muscles in the

01:21:38.850 --> 01:21:42.650
thigh contract that badly,
what happens? The thigh bone

01:21:42.710 --> 01:21:46.530
is driven into the socket at
such a rate that it fractures,

01:21:46.910 --> 01:21:50.750
right? So these were among the
complications that Metrazol

01:21:50.810 --> 01:21:54.730
produced. More than that,
he himself conceded

01:21:55.530 --> 01:21:59.260
that between the injection
and the seizure, the

01:21:59.390 --> 01:22:03.990
patient felt as though he were on--
or she was on the brink of death.

01:22:04.790 --> 01:22:10.790
Now imagine, pretend you're a mental patient.
You're brought in in a straitjacket, a

01:22:10.870 --> 01:22:14.850
man in a white coat with a big
hypodermic injects something into you,

01:22:16.330 --> 01:22:17.850
You feel as though you're gonna die.

01:22:18.810 --> 01:22:22.710
And maybe that lingers for two,
three, ten minutes, and then

01:22:22.770 --> 01:22:26.350
you seize, with those
possible fractures following.

01:22:27.090 --> 01:22:27.970
- Violent seizures.

01:22:28.090 --> 01:22:34.290
- It's violent and hard to witness
and is very unpredictable.

01:22:35.170 --> 01:22:39.070
So it's used, but people are
not very happy, and that's

01:22:39.130 --> 01:22:46.410
how we get electroconvulsive therapy,
electroshock, as it's first called. Two Italian

01:22:46.930 --> 01:22:50.470
psychiatrists, Cerletti and Bini,
experiment with electricity, and they

01:22:50.650 --> 01:22:52.250
first experiment on dogs.

01:22:53.330 --> 01:22:57.310
And they make a mistake initially. They
have an electrode on the head, an electrode

01:22:57.350 --> 01:23:01.270
on the anus. The electric current passes
through the body, it stops the heart, the

01:23:01.310 --> 01:23:01.850
dogs die.

01:23:03.410 --> 01:23:07.330
So that seems a dead end. And then somebody
says to them, "You know, you should go to

01:23:07.350 --> 01:23:11.110
the Rome slaughterhouse and see the pigs
being slaughtered, 'cause you'll learn

01:23:11.150 --> 01:23:14.990
something very interesting." So they
go, and the pigs are dangling by their

01:23:15.030 --> 01:23:20.770
hind legs, and as they come by,
two electrodes cross their head.

01:23:21.490 --> 01:23:25.170
Electroshock, they convulse,
they're unconscious, their

01:23:25.210 --> 01:23:35.280
throats are slit and pork arrives. So, um,
they try that on dogs, and current passing

01:23:35.330 --> 01:23:37.250
through the brain, it turns
out, doesn't kill them.

01:23:38.170 --> 01:23:41.570
So they decide to try it out.
They pick up a transient

01:23:41.650 --> 01:23:46.590
homeless person at the Rome train
station and they bring him in.

01:23:48.250 --> 01:23:52.150
And they try it, and at first
they don't use enough current.

01:23:52.170 --> 01:23:56.030
And nothing very much happens.
And they're very white-

01:23:56.090 --> 01:23:59.790
faced, they're quite worried off in the corner.
We have descriptions of this, and they're

01:23:59.830 --> 01:24:03.610
talking, "What should we do?" "Well,
we'll up the current." And the patient

01:24:03.670 --> 01:24:10.010
hears that and says, "No, another one, that's
deadly." They do it anyway, and he convulses.

01:24:10.709 --> 01:24:14.149
Another grand mal seizure, with the
same problems of spinal fractures

01:24:14.190 --> 01:24:19.930
and hip fractures and so on. Not
universally, obviously, but often enough.

01:24:21.030 --> 01:24:25.270
And he stops breathing.
You can imagine the scene.

01:24:27.230 --> 01:24:31.110
And then he spontaneously starts breathing
again, and when he comes around,

01:24:32.190 --> 01:24:36.170
he's in contact with reality.
They got this miracle cure,

01:24:36.210 --> 01:24:39.110
and it's very easy to administer, cheap,

01:24:40.430 --> 01:24:46.830
doesn't involve injecting things into
people's bodies. So that quickly spreads

01:24:47.930 --> 01:24:50.940
across the Atlantic and
other parts of Europe.

01:24:52.050 --> 01:24:57.840
And ECT becomes a very
widely used intervention.

01:24:59.210 --> 01:25:00.910
Couple of things to say about this.

01:25:02.050 --> 01:25:05.890
It turns out it's not very
useful for schizophrenia.

01:25:07.390 --> 01:25:10.930
Remember the connection
between seizures and

01:25:10.990 --> 01:25:14.710
seizure and schizophrenia
that was originally posited

01:25:15.290 --> 01:25:18.470
posited, but it seems to
work in cases of, um,

01:25:18.810 --> 01:25:21.650
depression, suicidal
depression particularly.

01:25:22.010 --> 01:25:25.540
- Fast forwarding to the modern day.
Mm-hmm. And this is something I

01:25:26.790 --> 01:25:31.840
learned by reading a bunch
recently. It seems to be one of the

01:25:32.970 --> 01:25:36.860
few evidence-based, like scientifically
backed methods that actually

01:25:36.890 --> 01:25:39.840
worked for clinical depression,
for serious depression.

01:25:40.030 --> 01:25:43.830
- Um, yes, if we fast forward,
we're looking at in some

01:25:43.910 --> 01:25:47.280
respects a different animal for
reasons I'll explain. Okay.

01:25:47.310 --> 01:25:48.700
- This is unmodified ECT.

01:25:49.170 --> 01:25:53.630
- Yeah, so we're talking about
unmodified ECT, which rules the roost

01:25:54.930 --> 01:25:58.250
really well into the 1950s,
and some places even into the

01:25:58.290 --> 01:26:02.050
1960s. And so it is associated
with all the problems of

01:26:02.170 --> 01:26:05.930
fractures that we've talked about.
It's also associated with memory

01:26:05.970 --> 01:26:09.770
problems. People often
lose memory. There's some

01:26:09.810 --> 01:26:13.730
dispute about how serious that is,
but it's pretty widely recognized

01:26:13.810 --> 01:26:16.410
that's one of the prices you're
going to pay for that treatment.

01:26:17.330 --> 01:26:22.110
The thing is, for mental
hospitals in the '40s and '50s,

01:26:23.110 --> 01:26:26.230
ECT was much more used as a

01:26:26.930 --> 01:26:30.370
device to control people's
behavior, than as a therapeutic

01:26:30.430 --> 01:26:38.280
intervention. It was quite punitive, seen
as such. Patients didn't want to repeat,

01:26:39.030 --> 01:26:43.870
and so they-- ...sort of controlled
themselves a bit. But yes,

01:26:44.750 --> 01:26:52.290
we don't know why it, quote, "works,"
but more recent work starting in

01:26:52.810 --> 01:26:56.730
probably the 1990s -- and I'm going to get
in trouble with some people for saying this

01:26:56.810 --> 01:26:59.790
because you mentioned patients and

01:27:00.150 --> 01:27:05.330
psychiatrists who swear by ECT.
There are others who swear at it.

01:27:05.690 --> 01:27:09.510
Um, partly because of the
memory problems I alluded

01:27:09.590 --> 01:27:13.190
to, and partly because of
claims that it may cause

01:27:13.630 --> 01:27:17.570
brain damage. Passing an electric
current through the brain is possible.

01:27:18.270 --> 01:27:25.250
What changed ECT a bit, quite a bit
actually, was giving muscle relaxants

01:27:26.270 --> 01:27:30.260
so that people didn't thrash about, and
the fractures were largely a thing of

01:27:30.290 --> 01:27:35.130
the past. When you introduce these muscle
relaxers, originally they used curare,

01:27:36.110 --> 01:27:39.730
but then they used
other, more modern drugs

01:27:39.810 --> 01:27:43.570
to paralyze the muscles temporarily.
The problem is, that would also

01:27:43.710 --> 01:27:47.010
paralyze your breathing muscles,
so that's not too good.

01:27:47.910 --> 01:27:51.370
So it became a more complicated
procedure because you needed

01:27:51.390 --> 01:27:55.370
anesthesiologists, breathing
support, and so forth

01:27:55.450 --> 01:27:59.210
during the procedure. But
you did eliminate the

01:27:59.230 --> 01:28:02.749
fractures. It still was a very widely

01:28:02.770 --> 01:28:06.410
disdained practice, I think,
particularly when they had drugs

01:28:06.470 --> 01:28:10.419
available. The thinking was that,
well, we'd sooner use those, but

01:28:10.450 --> 01:28:14.090
the drugs turn out to be
only partially effective,

01:28:14.130 --> 01:28:17.840
and pretty ineffective very
often for suicidal cases or in

01:28:17.870 --> 01:28:21.670
cases of extreme melancholia.
Now, a couple of

01:28:21.690 --> 01:28:25.170
things to say: Very often,
ECT has to be repeated at

01:28:25.250 --> 01:28:28.310
intervals, as a kind of
maintenance therapy.

01:28:28.490 --> 01:28:32.150
So it hasn't cured things,
but it temporarily

01:28:32.210 --> 01:28:36.060
alleviates the symptoms, and the
temporary may be fairly lengthy, but

01:28:36.150 --> 01:28:42.070
nonetheless, very often things will recur.
The memory problems can be quite severe.

01:28:43.190 --> 01:28:46.819
The worries about brain
damage are, I think,

01:28:47.970 --> 01:28:51.830
certainly things we have
to be very cautious about.

01:28:52.650 --> 01:28:55.880
And when we talk about
treatment-resistant depression,

01:28:56.850 --> 01:29:00.570
that's an interesting concept to
me. What it means is those are the

01:29:00.630 --> 01:29:02.840
patients who don't respond to drugs.

01:29:03.670 --> 01:29:07.650
They may not have a different
disease, but the drugs

01:29:07.670 --> 01:29:10.290
don't work for them, hence
treatment resistant.

01:29:11.090 --> 01:29:18.190
And the numbers of psychiatrists who are
willing to give ECT are rather small,

01:29:19.470 --> 01:29:23.130
and in many states it's hedged around with

01:29:23.270 --> 01:29:27.550
lots of legal restrictions. In
California, for example, um,

01:29:28.350 --> 01:29:31.550
ECT now almost can't be given to

01:29:31.670 --> 01:29:38.220
involuntarily confined patients 'cause you
have to, you have to volunteer for it.

01:29:38.690 --> 01:29:42.670
So as-- so it's unusual in
that most medical procedures

01:29:42.710 --> 01:29:46.510
aren't hedged about by legal
constraints like that. And

01:29:46.550 --> 01:29:50.370
there clearly is a very
powerful group of people,

01:29:50.910 --> 01:29:54.110
some of them psychiatrists,
many of them ex-patients,

01:29:55.190 --> 01:29:59.130
many of them other people who just
are suspicious of modern medicine and

01:29:59.210 --> 01:30:06.090
science, who form a group who are
very powerfully opposed to ECT. So

01:30:06.170 --> 01:30:12.150
although it's fair to say
there are, Trials now that

01:30:13.030 --> 01:30:16.350
seem to provide decent evidence
that for some patients

01:30:16.430 --> 01:30:19.940
this works and that those
patients are deeply

01:30:19.990 --> 01:30:23.950
distressed before the
treatment it's also still

01:30:23.960 --> 01:30:26.610
a controversial treatment,
I think it's fair to say.

01:30:26.690 --> 01:30:36.790
- Like basically every single topic, treatment,
problem subfield of psychiatry today.

01:30:36.910 --> 01:30:37.320
- Yes, yes.

01:30:37.320 --> 01:30:41.150
- For everything, everything we say
today, there will be at least

01:30:41.190 --> 01:30:43.930
one person upset- ...and writing a letter.

01:30:43.950 --> 01:30:48.550
- I think lots of people upset.
So if we talk about drugs,

01:30:49.770 --> 01:30:52.210
there'll be two kinds of
people who will be upset,

01:30:53.490 --> 01:30:57.350
those who think the drugs
are more powerful than they

01:30:57.470 --> 01:31:01.010
are or who have been
successfully treated by the

01:31:01.080 --> 01:31:04.710
drugs and go, "Well, it
worked for me, so, you know,

01:31:05.070 --> 01:31:09.050
stop criticizing it 'cause it really
is an effective treatment." And then

01:31:09.130 --> 01:31:12.810
on the other side of the
coin, there are those who

01:31:13.450 --> 01:31:17.110
either the drug treatment has been
used and it's failed, or they're being

01:31:17.190 --> 01:31:20.130
left with terrible side
effects that don't go away,

01:31:21.130 --> 01:31:24.470
or they're part of a general
group of people that

01:31:24.550 --> 01:31:31.130
unfortunately is of a growing number
these days who are so suspicious

01:31:31.530 --> 01:31:34.670
of medical science and
of the drug companies

01:31:35.510 --> 01:31:41.010
that no amount of evidence will
sway them. They are convinced that

01:31:43.070 --> 01:31:46.570
you know, the drug
treatments are poisonous.

01:31:47.150 --> 01:31:53.930
The Scientologists being a very extreme
example of that, who they have a whole

01:31:54.890 --> 01:32:00.690
museum in Los Angeles and the title
is Psychiatry Industry of Death.

01:32:01.694 --> 01:32:05.564
And then if you think about all
the resistance, for example, that

01:32:05.614 --> 01:32:09.104
has surfaced to vaccination in

01:32:09.134 --> 01:32:12.734
contemporary US and how the
trust in vaccination has been

01:32:12.754 --> 01:32:16.114
destroyed for a substantial
number of people,

01:32:16.874 --> 01:32:21.034
it's very difficult to convince
them that they're mistaken.

01:32:21.134 --> 01:32:24.954
- And not just the trust in
vaccination consequence of that is

01:32:25.034 --> 01:32:26.844
a general distrust in science-

01:32:26.894 --> 01:32:27.974
- A general distrust, exactly.

01:32:27.994 --> 01:32:29.874
- ... and distrust in medicine and so on.

01:32:30.154 --> 01:32:37.294
- That's one of my great worries about
our contemporary situation, that we're

01:32:37.374 --> 01:32:38.874
only-- we're less than a year in.

01:32:40.054 --> 01:32:43.574
After four years of this,
first of all, the degree of

01:32:43.634 --> 01:32:47.594
mistrust will have grown exponentially,
and once trust is lost, it's

01:32:47.654 --> 01:32:53.514
very hard to recover. Secondly,
the science itself is being

01:32:53.534 --> 01:32:58.884
destroyed. Clinical trials
that were midway through were

01:32:58.914 --> 01:33:02.864
aborted, so that knowledge
has been lost. You would have

01:33:02.914 --> 01:33:09.854
to start from square one, and that's years
of work. Scientists aren't being trained

01:33:11.174 --> 01:33:15.034
because funding has been cut. Scientists

01:33:15.254 --> 01:33:20.074
with successful careers no longer have
the funding necessary to do their work,

01:33:21.054 --> 01:33:26.054
and it takes at least six
or seven years to train

01:33:27.134 --> 01:33:29.594
a scientist at the
beginning of their career.

01:33:30.594 --> 01:33:33.974
And so if you have four years
with nobody being trained,

01:33:34.474 --> 01:33:41.394
you're talking about a decade being
lost, and what's lost is invisible--

01:33:41.414 --> 01:33:45.354
...because it's counterfactuals.
We don't know what that science

01:33:45.434 --> 01:33:49.174
will lead to or what that
medical treatment might

01:33:49.234 --> 01:33:53.124
lead to, and very many
times they fail. That's

01:33:53.174 --> 01:33:57.014
the nature of science. It's the nature of

01:33:57.114 --> 01:34:00.914
medicine and medical research
that not every bright

01:34:00.974 --> 01:34:04.834
idea we have is going to
eventuate in a breakthrough.

01:34:05.054 --> 01:34:08.934
It would be really simple and wonderful
if that opposite were the case.

01:34:09.574 --> 01:34:13.434
But the reality is we have to go
down lots of blind alleys, we

01:34:13.494 --> 01:34:16.714
have to try lots of different
things, and we have to take

01:34:17.074 --> 01:34:25.774
years to move from the laboratory to
practical application. And when we eliminate

01:34:26.094 --> 01:34:35.514
a whole segment of that, and when on top
of that we diminish people's trust--

01:34:35.614 --> 01:34:40.854
...in science, that's a
really, I think, a profoundly

01:34:42.234 --> 01:34:45.854
devastating thing that
probably given my advanced

01:34:45.914 --> 01:34:49.454
age, I won't live to see
the consequences of, but

01:34:49.594 --> 01:34:53.514
my children and grandchildren
will. It really

01:34:53.634 --> 01:34:59.614
is something cultural that
you've got to build up trust.

01:35:00.034 --> 01:35:03.814
And it can be destroyed very easily. So
one of the things, to go back to the

01:35:03.854 --> 01:35:10.394
very first question you asked me about, is
psychiatry in crisis? Well, genetics was

01:35:10.434 --> 01:35:14.154
supposed to provide a clear
picture of the origins

01:35:14.254 --> 01:35:17.854
of various mental diseases
'cause they do seem to run in

01:35:17.894 --> 01:35:21.654
families. So the expectation
was once we decoded the

01:35:21.694 --> 01:35:25.094
human genome and we could examine bits
and pieces of it, that we would very

01:35:25.154 --> 01:35:28.924
quickly find a Mendelian gene or set of

01:35:28.974 --> 01:35:32.354
genes for schizophrenia, let
us say. Hasn't happened.

01:35:33.414 --> 01:35:37.374
We now use genome-wide association
studies, that is throwing

01:35:37.454 --> 01:35:40.734
everything in the kitchen soup
into, into the picture, and then

01:35:41.254 --> 01:35:44.734
without any preconditions and seeing
what relates to what. And if we

01:35:44.874 --> 01:35:48.794
use three hundred small variations in

01:35:48.834 --> 01:35:52.694
the genome, we can account for
about ten percent of schizophrenia.

01:35:53.654 --> 01:36:00.314
That's not very powerful. Beyond
that, what psychiatric genetics has

01:36:00.954 --> 01:36:04.834
tended to throw up is
something that undermines the

01:36:04.934 --> 01:36:09.674
distinctions that we've made based
on symptomatology. So if you look,

01:36:10.814 --> 01:36:14.134
there is a great deal of
overlap in the kinds of genetic

01:36:14.294 --> 01:36:17.954
abnormalities that heighten
the susceptibility

01:36:18.034 --> 01:36:23.614
to bipolar disorder or schizophrenia or
autism. There's a lot of overlap there.

01:36:24.414 --> 01:36:28.374
What that suggests is these aren't
distinctive entities in the way

01:36:28.574 --> 01:36:32.734
that, you know... Schizophrenia,
as my friend Robin Murray,

01:36:33.554 --> 01:36:36.874
a British psychiatrist, has to
say, "It really seems to be the

01:36:36.914 --> 01:36:40.874
extreme end of psychosis, the
most serious." But it's sort

01:36:40.914 --> 01:36:47.044
of on a continuum, you know. And so if
psychiatry has to say in ten years,

01:36:47.094 --> 01:36:49.684
as some leading psychiatrists
are speculating,

01:36:50.484 --> 01:36:54.194
that there's no such thing as
schizophrenia, there's no such thing as

01:36:54.234 --> 01:36:57.934
bipolar disorder, that will tend, I

01:36:57.994 --> 01:37:02.204
suspect, to have pretty bad
effects on people's trust-

01:37:02.274 --> 01:37:06.073
...in psychiatry, and yet that's
where the science may lead them.

01:37:06.854 --> 01:37:14.674
So it's a complicated picture, but this
issue of, of trust and its absence

01:37:15.154 --> 01:37:20.984
and is, is vital, I think,
in looking at not just what

01:37:20.994 --> 01:37:24.384
we're talking about today, but
across a whole spectrum of things,

01:37:25.054 --> 01:37:31.694
even outside the medical realm altogether.
If you lose trust in institutions,

01:37:32.314 --> 01:37:36.274
trust in science, trust in history. So

01:37:36.434 --> 01:37:40.134
I think more humility
and less arrogance, more

01:37:40.174 --> 01:37:47.914
willingness to confess the limits of what
we can do, more awareness of the dangers

01:37:48.054 --> 01:37:58.054
of enthusiasm, more skepticism when
we're told something is a breakthrough.

01:37:59.194 --> 01:38:05.994
One of the things I worry about is the
tendency of science journalism and medical

01:38:06.094 --> 01:38:10.443
journalism to hype things and then

01:38:11.254 --> 01:38:15.793
When the hype turns out to be
just that, that undermines trust.

01:38:16.754 --> 01:38:20.854
You know? So be cautious when
things come along- ... don't be

01:38:21.754 --> 01:38:28.294
so sure that it represents a breakthrough.
I'm very worried at the moment. I see,

01:38:29.174 --> 01:38:35.164
Ketamine and psychedelics being propounded
as a miracle cure for depression,

01:38:36.014 --> 01:38:39.854
and the evidence for that is
enormously weak is the best

01:38:39.914 --> 01:38:43.594
way to put it. And I've seen
this movie before too many

01:38:43.714 --> 01:38:49.994
times. You know? I mentioned that
80% cure for Cotton's work, 80% cure

01:38:51.134 --> 01:38:55.574
for insulin coma therapy, 80%
cure for the early asylums.

01:38:56.844 --> 01:39:00.264
This is overblown rhetoric,
and the reality is

01:39:00.354 --> 01:39:03.724
usually progress comes in small steps.

01:39:03.924 --> 01:39:07.834
Sometimes it comes in big
steps. Penicillin, I think

01:39:08.634 --> 01:39:12.994
was a huge step. Uh, I was
lucky enough to grow up

01:39:13.954 --> 01:39:17.714
in the era when penicillin
and other antibiotics

01:39:17.754 --> 01:39:21.694
became widely available. They
hadn't been overused by then.

01:39:21.734 --> 01:39:25.644
And so if I had a strep throat,
I had something that got rid of

01:39:25.674 --> 01:39:29.554
it right away. I didn't run the
risk of heart valve damage, which

01:39:29.634 --> 01:39:33.494
in previous times would've
been the case. So you know,

01:39:33.674 --> 01:39:36.934
once in a while you do have
these dramatic shifts,

01:39:37.774 --> 01:39:41.614
and maybe AI will help
us in that regard, but

01:39:41.694 --> 01:39:45.874
maybe it won't. It's another
potential double-edged sword.

01:39:46.414 --> 01:39:50.174
- I should mention that
psychedelics, psilocybin in

01:39:50.194 --> 01:39:54.094
particular, has been demonized for a long
time. And so there's studies now out of John

01:39:54.174 --> 01:39:59.034
Johns Hopkins- ... that are
doing serious studies on cases

01:39:59.214 --> 01:40:02.824
where it is effective. I
think it's nice to give a

01:40:02.854 --> 01:40:08.594
chance to the different treatments with
the rigor of science, but with caution -

01:40:08.634 --> 01:40:12.094
... and basically ignoring like
you're saying, science journalists

01:40:12.574 --> 01:40:16.174
who are basically hyping every new thing
'cause they have to get clicks and all-

01:40:16.184 --> 01:40:17.054
... this kind of stuff and-

01:40:17.714 --> 01:40:21.783
... Look at the actual science
in the modern day. So the-

01:40:21.813 --> 01:40:25.174
... in the past, the rigor was not
there. In the modern day, there's more.

01:40:25.574 --> 01:40:32.454
- Yes. We can add beyond the realm of science
journalists. I think Science and Nature

01:40:33.434 --> 01:40:37.374
make choices about what they're
gonna foreground, and they too

01:40:37.434 --> 01:40:42.034
have this tendency to look for
things that make a big splash.

01:40:42.374 --> 01:40:44.964
- Oh, you mean the, the editors
and the- ... the major journals.

01:40:44.964 --> 01:40:48.874
- I think the editors, the major journals,
that's what they're looking for. As,

01:40:48.934 --> 01:40:49.514
you know, I mean

01:40:50.994 --> 01:40:54.934
negative findings are very important in
science. They're, they're the things that

01:40:55.234 --> 01:40:59.034
help us avoid mistakes,
but they're not the

01:40:59.054 --> 01:41:01.624
things that are going to get
you published in the journal.

01:41:01.674 --> 01:41:05.784
- Right. So it's not just the
surface level science journalism.

01:41:05.814 --> 01:41:09.804
It is also the extra journals and the
conferences and the publication process.

01:41:10.414 --> 01:41:13.474
- Having lots of scientists working on

01:41:14.354 --> 01:41:17.914
these problems in different
sites and different places

01:41:19.494 --> 01:41:22.734
turns out to be very important,
I think, as a check on

01:41:22.794 --> 01:41:28.194
enthusiasm, as a check on premature
claims that turn out to be unfounded.

01:41:29.214 --> 01:41:33.174
And also because you're often
partially right but you're not

01:41:33.214 --> 01:41:37.444
fully right and someone else
following the same idea-

01:41:37.534 --> 01:41:42.514
... is, is perhaps gonna be a little
closer to the truth than you were. And so

01:41:43.134 --> 01:41:46.883
it's, it's very helpful to have
multi-centered things and not

01:41:47.234 --> 01:41:50.734
everything under one all-knowing- ... uh,

01:41:51.054 --> 01:41:54.734
thing. And that's a problem
with funding agencies. The

01:41:54.754 --> 01:41:59.094
maverick scientist has a
hard time- ... very often-

01:41:59.274 --> 01:42:02.574
... getting a hearing. And we know of lots

01:42:02.634 --> 01:42:06.554
of examples of that in
history where after the

01:42:06.594 --> 01:42:10.574
fact we go "oh well yes," we
should have supported that line of

01:42:10.594 --> 01:42:12.354
research, but we didn't."

01:42:12.434 --> 01:42:15.674
- Uh, very well put. Let us return to,

01:42:15.814 --> 01:42:19.734
the origins of ECT and how it was
applied. But first, if it's okay,

01:42:19.794 --> 01:42:20.794
a quick bathroom break.

01:42:21.874 --> 01:42:25.674
Quick ten second thank you to our sponsors.
Check them out in the description. It

01:42:25.714 --> 01:42:28.453
really is the best way to
support this podcast. Go to

01:42:28.494 --> 01:42:31.974
lexfridman.com/sponsors. And now dear

01:42:32.013 --> 01:42:38.224
friends back to my conversation
with Andrew Scull. Now we're back

01:42:39.074 --> 01:42:42.374
just to talk a little bit more about
ECT and One Flew Over the Cuckoo's

01:42:42.454 --> 01:42:46.234
Nest. So what can we say about
that, that little cultural

01:42:46.274 --> 01:42:50.034
moment, one of the most famous
moments about psychiatry?

01:42:50.434 --> 01:42:54.374
First of all, Ken Kesey wrote this
book about his own experience

01:42:54.414 --> 01:42:55.273
in a mental institution.

01:42:55.314 --> 01:42:56.734
- Yeah, Menlo Park. Yes.

01:42:57.114 --> 01:43:00.494
- How representative is it
of the system at the time?

01:43:01.354 --> 01:43:08.194
- Mental hospitals have had a very
patchy and complicated history.

01:43:09.094 --> 01:43:11.934
He was working actually in
a hospital for veterans.

01:43:12.914 --> 01:43:16.014
Those were largely created
after the Second World War

01:43:16.794 --> 01:43:20.554
when there were very many more
psychiatric casualties among

01:43:20.694 --> 01:43:27.344
American troops than even the First World
War. The interesting thing is we all by

01:43:27.414 --> 01:43:31.294
osmosis know that in the First World
War there was something called

01:43:31.334 --> 01:43:34.954
shell shock that afflicted
the troops and that the

01:43:34.974 --> 01:43:41.034
military initially resisted recognizing
and ultimately were forced to grasp.

01:43:41.854 --> 01:43:45.554
But in World War II, American psychiatric

01:43:45.754 --> 01:43:49.644
casualties among the troops were two to
three times as high as in World War I,

01:43:50.614 --> 01:43:54.574
and that's an important part of
the history of psychiatry. But the

01:43:54.634 --> 01:43:58.394
upshot of that was that post-war the VA

01:43:58.494 --> 01:44:02.034
was heavily involved in,
first of all, paying to train

01:44:02.294 --> 01:44:08.634
psychiatrists and even psychologists,
and then had in its mental hospital

01:44:08.674 --> 01:44:14.154
system a considerable involvement
with psychiatric disorders.

01:44:15.054 --> 01:44:20.264
Kesey-- Well, the book is different than
the film is the first thing to say.

01:44:21.144 --> 01:44:25.744
Obviously, the film is heavily indebted to
the book, but it changes various things.

01:44:26.204 --> 01:44:29.284
- Uh, if I may just go into
Perplexity, "The book and the

01:44:29.384 --> 01:44:33.124
1975 film tell the same
basic story of McMurphy

01:44:33.684 --> 01:44:37.504
challenging an oppressive psychiatric
ward, but they differ sharply in

01:44:37.544 --> 01:44:41.364
point of view, tone, and what
the story is about. The novel is

01:44:41.464 --> 01:44:45.184
weirder, more political, and
more about Chief Bromden's

01:44:45.344 --> 01:44:49.034
inner world and the combine while the

01:44:49.144 --> 01:44:53.044
film is more naturalistic, character-driven,
and turns McMurphy into the

01:44:53.084 --> 01:44:53.844
central hero."

01:44:54.704 --> 01:44:56.264
- Yes, I think that's right.

01:44:56.544 --> 01:45:00.104
- McMurphy is the person that received
ECT played by Jack Nicholson.

01:45:00.304 --> 01:45:03.164
- Yes. And you have a nurse
figure in Nurse Ratched.

01:45:03.264 --> 01:45:03.634
- Yeah. Yeah.

01:45:03.644 --> 01:45:08.024
- Louise Fletcher, uh, I think is
an equally powerful performance.

01:45:08.124 --> 01:45:11.404
- It's one of the greatest films
of all time who happens to be--

01:45:12.014 --> 01:45:14.474
which is unfortunate for,
you know, maybe psychiatry.

01:45:14.474 --> 01:45:18.344
- Psychiatry, yes. It's very
interesting. I used to teach, um, a

01:45:18.424 --> 01:45:25.044
class called Madness In The Movies,
and, um, I didn't just use... In fact,

01:45:25.184 --> 01:45:32.984
I used relatively few contemporary films
and all among all the films from back

01:45:33.044 --> 01:45:36.864
then, the one almost
everybody in the class had

01:45:36.924 --> 01:45:43.404
seen was One Flew Over the Cuckoo's
Nest. So 18, 19, and 20-year-olds,

01:45:44.284 --> 01:45:48.184
In two thousand and fifteen If I

01:45:48.204 --> 01:45:52.024
showed them Alfred Hitchcock's
Spellbound, no chance they'd ever

01:45:52.104 --> 01:45:53.484
seen that, maybe one,

01:45:54.604 --> 01:45:58.404
because they were a class of people
interested in film. But everybody had

01:45:58.464 --> 01:46:00.784
seen One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest.

01:46:01.104 --> 01:46:06.024
- On that tangent really quick, what is the
greatest film on madness in your view?

01:46:06.324 --> 01:46:06.884
- Oh, well...

01:46:06.924 --> 01:46:08.794
- Would that be One Flew
Over the Cuckoo's Nest?

01:46:08.864 --> 01:46:11.384
- I think One Flew Over the
Cuckoo's Nest, I think

01:46:12.544 --> 01:46:16.474
oddly a very different film
appeared at about the same

01:46:16.544 --> 01:46:19.864
time was I Never Promised
You a Rose Garden,

01:46:20.564 --> 01:46:24.284
which is a much more sympathetic
portrait of a different

01:46:24.364 --> 01:46:28.204
kind of psychiatry, a very
Freudian psychiatrist,

01:46:28.244 --> 01:46:31.943
really about Frieda
Fromm-Reichmann who worked

01:46:32.104 --> 01:46:35.754
at Chestnut Lodge in Maryland and treated

01:46:35.884 --> 01:46:39.644
schizophrenia with psychotherapy
rather than with drugs

01:46:39.804 --> 01:46:46.694
or other forms of physical intervention.
And that was a bestselling novel by a young

01:46:47.104 --> 01:46:54.244
girl who had been her patient with
some fairly serious delusions

01:46:55.224 --> 01:47:00.624
and a very complicated family
background, and again the film

01:47:01.544 --> 01:47:07.184
changed a lot of things in the
novel. That's what films do. My book

01:47:07.264 --> 01:47:11.094
Madhouse at one point interested
Hollywood, and one of the

01:47:11.184 --> 01:47:15.034
two principals said to me, "I really
like this story. It's got a great

01:47:15.124 --> 01:47:18.824
first act and a great second act,
but where's the third act?" Meaning,

01:47:18.944 --> 01:47:22.724
"Where's the happy ending?" And I
had to say there wasn't any happy

01:47:22.744 --> 01:47:25.984
ending to that story. It
was just rather grim.

01:47:26.084 --> 01:47:30.284
- Madhouse: A Tragic Tale of
Megalomania and Modern Medicine

01:47:31.144 --> 01:47:34.614
is the book you're referring to. Yes, that's right.
Now that reminds me of Flowers for Algernon. That

01:47:35.264 --> 01:47:38.824
doesn't have a happy ending, and
it's not about mental health

01:47:38.844 --> 01:47:42.804
necessarily, but it's about the
journey of institution in relation to

01:47:42.864 --> 01:47:43.984
the health of a patient.

01:47:44.104 --> 01:47:47.614
- Yes, Pat Barker's trilogy
of novels about World War

01:47:48.204 --> 01:47:51.204
I was turned into a film.
I think it was called

01:47:51.264 --> 01:47:55.224
Regeneration, and that was quite powerful.

01:47:55.304 --> 01:47:59.144
It was about World War I and the
treatment of shell shock. Mm-hmm.

01:47:59.584 --> 01:48:06.484
And I thought was quite well done. And my
friend Patrick McGrath, who's a novelist,

01:48:07.404 --> 01:48:11.224
wrote a book called
Asylum. Patrick grew up

01:48:11.744 --> 01:48:15.504
in the grounds of Broadmoor.
Broadmoor is England's

01:48:15.544 --> 01:48:17.824
premier hospital for
the criminally insane,

01:48:18.664 --> 01:48:22.624
and he was babysat by some of the patients.
And when you read his novels, you

01:48:22.664 --> 01:48:30.764
can see how that upbringing affected his
rather macabre imagination. But anyway,

01:48:31.444 --> 01:48:35.334
so One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest,
the poverty of the environment,

01:48:36.104 --> 01:48:42.664
room that the patients were in, I think
fairly successfully recreates that

01:48:44.704 --> 01:48:52.373
way in which staff very often put patients
down, didn't listen to them, or poked fun at

01:48:52.424 --> 01:48:56.524
them, or even were physically
abusive, although you don't see that.

01:48:58.144 --> 01:49:03.184
Those were all features of mental
hospitals. The general boredom of life,

01:49:04.544 --> 01:49:07.584
sort of there, but yeah, hard to reprea--

01:49:09.344 --> 01:49:14.474
putting boredom on the screen will
turn an audience off rather fast.

01:49:14.544 --> 01:49:18.644
- What about nurses and this kind
of abusive element, you know?

01:49:18.944 --> 01:49:22.444
- Yeah, I think there was an abusive
element in a lot of mental

01:49:22.504 --> 01:49:30.784
hospitals, and it was almost
inevitable. Look at who had the

01:49:30.824 --> 01:49:34.784
most contact with the patients:
it was the lowest paid,

01:49:35.944 --> 01:49:43.344
least respected ward attendants,
very few even RNs, and you know,

01:49:43.744 --> 01:49:51.214
ratio of doctor to patient in the large
state mental hospitals meant that patients

01:49:51.224 --> 01:49:53.254
hardly ever saw a physician, you know.

01:49:54.124 --> 01:49:57.934
Remarkable about the film,
there are many remarkable

01:49:57.964 --> 01:50:01.064
things about the film as
well as its polemical edge,

01:50:02.244 --> 01:50:06.184
think is that chief
psychiatrist that you see

01:50:06.224 --> 01:50:11.184
in the film is the real head of Oregon
State Mental Hospital. He really was

01:50:12.864 --> 01:50:16.524
introduction to acting, and I thought
he was pretty remarkable, actually.

01:50:17.244 --> 01:50:20.664
Obviously, there's a lot
of exaggeration there, but

01:50:20.984 --> 01:50:24.824
ECT was used in the fifties and sixties

01:50:24.944 --> 01:50:28.864
as a tool of discipline in
the hospitals. It was also

01:50:28.964 --> 01:50:32.684
used therapeutically, but
overwhelmingly it was used

01:50:33.844 --> 01:50:39.304
a tool of discipline and control.
And that's true to life. Lobotomy...

01:50:40.764 --> 01:50:44.384
One of the interesting things we haven't talked
about with all these treatments we've been

01:50:44.424 --> 01:50:48.004
discussing is that almost invariably,

01:50:49.264 --> 01:50:53.164
except for the case of the syphilitic
patients, for obvious reasons, men

01:50:53.204 --> 01:50:56.324
were more troubled by that
condition than women,

01:50:57.284 --> 01:51:01.084
it was women that got the
brunt of these experiments. So

01:51:01.144 --> 01:51:04.794
Henry Cotton, about 70% of his
patients who were treated were

01:51:04.944 --> 01:51:08.814
female. Lobotomy patients,
it's hard to get

01:51:08.924 --> 01:51:12.914
overall numbers, but those
of us who've looked at

01:51:12.944 --> 01:51:16.744
the records of a number of
different hospitals again and

01:51:16.804 --> 01:51:24.624
again discover, again, 60 or 70%
of the patients are female. ECT

01:51:24.664 --> 01:51:28.864
tends to be heavily female. That's
complicated by the fact that

01:51:29.784 --> 01:51:33.584
it's used primarily as,
as we were discussing in

01:51:33.664 --> 01:51:37.544
serious cases of depression and
so-called treatment-resistant

01:51:37.564 --> 01:51:41.454
depression, and depression
is a diagnosis that is

01:51:41.984 --> 01:51:45.804
more to be found among women than
men. Not that there aren't very many

01:51:45.844 --> 01:51:49.644
men with depression, but
again, the ratio is such. Men

01:51:49.704 --> 01:51:52.884
tend to get a different--
they get different diagnoses.

01:51:53.484 --> 01:51:58.364
Personality disorders, for example, are
very, very common and are more a male

01:51:59.224 --> 01:52:08.204
diagnosis. Um, ADHD- ...is more
male than female and so on. So

01:52:08.784 --> 01:52:13.024
it's good to point that
out. Uh, I think it helped

01:52:14.264 --> 01:52:18.024
end interest in ECT, except for a

01:52:18.144 --> 01:52:22.534
small handful of enthusiasts
for decades, that film.

01:52:22.964 --> 01:52:30.204
Um, and probably even now it creates
hesitation in people about the

01:52:30.264 --> 01:52:35.504
treatment. So it's one that has

01:52:35.844 --> 01:52:39.664
had a very powerful and
long-lasting effect, I think.

01:52:39.744 --> 01:52:43.493
- I think the surprising thing is I
recently learned a friend of mine tried

01:52:43.524 --> 01:52:47.364
everything, about 20 years
ago, tried everything to

01:52:47.624 --> 01:52:53.644
--with depression, and ECT is the last
thing he tried, and it changed his life-

01:52:54.024 --> 01:52:54.764
...for the better.

01:52:54.824 --> 01:52:57.844
- That's not an uncommon story. Yeah.

01:52:57.904 --> 01:53:01.324
- And then I looked online and
there's a lot of stories like this.

01:53:01.364 --> 01:53:07.604
And I-- before learning of that, my, I'm
embarrassed to say, knowledge of ECT

01:53:08.404 --> 01:53:11.264
was just "One Flew Over
the Cuckoo's Nest."

01:53:11.604 --> 01:53:14.424
- You know, it has a terrible history-

01:53:14.504 --> 01:53:18.604
in the, in the '40s, '50s and
'60s. There are lots of,-

01:53:18.724 --> 01:53:18.944
- Abuses

01:53:18.954 --> 01:53:23.684
- Uh, the CIA funded Ewen
Cameron up in Canada,

01:53:24.724 --> 01:53:28.444
and he was giving multiple ECTs a day and

01:53:28.484 --> 01:53:32.364
reducing peoples to...
Well, they couldn't walk,

01:53:32.444 --> 01:53:36.304
they couldn't talk, they couldn't feed
themselves, they were incontinent, and then he

01:53:36.344 --> 01:53:40.184
built them back up, or so he claimed, but
in many cases, they were left permanently

01:53:40.284 --> 01:53:45.244
damaged. So I could recite lots
of real horror stories about ECT,

01:53:45.884 --> 01:53:49.624
but it's also, if you're
honest about the thing,

01:53:50.444 --> 01:53:57.274
what you just described, that is patients
who were on the brink of suicide,

01:53:58.104 --> 01:54:01.394
who had long-running
depressions, some of them

01:54:02.584 --> 01:54:10.784
had ECT and they describe it as lifesaving.
And as I say when more controlled

01:54:10.844 --> 01:54:12.554
trials have been done recently,

01:54:13.544 --> 01:54:17.444
there's enough evidence now that
it's hard to say, "This never

01:54:17.524 --> 01:54:21.244
works. This is just one of these
desperate remedies we should consign to

01:54:21.364 --> 01:54:23.774
dark ages." The complicating thing is

01:54:24.804 --> 01:54:29.104
we'd have no clue why it works.
It's a purely empirical treatment.

01:54:29.164 --> 01:54:29.274
- Right. Right.

01:54:29.304 --> 01:54:32.884
- And that itself I think
tends to put people off.

01:54:33.744 --> 01:54:37.674
If you have a curable form of
cancer and the surgeon says,

01:54:37.713 --> 01:54:44.524
"Well, I'm gonna remove it," and she does,
that's that. But because we understand

01:54:45.144 --> 01:54:48.864
even a little bit, even if our
knowledge of human biology is pretty

01:54:49.004 --> 01:54:52.764
primitive, we do understand
a little bit that cancer is

01:54:53.964 --> 01:54:57.774
cells dividing uncontrollably
and taking up and doing

01:54:57.864 --> 01:54:59.804
damage to the body and
eventually killing you,

01:55:00.684 --> 01:55:04.484
and the fact that we can
surgically remove it is a big

01:55:04.604 --> 01:55:08.624
deal. There are lots of disorders.
I have high blood pressure.

01:55:09.604 --> 01:55:16.354
If untreated, my blood pressure is like
a 20-year-old's thanks to treatment.

01:55:17.924 --> 01:55:21.704
It's an ongoing thing. I take the
damn pill every day and it has a

01:55:21.764 --> 01:55:25.534
few minor side effects, but
for me very minor ones. And it

01:55:26.444 --> 01:55:30.144
turns what could have killed me
via a stroke or a heart attack

01:55:30.284 --> 01:55:33.884
into a condition that's
very well controlled. So,

01:55:34.324 --> 01:55:38.264
you know, it-- All of these things,
they're, they're complicated picture-

01:55:38.444 --> 01:55:42.104
It's easy with some of these
things, with lobotomy,

01:55:42.864 --> 01:55:45.724
with insulin comas, deep sleep treatments,

01:55:46.814 --> 01:55:50.474
Henry Cotton's endeavors.
Those you can just say,

01:55:50.534 --> 01:55:54.364
"Well, we'll throw them away."
And for a long time, I think ECT

01:55:54.504 --> 01:55:59.464
would have formed part of that cast
of characters. So when I first

01:56:00.904 --> 01:56:05.344
conceived the idea of writing
Desperate Remedies, the book,

01:56:06.544 --> 01:56:10.324
it was back in 1981 and I was in London

01:56:10.864 --> 01:56:14.804
on a Guggenheim Fellowship at the
Wellcome Institute. And I hadn't done

01:56:14.864 --> 01:56:18.784
research in detail, but I was
aware there were all these things

01:56:18.984 --> 01:56:22.804
lurking about that had happened in
the 1920s and '30s and I thought

01:56:22.884 --> 01:56:26.764
that would be a very
interesting thing to study. And

01:56:26.844 --> 01:56:31.684
luckily I didn't do it right away or
I did it piecemeal over the years

01:56:33.064 --> 01:56:36.374
and I ended up writing a much
more comprehensive look at

01:56:36.916 --> 01:56:40.356
psychiatry really from its origins to now.

01:56:41.136 --> 01:56:44.696
And I couldn't have written that
book back then, and I would've

01:56:44.776 --> 01:56:48.436
missed all the developments
from 1980 onwards, which are

01:56:48.616 --> 01:56:52.296
very, very important to the,
to the overall picture.

01:56:52.336 --> 01:56:54.706
- So forty years later, 2022.

01:56:54.936 --> 01:56:56.576
- Yes. Yes, exactly.

01:56:56.616 --> 01:56:58.056
- Your own Desperate Remedies: Psychiatry's-

01:56:58.076 --> 01:56:58.086
- Yeah. Yeah

01:56:58.086 --> 01:57:00.026
- ... Turmoil in Quest to
Cure Mental Illness.

01:57:00.156 --> 01:57:02.896
- I've written a lot of books
and, and along the way--

01:57:03.776 --> 01:57:07.356
part of the way, I think I've
been productive and kept

01:57:07.456 --> 01:57:11.296
interested. I always had two... at
least two projects on the go at once.

01:57:11.716 --> 01:57:15.596
You can't simultaneously write two
things, but I'd have one and I'd work

01:57:15.636 --> 01:57:19.616
on it, and if I got tired of it, I'd pick up
the other one for a bit and then go back.

01:57:20.196 --> 01:57:24.156
And it also meant when I was
working on one main project, I had

01:57:24.276 --> 01:57:27.416
other things percolating in, in my head,

01:57:28.416 --> 01:57:32.046
and I would, I would come
across things that were

01:57:32.076 --> 01:57:37.636
relevant to them and I'd make a note and
then I'd go back. So when I finally did

01:57:38.816 --> 01:57:42.816
Desperate Remedies, I'd been thinking
about those issues for forty years,

01:57:43.816 --> 01:57:47.516
and that made a big difference,
I think, to the way I approached

01:57:47.556 --> 01:57:51.336
things and to what I thought.
Uh, 'cause you just... You

01:57:51.456 --> 01:57:55.386
either like Thomas says, you say
the same thing over and over

01:57:55.396 --> 01:57:59.296
and over again for forty years or

01:57:59.336 --> 01:58:03.016
you learn new things and you
broaden what you know, and you

01:58:03.176 --> 01:58:07.156
think about things in a different
way because you realize you

01:58:07.216 --> 01:58:10.396
haven't grasped the full complexity
of what you're looking at.

01:58:11.216 --> 01:58:17.316
And so look, what I found with
psychiatry is there are really

01:58:18.176 --> 01:58:21.556
a couple of fundamental things that
have kept me engaged with the field.

01:58:22.736 --> 01:58:26.176
One, it's an arena where
there's tremendous human

01:58:26.256 --> 01:58:29.596
suffering, and it spreads out and it's

01:58:30.296 --> 01:58:33.876
as far as I know -- and I wrote a big
book called Madness in Civilization

01:58:33.936 --> 01:58:37.716
about from the ancient Greeks and
ancient China to now -- in every

01:58:37.756 --> 01:58:41.336
society I've studied they have
to cope with this. It takes

01:58:41.376 --> 01:58:45.256
different forms. It's regarded
in different ways. It's treated

01:58:45.276 --> 01:58:49.016
differently, but that there
are people that deviate

01:58:49.476 --> 01:58:54.676
so far from the norm of what we
regard as culturally appropriate,

01:58:55.776 --> 01:58:59.806
they exist everywhere. So the suffering,

01:59:00.656 --> 01:59:05.576
the difficulty of studying it,
and then the fact that it is such

01:59:06.556 --> 01:59:11.116
a complex and difficult
subject to understand.

01:59:13.236 --> 01:59:16.936
The very fact that we have
such limits to our knowledge

01:59:18.656 --> 01:59:22.436
means there's space there to
examine things in a, in a

01:59:22.536 --> 01:59:26.856
very in what needs to be
a very complicated way.

01:59:27.696 --> 01:59:31.366
And so it's, it's intellectual puzzles

01:59:31.396 --> 01:59:35.896
attracted some very, very smart
people, but there's a long way to go.

01:59:35.936 --> 01:59:39.256
- Yeah, we have glimmers of insights
about how the, mind works but-

01:59:39.265 --> 01:59:43.076
....if you s-, if you look at the span of
human history, we're probably in the very

01:59:43.096 --> 01:59:47.336
early days of understanding this
particular one. I have to, if it's okay-

01:59:47.496 --> 01:59:48.356
- Yes, of course

01:59:48.476 --> 01:59:52.456
- ...so that we've been carrying
multiple threads together. One

01:59:52.516 --> 01:59:56.416
of the threads that I think,
uh, is really exciting to

01:59:56.456 --> 02:00:01.716
me and really important to the history
of psychiatry is the psychotherapy side.

02:00:01.736 --> 02:00:05.466
We have mentioned the psychopharmacology
that will also-- it would

02:00:05.476 --> 02:00:11.636
be nice to discuss when the two clash
and there's a revolution where phar--

02:00:11.696 --> 02:00:19.016
Psychopharmacology kinda wins over
psychoanalysis for a time. But let us start

02:00:19.836 --> 02:00:23.556
at the somewhat beginning,
in the nineteenth century

02:00:23.956 --> 02:00:27.776
when talk therapy starts
coming to life maybe in the

02:00:27.836 --> 02:00:32.176
religious context with Christian Science
and then psychoanalysis context.

02:00:32.316 --> 02:00:36.056
- Yes. So I'd mentioned late nineteenth

02:00:36.116 --> 02:00:40.036
century psychiatry confined as it
was to the mental hospital and

02:00:40.096 --> 02:00:44.056
to people incarcerated in

02:00:44.096 --> 02:00:47.976
those places had become very
biological. But there were people

02:00:48.036 --> 02:00:55.276
experiencing mental troubles of
various kinds, sadness, confusion,

02:00:56.156 --> 02:01:00.116
loss of social relationships
that were troubling them,

02:01:00.576 --> 02:01:04.236
grief, all sorts of things
like that, that didn't

02:01:04.296 --> 02:01:08.276
involve time in a mental
hospital but nonetheless

02:01:08.336 --> 02:01:12.256
involved a good deal of distress
as they continued to do.

02:01:13.236 --> 02:01:17.136
And one of the things that was interesting
about nineteenth century America

02:01:17.156 --> 02:01:21.076
is it spawned a number of new
religions, sort of variants

02:01:21.176 --> 02:01:26.236
of Christianity. So you
had Seventh Day Adventists

02:01:27.256 --> 02:01:34.236
group that still exists who actually
spawned a sanitarium for their depressed

02:01:34.276 --> 02:01:38.176
congregants that was
later taken over by two

02:01:38.556 --> 02:01:41.776
prominent members of the Adventist
Church, the Kellogg family.

02:01:42.556 --> 02:01:48.816
everybody knows them through cereal.
But they ran a huge sanitarium to which

02:01:48.876 --> 02:01:52.176
Abraham Lincoln's widow went, Tarzan went,

02:01:53.016 --> 02:01:56.916
Henry Ford went, lots of very
prominent industrialists

02:01:56.956 --> 02:02:00.436
and politicians and, you
know, it was kind of a

02:02:00.896 --> 02:02:04.856
a farm to go and recover
your mental stability and

02:02:04.896 --> 02:02:10.676
health and it was all bound up
also with beliefs about diet and

02:02:12.336 --> 02:02:16.096
defecation and all sorts
of things. There were the

02:02:16.136 --> 02:02:20.036
Mormons or Church of Jesus Christ
of Latter-day Saints. Many

02:02:20.116 --> 02:02:23.956
Christians don't believe they're really
Christian but they think they are and

02:02:24.016 --> 02:02:27.776
call themselves such. So you have
a number of these and one of them

02:02:28.676 --> 02:02:34.226
was Christian Science, which was the
invention of a woman named Mary Baker Eddy.

02:02:40.452 --> 02:02:44.272
Developed the idea, and there are still
Christian Science churches and Christian

02:02:44.352 --> 02:02:46.882
Science reading rooms all across America,

02:02:47.892 --> 02:02:51.632
that there wasn't such a thing as
disease. That it could be prayed

02:02:51.672 --> 02:02:56.662
away; that it was just a lack of
sufficient faith. So faith healing,

02:02:58.192 --> 02:03:01.932
tended to work, I think,
better if it worked at all for

02:03:01.992 --> 02:03:10.812
psychiatric problems than it did if you
had, say, cancer. But Christian Science

02:03:11.232 --> 02:03:14.432
achieved a considerable
number of followers

02:03:15.432 --> 02:03:22.712
disproportionately women but not
only women. And it began to treat

02:03:23.952 --> 02:03:27.922
many of the people suffering from
what we would think of as the milder

02:03:28.372 --> 02:03:31.232
mental disorders. And it attracted

02:03:32.772 --> 02:03:38.072
both adherents and severe
critics. Mark Twain, for

02:03:38.112 --> 02:03:41.572
example, was thoroughly dismissive of

02:03:42.052 --> 02:03:45.672
Mary Baker Eddy. But it was
very successful for a time.

02:03:46.572 --> 02:03:50.352
There were other religiously
based attempts to

02:03:51.272 --> 02:03:54.872
join in. The most important
of which in New England was

02:03:54.932 --> 02:03:58.232
something called Emmanuel Movement
centered around the Church of the

02:03:58.272 --> 02:04:03.972
Emmanuel in Boston, which was an
attempt actually initially to

02:04:04.032 --> 02:04:10.652
bring medical and religious approaches
to helping the mentally troubled.

02:04:12.192 --> 02:04:16.102
Rather quickly, the doctors
involved decided this

02:04:16.172 --> 02:04:19.952
was veering too much in the
direction of medically-based

02:04:19.972 --> 02:04:26.932
therapeutics. They kind of withdrew from
enterprise. It dispensed-- There were

02:04:28.932 --> 02:04:34.692
talk therapies obviously with a strong
religious component around them.

02:04:36.212 --> 02:04:40.932
This was also at a time when some...

02:04:42.992 --> 02:04:46.132
guess we can call them psychiatrists,
they were often neurologists,

02:04:47.292 --> 02:04:51.672
were beginning to get lots of
patients with these kinds of

02:04:52.952 --> 02:04:59.672
difficult to treat disorders. Neurology had
emerged in America after the Civil War.

02:05:00.372 --> 02:05:03.992
Civil War provided a lot of naturalistic

02:05:04.052 --> 02:05:07.882
experiments on what happens to the
human brain and the human nervous

02:05:07.972 --> 02:05:11.712
system when trauma affect -
I don't mean psychological

02:05:11.732 --> 02:05:15.162
trauma, I mean bullets blowing
holes in your brain. A

02:05:16.332 --> 02:05:20.312
group of new specialists
emerged after the Civil War who

02:05:20.372 --> 02:05:23.912
claimed expertise in the
brain and the nervous system.

02:05:24.012 --> 02:05:30.712
Well, one of the other side parts of that
is insanity, because insanity is also seen

02:05:30.832 --> 02:05:38.412
as a brain disease. So there is a
conflict that erupts in the 1870s and

02:05:38.452 --> 02:05:41.792
'80s between neurologists
and psychiatrists.

02:05:42.752 --> 02:05:46.462
But the neurologist can't, for the most
part, get into the asylum where the most

02:05:46.532 --> 02:05:50.292
seriously ill patients
are. Gradually, what

02:05:50.372 --> 02:05:54.052
comes to their waiting room
along with people like multiple

02:05:54.172 --> 02:05:58.012
sclero-- suffering from things like
multiple sclerosis are people with

02:05:58.112 --> 02:06:04.792
functional mental disorders. There's the
beginning of an outpatient practice,

02:06:05.672 --> 02:06:14.152
um which initially involves
some drugs, the use of

02:06:14.292 --> 02:06:18.172
electricity. Not ECT, but
the use, for example, of

02:06:18.252 --> 02:06:21.572
static electricity, because
it produces obvious

02:06:21.612 --> 02:06:27.912
physiological responses. Electricity is seen
as dominating the workings of the body.

02:06:30.052 --> 02:06:33.992
Sometimes tonics of one sort or
another. Most notoriously of

02:06:34.092 --> 02:06:36.112
all, something called the rest cure.

02:06:37.192 --> 02:06:40.932
Silas Weir Mitchell, one of
the leading lights of American

02:06:40.942 --> 02:06:48.752
neurology, develops complete bedrest,
lots of calories, lots of food, complete

02:06:48.812 --> 02:06:52.552
lack of intellectual stimulation.
Supposedly, this is going

02:06:52.572 --> 02:06:57.932
to cure you. Mainly aimed at women. I
feel like that's another evidence-backed

02:06:58.952 --> 02:07:02.902
technique that works well.
Well, you know, I've

02:07:02.992 --> 02:07:05.412
partaken in this and I
know it has helped me.

02:07:08.152 --> 02:07:11.822
Laying in bed doing nothing, eating
snacks. Virginia Woolf was one of the

02:07:11.892 --> 02:07:15.692
people subjected to this. She claimed
it practically drove her mad or

02:07:15.732 --> 02:07:19.572
made her worse, you know? Just to clarify, we're
talking about laying in bed, eating snacks.

02:07:19.992 --> 02:07:23.932
Eating lots of snacks. Very
high calorie. Okay, Weir

02:07:23.972 --> 02:07:27.072
Mitchell wrote two popular bestsellers,

02:07:27.692 --> 02:07:31.632
self-help books, suppose you'd call
them in the modern genre. One was

02:07:31.672 --> 02:07:37.672
called Wear and Tear. The pace of
modern life, the telegraph, the

02:07:37.792 --> 02:07:41.172
railway was all too much.
Your nervous system was

02:07:41.212 --> 02:07:45.072
overstressed. Either your
batteries ran down, that was

02:07:45.132 --> 02:07:49.612
one analogy, or you overtaxed
your system and went bankrupt.

02:07:50.812 --> 02:07:54.692
Wear and tear, that was
the problem. The solution

02:07:54.812 --> 02:07:57.952
was Fat and Blood. That was
the title of the other book.

02:07:59.272 --> 02:08:02.452
You got scrawny, nouveau,
nervous, twitchy.

02:08:03.272 --> 02:08:07.072
And what you really needed was
to build back up your strength.

02:08:07.252 --> 02:08:10.672
including your nervous strength.
Isn't it fascinating to look at that?

02:08:11.012 --> 02:08:14.462
120 years plus ago? They're talking about

02:08:14.492 --> 02:08:18.572
how anxiety inducing society
is, how much is going on.

02:08:18.612 --> 02:08:23.652
And we in the modern day talk in exact same way about
social media, the internet, all that kind of stuff.

02:08:23.672 --> 02:08:51.052
Yeah, yeah, it is. We look back on the nineteenth century and think of it in idyllic terms of a much slower pace of life, and people, they thought it was stressful in exactly the ways we
do. There was a class of potential patients. Some of them were seeking help in the neurologists and a few psychiatrists who moved out of the asylum. But there were also these mental healing

02:08:51.944 --> 02:08:55.304
groups that were religious,
and they're all around

02:08:55.984 --> 02:09:02.604
at the beginning of the 20th century.
And in 1909, a Viennese gentleman

02:09:02.684 --> 02:09:06.604
and two of his close
colleagues travel across the

02:09:06.624 --> 02:09:10.444
Atlantic on a German
steamer and arrive in New

02:09:10.524 --> 02:09:15.004
York and then transport themselves
up to Worcester, Massachusetts,

02:09:16.084 --> 02:09:20.884
where Clark University is
celebrating its 20th anniversary.

02:09:21.744 --> 02:09:25.564
Clark University was then set
up-- it still exists, but it was

02:09:25.604 --> 02:09:29.194
set up to copy the German
research university. The

02:09:29.924 --> 02:09:36.524
only comparable example at the time was
Johns Hopkins in Baltimore, and Hopkins

02:09:36.564 --> 02:09:40.084
developed the leading
medical school of the time

02:09:40.104 --> 02:09:46.144
borrowing from that German concept of mixing
research and teaching and patient care.

02:09:46.624 --> 02:09:49.164
- I like how you're telling
this in a cinematic way

02:09:50.204 --> 02:09:52.964
the story of Sigmund Freud and Carl Jung--

02:09:53.004 --> 02:09:56.844
...coming to America to give a
lecture. I like, I like how it's like

02:09:56.924 --> 02:10:00.864
the movie opens and there, yeah. Yeah, well, here
he is, you know, like, how do you feel who he

02:10:00.904 --> 02:10:04.884
is? He's fairly obscure. We should say the
reason you're actually telling it that

02:10:04.924 --> 02:10:11.804
way is America has been a really
defining place for psychiatry.

02:10:12.344 --> 02:10:16.034
Yes. And then Freud had his own views
on America and so on. But this--

02:10:16.084 --> 02:10:23.284
this was, in terms of the history, yes,
psychiatric medicine, America as central.

02:10:23.624 --> 02:10:26.744
Right. Many of the in-house
histories of psychiatry

02:10:27.004 --> 02:10:30.924
portray this as Freud conference.

02:10:31.784 --> 02:10:35.564
But it wasn't. Freud was almost an
afterthought of the conference.

02:10:35.884 --> 02:10:39.634
Um, the head of Clark was
a psychologist, and he was

02:10:39.704 --> 02:10:43.304
interested in Freud,
but actually there were

02:10:43.364 --> 02:10:47.064
about thirty speakers at
the Clark conference, two

02:10:47.104 --> 02:10:51.064
Nobel Prize winners in
physics, Franz Boas, who

02:10:51.144 --> 02:10:54.764
was the leading anthropologist
of his generation, and a

02:10:54.904 --> 02:10:58.304
host of other scholars,
including a couple of Freud's

02:10:58.624 --> 02:11:02.504
fierce critics, one of whom,
Stern, was on the same boat as

02:11:02.564 --> 02:11:06.544
him. And they avoided one another
like the plague. But anyway, it's

02:11:06.564 --> 02:11:10.334
important to see that, and
Freud, it's a mark of how

02:11:10.334 --> 02:11:14.204
important German science and German
medical science was in the late

02:11:14.244 --> 02:11:18.253
nineteenth, early twentieth century,
where it was THE most advanced

02:11:19.064 --> 02:11:23.244
in the world, that Freud
delivered his lectures IN GERMAN,

02:11:24.604 --> 02:11:28.504
AND THE AUDIENCE, THAT WASN'T A PROBLEM FOR
THE AUDIENCE 'CAUSE THEY'D ALL LEARNED GERMAN

02:11:28.584 --> 02:11:32.424
so they could read German
literature on medicine. So quite an

02:11:32.464 --> 02:11:35.864
extraordinary thing. Um
William James attends

02:11:36.444 --> 02:11:40.404
one of Freud's lectures and has
a conversation with him. William

02:11:40.484 --> 02:11:44.174
James is one of the people thinking
about the psychology of the

02:11:44.244 --> 02:11:51.464
human mind in interesting ways. He's not
impressed by Freud, and he also has a bad

02:11:51.544 --> 02:11:55.334
heart condition. He dies not
all that long afterwards, but

02:11:55.384 --> 02:11:59.224
he's not impressed by Freud.
A handful of people are,

02:11:59.234 --> 02:12:02.944
some of the neurologists. And
Freud's lectures explicitly

02:12:02.984 --> 02:12:06.494
attack religiously based
psychotherapy, says

02:12:06.504 --> 02:12:10.944
psychotherapy is like a surgical operation
on the mind. And only us doctors

02:12:11.764 --> 02:12:15.704
or only us thoroughly trained
people cause actually he

02:12:15.744 --> 02:12:19.584
doesn't believe that psychoanalysis is
only something that can be practiced

02:12:19.624 --> 02:12:23.614
by medically qualified,
but the lecture is on

02:12:23.704 --> 02:12:27.334
psychoanalysis. Yes, it's
five lectures. They're

02:12:27.384 --> 02:12:31.033
published subsequently, and
he makes a very important

02:12:31.164 --> 02:12:38.004
convert there. Um James Jackson Putnam is
a Boston Brahmin, one of the upper-class

02:12:38.344 --> 02:12:41.784
Bostonians who's extremely
well connected and is

02:12:41.864 --> 02:12:45.544
professor of neurology at
Harvard, and he becomes a

02:12:45.604 --> 02:12:50.764
Freudian at that conference. So
mainstream American psychiatry

02:12:51.964 --> 02:12:55.764
pays very little attention to to
Freud's arrival on the scene.

02:12:56.084 --> 02:13:03.144
And when he becomes a bit more visible,
they tend to be very dismissive. They uh

02:13:04.224 --> 02:13:08.044
They regard the idea of talk therapy for
something they regard as a biological

02:13:08.124 --> 02:13:12.044
condition as a ridiculous idea.
Maybe just uh mention a few

02:13:12.103 --> 02:13:15.974
things. Sigmund Freud, of course, is
widely acknowledged to be the father of

02:13:16.004 --> 02:13:19.804
psychoanalysis. And uh he has
a bunch of ideas, one of which

02:13:19.904 --> 02:13:23.844
is there's this unconscious
mind that is the source of many

02:13:23.884 --> 02:13:27.784
of our uh behaviors. Exactly. And
then psychoanalysis is a way to

02:13:27.824 --> 02:13:31.804
delve deep into that mind,
and the tools he used to do

02:13:31.884 --> 02:13:36.444
that is talking. Yes, Freud was
trained as a neurologist himself

02:13:37.404 --> 02:13:41.284
uh and studied under the most famous late

02:13:41.484 --> 02:13:45.264
Nineteenth-century
neurologist Charcot in Paris

02:13:45.884 --> 02:13:49.704
and translated Charcot into
German and endeared himself to

02:13:49.744 --> 02:13:53.024
Charcot, by doing that,
that's, um, a clever way to

02:13:54.364 --> 02:13:59.124
help your career along. But his
Viennese colleagues didn't think

02:13:59.944 --> 02:14:03.624
much of Charcot, and they didn't
think much of Freud's ideas either.

02:14:03.764 --> 02:14:08.044
So he had a bit of a hard time,
but he did develop a successful

02:14:09.304 --> 02:14:14.344
practice in the sense that patients
came to him. He was accompanied by

02:14:15.304 --> 02:14:21.164
two of his close disciples to the Clark
conference, one of them Carl Jung.

02:14:21.604 --> 02:14:25.584
was then anointed the crown
prince. He was supposed

02:14:25.624 --> 02:14:33.304
to inherit Freud's enterprise until the men
had a very serious falling out, mm-hmm, uh

02:14:33.364 --> 02:14:39.364
just a little bit later in 1913, and
Freud and Jung went their separate ways.

02:14:40.524 --> 02:14:43.444
That's a complicated story.
Freud, as one of his

02:14:43.484 --> 02:14:47.424
close, uh, friends and
collaborators, put it, was a great

02:14:47.484 --> 02:14:51.224
Hater, if you fell out with
Freud, it was bad news.

02:14:51.584 --> 02:14:56.924
hater. If you fell out with Freud, it was bad news. Oh yeah, yeah,
you were excommunicated if it was Adler, but Jung especially.

02:14:57.948 --> 02:15:01.868
- Jung was Swiss and he worked
in the major mental hospital

02:15:01.908 --> 02:15:04.868
in Zurich, and he was attracted to

02:15:04.888 --> 02:15:08.348
psychoanalysis and to Freud.
And for the first few

02:15:08.368 --> 02:15:11.448
years there, there was this
obvious close alliance.

02:15:12.368 --> 02:15:16.348
Anyway, so Jung was the person who
actually persuaded Freud to go

02:15:16.468 --> 02:15:19.968
to America. Freud initially--
Freud had a very low opinion of

02:15:20.028 --> 02:15:23.948
America. He said it should
be renamed Dollaría because

02:15:23.968 --> 02:15:27.608
it was only interested in the dollar.
And American women were far too

02:15:27.688 --> 02:15:31.468
pushy and powerful, and American food
was dreadful and it poisoned him, and

02:15:32.168 --> 02:15:33.048
on and on. Um--

02:15:33.168 --> 02:15:35.508
- So as you said, Freud was a master hater.

02:15:35.788 --> 02:15:39.748
- Yeah, he was. And one of the things
he hated was America, even though

02:15:39.808 --> 02:15:43.508
America brought him some
fairly rich patients. But--

02:15:43.608 --> 02:15:49.278
...the richest patients of all
went to Jung, not to Freud.

02:15:49.728 --> 02:15:53.288
Um, and I think that probably
exacerbated this split.

02:15:53.548 --> 02:15:57.108
- Let's actually focus first before the
conference on the actual ideas of

02:15:57.168 --> 02:16:00.848
psychoanalysis. So Freud was the
originator of psychoanalysis. This is

02:16:00.928 --> 02:16:04.908
19th century. So tell me about
the original case of Anna

02:16:05.008 --> 02:16:07.388
O. and like what is psychoanalysis?

02:16:07.468 --> 02:16:12.548
- Yeah. So Freud's academic career
had been failing in Vienna,

02:16:13.728 --> 02:16:17.408
and he faced a prospect of
either having to emigrate to

02:16:17.448 --> 02:16:20.788
America, which he regarded
with horror in the 1880s,

02:16:21.788 --> 02:16:25.328
or perhaps reviving his
career by going and

02:16:25.408 --> 02:16:30.048
studying under the most famous neurologist
of the era, Jean-Martin Charcot,

02:16:30.848 --> 02:16:34.568
who was then delivering
lectures on hysteria in Paris.

02:16:35.248 --> 02:16:38.528
And so Freud went and
spent some months there

02:16:39.568 --> 02:16:44.358
and came back imbued with
Charcot's ideas about hysteria

02:16:45.668 --> 02:16:53.888
and found a Viennese medical establishment.
He started a consulting career

02:16:54.028 --> 02:16:58.488
and that involved-- He had a
close friend named Josef Breuer.

02:16:59.378 --> 02:17:06.657
And Breuer was more senior, had a very
large practice, and referred patients to

02:17:06.688 --> 02:17:11.928
Freud. And the two of them began
to be interested in this problem

02:17:11.988 --> 02:17:15.488
of hysteria. Breuer had treated

02:17:16.708 --> 02:17:20.668
a woman who's now known as the sort
of foundation patient for-- the

02:17:20.728 --> 02:17:25.888
Ur patient for psychoanalysis,
who was known to us as Anna O.

02:17:26.888 --> 02:17:30.748
She-- We actually know she was
Bertha von Pappenheim, but that was

02:17:31.068 --> 02:17:34.008
hidden for a long time. A
lot of these patients had

02:17:34.808 --> 02:17:41.178
pseudonyms because obviously the cases
exposed a lot about their inner psychology

02:17:42.028 --> 02:17:45.708
and quite reasonably,
even without the modern

02:17:45.828 --> 02:17:50.088
concerns about privacy, there was
a reluctance to identify them.

02:17:50.588 --> 02:17:54.448
So Anna O. had nursed her father. She was

02:17:54.488 --> 02:17:58.308
Breuer's patient, not Freud's.
She'd nursed her father through an

02:17:58.388 --> 02:18:00.468
illness, and he'd ended up dying, and she

02:18:01.348 --> 02:18:05.328
developed various physical
symptoms that troubled

02:18:05.348 --> 02:18:10.388
her a great deal and was also somewhat
depressed. And Breuer and Freud

02:18:11.648 --> 02:18:15.148
dealt with that by developing
a notion that what she was

02:18:15.228 --> 02:18:18.538
suffering from was repressed
memories and trauma.

02:18:19.908 --> 02:18:23.568
And this would become central
to psychoanalysis, the

02:18:23.628 --> 02:18:27.168
idea of half-muttered memories
lurking in your subconscious

02:18:27.908 --> 02:18:31.168
that emerged in distorted forms of

02:18:32.188 --> 02:18:35.638
psychological symptoms and ways
of being in the world that

02:18:35.688 --> 02:18:39.538
were disruptive. Freud, of
course, develops a much more

02:18:39.608 --> 02:18:43.478
elaborate theory of this
in years to come. And

02:18:43.548 --> 02:18:47.158
Breuer collaborate on a book
called Studies in Hysteria,

02:18:48.288 --> 02:18:53.818
and they're a series of case vignettes
in there of patients Freud has treated.

02:18:54.648 --> 02:18:58.588
And he develops the notion
that people have these

02:18:58.598 --> 02:19:02.328
past experiences. Initially, he thinks
they're real, that they're-- For

02:19:02.368 --> 02:19:05.328
example, they have been
sexually assaulted as a child,

02:19:06.168 --> 02:19:10.068
and that they can't face that,
and so they hide it away

02:19:10.128 --> 02:19:13.997
from themselves. But they can't
completely, so it emerges in this tortured

02:19:14.088 --> 02:19:16.747
series of forms. And

02:19:18.028 --> 02:19:23.688
as things develop, the notion of
resistances emerges, that you can't easily

02:19:23.748 --> 02:19:27.398
retrieve these memories,
and indeed, you resist them

02:19:27.468 --> 02:19:31.408
surfacing, and you have a hard
time acknowledging them. And only

02:19:31.508 --> 02:19:37.488
with long and painful work will it
become possible for-- But as you

02:19:38.828 --> 02:19:42.708
bring them into-- from the
unconscious into the conscious world,

02:19:43.248 --> 02:19:47.128
you learn to cope with them in a
different way and, and your personality

02:19:47.168 --> 02:19:48.858
is transformed and you're made better.

02:19:49.308 --> 02:19:53.328
- But crucially, the stuff that
happens in childhood is important.

02:19:53.468 --> 02:20:00.708
- Yes. The early experiences have a dramatic
effect, and I think even some biological

02:20:00.808 --> 02:20:04.798
psychiatrists would agree with
that notion these days. That,

02:20:04.828 --> 02:20:08.568
you know, to the extent we abolish
this distinction between mind and

02:20:08.628 --> 02:20:13.258
body and, and acknowledge that they
are closely tied together, um,

02:20:14.388 --> 02:20:18.148
traumatic experiences in
childhood, various socialization

02:20:18.228 --> 02:20:26.148
experiences, loss and so on deeply mark the
human psyche, I think, and, and can have

02:20:27.428 --> 02:20:30.588
long-term effects that
are, are very powerful.

02:20:31.048 --> 02:20:34.608
- Of course Freud added a bunch
of stuff in the realm of

02:20:34.688 --> 02:20:37.908
psychosexual stages of childhood.

02:20:38.128 --> 02:20:41.868
- Yes. As he begins to develop
his theories, he more and more

02:20:42.008 --> 02:20:48.928
comes to see at first the libido, which gets
sort of transformed into the sex drive-

02:20:48.938 --> 02:20:52.188
... And sexual experiences
and the repression of

02:20:52.268 --> 02:20:56.528
sexuality and the modification
of sexuality as people grow.

02:20:57.528 --> 02:21:01.300
Um, all of that enters the picture.
It's one of the things that

02:21:01.400 --> 02:21:05.380
ends up dividing Freud and
Jung, Jung somewhat because

02:21:05.460 --> 02:21:10.170
Jung downplays that sexual side
of things after the break.

02:21:11.250 --> 02:21:18.360
Um, but so Freud develops a very
complicated theory of mind initially

02:21:19.100 --> 02:21:22.400
reflecting his training as a neurologist.

02:21:23.300 --> 02:21:26.650
He tries to write an essay which
survives called Project for a

02:21:26.740 --> 02:21:30.480
Scientific Psychology, and the
scientific psychology is gonna

02:21:30.560 --> 02:21:33.780
tie psychology back into neurology.

02:21:34.740 --> 02:21:37.970
But he abandons that, and
once he's abandoned that,

02:21:38.600 --> 02:21:42.400
he goes on to develop increased...
an increasingly elaborate theory. He

02:21:42.500 --> 02:21:46.460
writes The Interpretation of
Dreams, for example, because he

02:21:46.480 --> 02:21:53.820
begins to regard dreams as an arena where
these hidden memories, these suppressed-

02:21:53.860 --> 02:22:00.760
... things reemerge in disguised
form in your dream life. And so

02:22:00.980 --> 02:22:06.000
that book marks an important
step forward. He becomes

02:22:06.100 --> 02:22:11.290
interested in things like slips of the
tongue, so-called Freudian mistakes.

02:22:11.380 --> 02:22:14.980
As seeing those as revealing
what really is hidden from you.

02:22:15.700 --> 02:22:20.980
And so there's this very elaborate
dissection of things that relies upon

02:22:21.900 --> 02:22:25.920
long extended talk therapy.

02:22:26.300 --> 02:22:30.280
- Of course underlying it he's building a

02:22:30.420 --> 02:22:34.240
model of how this whole
mind thing works. There's-

02:22:34.250 --> 02:22:37.950
... three interacting parts of the
personality. First the id, which is the

02:22:37.980 --> 02:22:41.680
primitive entirely unconscious
driven by pleasure principle seeking

02:22:41.720 --> 02:22:45.100
immediate gratification or basic
drives such as sex and aggression.

02:22:45.620 --> 02:22:49.360
There's the ego, which
is the rational mediator

02:22:49.520 --> 02:22:53.140
operating under reality principle
balancing the id's demands with

02:22:53.220 --> 02:22:56.680
external reality. And
finally, the super ego,

02:22:57.180 --> 02:23:01.000
which is the internalized moral
standards and ideals producing

02:23:01.040 --> 02:23:06.660
guilt or pride and striving for
perfection. So that's the structure.

02:23:06.699 --> 02:23:10.570
- Yeah. That is broadly speaking the
structure that Freud ends up with,

02:23:11.280 --> 02:23:15.020
and the conflicts between
these entities and

02:23:15.060 --> 02:23:19.940
the ways they interact are
obviously the thing that

02:23:21.400 --> 02:23:25.300
creates your mental universe, your
way of being in the world and

02:23:25.580 --> 02:23:29.700
in many cases creates pathology, which

02:23:30.640 --> 02:23:34.920
through the process of
psychoanalysis you can transform

02:23:36.080 --> 02:23:39.900
grotesque unhappiness into ordinary
unhappiness or something like that.

02:23:40.500 --> 02:23:41.120
You know? So-

02:23:41.170 --> 02:23:41.520
- Yeah, sure

02:23:41.680 --> 02:23:45.000
- Yes, you get that. And initially,

02:23:45.580 --> 02:23:51.140
Jung gets one of the heirs to the International
Harvester fortune as a patient, and,

02:23:52.000 --> 02:23:57.160
um, the McCormick family are
as rich as the Rockefellers

02:23:57.240 --> 02:24:02.410
and the Carnegies and the, you know,
the Vanderbilts of that world.

02:24:02.940 --> 02:24:06.870
This guy is a US senator
who ends up committing

02:24:06.980 --> 02:24:10.160
suicide in nineteen twenty-five
after a lot of treatment from,

02:24:11.140 --> 02:24:15.020
from Jung when he fails to win
reelection to the US Senate. He

02:24:15.040 --> 02:24:18.640
kills himself in Washington in
the interregnum. But he also gets

02:24:19.020 --> 02:24:22.470
another one of that brood. One
of the Rockefeller daughters,

02:24:22.800 --> 02:24:26.080
Edith Rockefeller marries a McCormick.

02:24:26.200 --> 02:24:30.140
And she has a lot of psychological
issues, and she tries

02:24:30.180 --> 02:24:32.300
to get Jung to move to Chicago

02:24:33.140 --> 02:24:37.100
promising him she'll set him up
in a mansion with lots of her

02:24:37.220 --> 02:24:40.840
friends as his patients,
and Jung has no interest.

02:24:41.820 --> 02:24:46.060
So then she finally persuades him to come

02:24:47.300 --> 02:24:50.860
to New York and accompany
her on the liner across to

02:24:50.920 --> 02:24:54.720
Zurich to be treated.
She's agoraphobic, and

02:24:54.820 --> 02:24:58.600
so she has a hard time being
out, out of her little cocoon.

02:24:59.380 --> 02:25:03.200
So she's treated there as an
example of how peculiar she was. I

02:25:03.360 --> 02:25:09.060
mentioned the agoraphobia. So she occupies
a huge array of suites with all her

02:25:09.160 --> 02:25:13.080
servants and so on in
Zurich. And she takes a

02:25:13.140 --> 02:25:17.040
train journey, it's stopping
train, and her chauffeur

02:25:17.079 --> 02:25:20.960
follows the train in the Rolls Royce in
case she has to jump out of the train at

02:25:20.980 --> 02:25:24.760
a station 'cause she can't bear
it anymore. And she becomes a

02:25:24.800 --> 02:25:28.640
Jungian analyst, but she's
very wealthy and she writes

02:25:28.760 --> 02:25:32.460
big checks to Jung which is the
important thing. And then Jung

02:25:32.500 --> 02:25:35.780
attracts Paul Mellon and his wife Mary.

02:25:36.560 --> 02:25:40.020
And so Jung's works are
published by Princeton

02:25:40.120 --> 02:25:43.780
University Press with a subvention
from that Mellon Foundation.

02:25:44.500 --> 02:25:48.200
So it's a long-running
thing. Freud doesn't get

02:25:48.260 --> 02:25:51.360
patients quite that rich. He
gets some rich Americans.

02:25:51.820 --> 02:25:55.620
- We should say that Freud became--
Freud's ideas became quite

02:25:55.680 --> 02:25:59.900
popular among the intellectual and artist
class in the nineteen twenties in America.

02:26:00.070 --> 02:26:03.390
- And another thing
happened. World War I saw

02:26:04.540 --> 02:26:08.400
the breakdown of many soldiers
from something that came

02:26:08.500 --> 02:26:14.600
to be labeled as shell shock. The label
shell shock intimates its, the first

02:26:14.660 --> 02:26:18.600
theories about its origin, which
was that shells bursting near

02:26:18.680 --> 02:26:22.380
you and bombs bursting near you
shook up your body and your

02:26:22.400 --> 02:26:26.120
brain and created some
physical damage that then

02:26:26.180 --> 02:26:30.670
accounted for the symptoms of shell
shock: your mutism, your blindism, your

02:26:31.540 --> 02:26:34.960
constant shaking, your
nightmares, all of that.

02:26:35.100 --> 02:26:38.820
But it became increasingly apparent that

02:26:38.830 --> 02:26:43.900
shell shock was actually a
psychological thing. The trauma of war

02:26:44.720 --> 02:26:49.200
in significant number of cases
brought about mental breakdowns.

02:26:50.180 --> 02:26:54.140
And in thinking about that,
Freud's ideas about the

02:26:54.180 --> 02:26:58.060
unconscious mind and
trauma and its connection

02:26:58.140 --> 02:27:02.020
to symptomatology acquired
a new significance

02:27:02.040 --> 02:27:05.560
for a lot of people in that
period. And then after the war,

02:27:05.620 --> 02:27:13.564
you're absolutely right among a certain
smart section of society, those

02:27:13.664 --> 02:27:16.164
ideas developed a considerable purchase.

02:27:16.764 --> 02:27:20.364
Psychiatrists who were
mostly stuck in the asylums

02:27:21.164 --> 02:27:24.924
dealing with psychotic patients
wanted nothing to do with these

02:27:25.004 --> 02:27:28.164
ideas and this talk
therapy. The mainstream

02:27:29.164 --> 02:27:32.984
psychotherapy which was
mostly in a clinical setting

02:27:33.164 --> 02:27:39.544
Yeah, Freud's ideas were not popular. No.
The only partial exception to that was

02:27:40.324 --> 02:27:44.264
The mental hospital in
Washington DC--the only federal

02:27:44.364 --> 02:27:48.264
mental hospital, St. Elizabeths.
William Alanson White,

02:27:48.364 --> 02:27:52.944
who was then the superintendent, was
somewhat sympathetic to Freud's ideas and

02:27:54.084 --> 02:27:58.064
tried a bit, but you're
talking about thousands

02:27:58.104 --> 02:28:01.944
of patients and the idea of talk
therapy for them is obviously out of

02:28:02.004 --> 02:28:11.124
reach, plus they are much more severely
disturbed, so it does happen. Novelists,

02:28:12.024 --> 02:28:15.044
painters, artists, playwrights,

02:28:16.164 --> 02:28:20.944
uh and the audience for
those elite forms of culture

02:28:21.804 --> 02:28:28.964
do tend to embrace Freudian ideas. Sex
sells in the theater and on the movies. Um,

02:28:29.124 --> 02:28:35.264
obviously. But beyond that, part of Freud's
appeal to everybody, it was something he

02:28:35.384 --> 02:28:38.964
lamented at one point. He said, "You know,

02:28:39.664 --> 02:28:44.644
my case histories read like short
stories. Like works of fiction.

02:28:46.104 --> 02:28:50.524
And in that sense, people think they
lack the stamp of serious science.

02:28:51.384 --> 02:28:55.044
But I'm driven to that because that's
how I unpack what's going on."

02:28:55.504 --> 02:28:59.444
Well, psychoanalysis is telling
stories. It's getting people to recover

02:28:59.604 --> 02:29:02.744
stories, recover memories,
to rebuild, right?

02:29:04.064 --> 02:29:11.084
Um, and its elements of psychological
conflict and hidden motives and so forth

02:29:12.224 --> 02:29:15.844
naturally very appealing to people writing

02:29:15.904 --> 02:29:21.884
novels. People writing plays.
People doing screenplays." And

02:29:22.704 --> 02:29:25.844
for artists of the modern sort

02:29:26.664 --> 02:29:34.004
the idea that we can extract things from
the subconscious Surrealism and the

02:29:34.044 --> 02:29:37.844
like, for example, are clearly
very heavily influenced by

02:29:37.884 --> 02:29:41.503
Freudian ideas But it's
important to know circa

02:29:41.574 --> 02:29:46.924
1930 there are probably three hundred
psychoanalysts in North America.

02:29:48.844 --> 02:29:53.724
if they each Classical psychoanalysis
involving five hours a week

02:29:55.904 --> 02:29:59.744
They can't treat that many
patients A few thousand

02:30:00.284 --> 02:30:03.774
when there are hundreds of thousands
in the hospitals Right Mm-hmm So

02:30:04.144 --> 02:30:11.404
what changes that Hitler
starts killing off Jews

02:30:12.344 --> 02:30:15.824
and killing off psychoanalysts
The ones who can

02:30:15.864 --> 02:30:20.584
escape some of them go to
England and some come to America

02:30:21.464 --> 02:30:25.264
the most famous escapee is
Freud himself and his daughter

02:30:25.344 --> 02:30:28.964
Anna Who Princess Bonaparte who's one of

02:30:28.984 --> 02:30:32.214
Freud's great supporters
bribes the Nazis to get him

02:30:32.304 --> 02:30:36.264
out And he travels by train
to London Very sick with

02:30:36.844 --> 02:30:39.564
cancer of the jaw As he has
been since the mid-'20s

02:30:40.444 --> 02:30:44.384
So he sets up shop in London
and Anna succeeds him really

02:30:44.444 --> 02:30:48.184
as one of the central figures in
British psychoanalysis But other

02:30:48.264 --> 02:30:53.924
analysts come here to America So
the numbers of analysts probably by

02:30:54.824 --> 02:30:58.784
1940 America's not yet in
the war have a bit more than

02:30:58.864 --> 02:31:02.464
doubled And more
importantly psychoanalytic

02:31:02.504 --> 02:31:06.204
training has become much more
organized here The institutes

02:31:06.544 --> 02:31:10.384
outside the universities
control psych... And

02:31:10.504 --> 02:31:14.274
universities are not yet the knowledge
factories they become after the

02:31:14.364 --> 02:31:17.564
war mm-hmm And that's
a crucial mistake that

02:31:17.604 --> 02:31:22.384
psychoanalysis makes It's great because
it controls its training completely

02:31:23.264 --> 02:31:27.003
The bad thing is when the center
of gravity moves to the university

02:31:27.964 --> 02:31:34.484
it's not there and either resists being
incorporated or the university resists

02:31:34.544 --> 02:31:38.524
incorporating it So I think that's
a structural weakness for that

02:31:38.564 --> 02:31:47.784
But what really transforms
things is World War II Five

02:31:47.844 --> 02:31:55.754
fifty minute hours famously They last
fifty minutes 'cause then there's

02:31:55.784 --> 02:32:02.403
ten ten minutes for four analysts to recover
before the next patient arrives Yes You

02:32:03.104 --> 02:32:11.204
are free associating on the couch

02:32:12.204 --> 02:32:16.864
and gradually being coaxed
to see as material emerges

02:32:17.684 --> 02:32:19.184
what you're hiding from yourself

02:32:20.044 --> 02:32:23.944
I mean initially they tried This is in
their early early days That they tried

02:32:23.953 --> 02:32:27.924
hypnosis yes But they've You know this
is where Freud took the big leap and

02:32:27.984 --> 02:32:31.964
expanded free association
Right So I mentioned

02:32:32.064 --> 02:32:36.854
Freud training under Charcot Charcot
hypnotized his hysterical patients

02:32:37.984 --> 02:32:41.904
We now know a lot of that was
fakery Not I don't think he

02:32:41.964 --> 02:32:45.684
was conscious of the fakery
but the patients were on

02:32:45.724 --> 02:32:49.534
display over and over again and they
worked their routines up very well and

02:32:49.584 --> 02:32:57.164
they deceived him and they deceived
the audience But Freud came back from

02:32:57.224 --> 02:33:02.764
Paris bringing with him the idea
that hypnosis was the way forward

02:33:04.044 --> 02:33:07.684
But after the break with
Breuer Breuer had a general

02:33:07.784 --> 02:33:11.664
medical practice and lost interest in
hysteria and didn't want anything to do with

02:33:11.704 --> 02:33:15.184
the revisions of Freud's work on hysteria

02:33:16.224 --> 02:33:22.444
After that happened Freud who was a very
clumsy hypnotist by his own account

02:33:24.444 --> 02:33:28.284
began to develop this
alternative of free association

02:33:28.324 --> 02:33:32.304
and getting people to speak
whatever came into their head

02:33:32.364 --> 02:33:36.124
without a censor. That was
the important thing, that the

02:33:36.164 --> 02:33:39.664
half-murdered memories were being
hidden from you and your-- uh,

02:33:40.464 --> 02:33:43.984
the constellation of psychological
forces in your unconscious was

02:33:43.994 --> 02:33:49.704
squashing them down but they were
reemerging as other kinds of symptoms

02:33:49.764 --> 02:33:53.544
- So how well does psychoanalysis work? How

02:33:53.604 --> 02:33:58.344
much do we understand? We've talked about
all these approaches that didn't work.

02:33:59.124 --> 02:34:00.524
How well did it work at that time?

02:34:01.024 --> 02:34:04.884
- You know, one of the things after
World War II when psychoanalysis

02:34:04.964 --> 02:34:08.954
was making great strides in America
and the Rockefeller Foundation was

02:34:09.044 --> 02:34:12.614
still heavily involved in
promoting psychiatry and

02:34:12.664 --> 02:34:16.164
developing what it hoped
would be new tools in it

02:34:16.984 --> 02:34:20.604
The then heads of the institute-- Of the

02:34:20.884 --> 02:34:24.344
funding program, Alan
Gregg and Robert Morison,

02:34:24.684 --> 02:34:28.284
especially Morrison, kept
pressing the analysts:

02:34:28.294 --> 02:34:31.144
"Provide us proof that what you do works."

02:34:31.964 --> 02:34:35.744
And the analyst kept resisting and
resisting and resisting, saying

02:34:35.764 --> 02:34:39.714
"It's much more complicated than
that. There's no easy measure because

02:34:39.764 --> 02:34:44.284
what we're doing is reconstructing entire
personalities, entire ways of being."

02:34:45.784 --> 02:34:49.104
Sense People sense themselves,
their ways of being in the world.

02:34:49.144 --> 02:34:52.254
"And we don't have easy
ways to measure that."

02:34:52.504 --> 02:34:56.304
- To contrast that with the
80% promises over and

02:34:56.384 --> 02:34:57.504
over and over and over. Yes, yes, yes.

02:34:57.704 --> 02:35:01.704
- And it's a long and complicated pro...
That was very important. Because

02:35:04.544 --> 02:35:10.484
another kind of psychotherapeutics is
emerging in, in the aftermath, during

02:35:10.504 --> 02:35:14.444
and in the aftermath of the
war as a rival for both

02:35:15.144 --> 02:35:19.124
psychiatry and for
psychoanalysis. Initially

02:35:19.224 --> 02:35:23.064
not terribly successful,
but as time goes on,

02:35:23.144 --> 02:35:29.564
a more and more important part of the
story. So to focus on the war for a minute,

02:35:29.684 --> 02:35:33.364
Um, America's psychiatrists
went to the military

02:35:33.444 --> 02:35:37.364
brass and to the politicians
before America entered the

02:35:37.404 --> 02:35:41.224
war, and it had some advance warning
because America didn't enter till

02:35:41.324 --> 02:35:45.144
Pearl Harbor, but the European war
had been going on. And they said,

02:35:45.224 --> 02:35:48.764
"Look, if we have to fight, we're gonna
have the same problem we had in the

02:35:48.824 --> 02:35:52.684
First World War. We're gonna
train these soldiers, gonna equip

02:35:52.694 --> 02:35:56.264
them, we're gonna put them
in the battlefield and the

02:35:56.284 --> 02:36:00.184
psychologically vulnerable among
them are gonna break down."

02:36:00.744 --> 02:36:08.684
"They'll be shell shock all over again,
so we should screen all recruits to make

02:36:08.724 --> 02:36:12.884
sure they're not psychologically
weak and susceptible."

02:36:14.824 --> 02:36:18.724
And they screen out 1 3/4 million people

02:36:20.104 --> 02:36:24.024
and say, "These men are not
fit. So now we won't have the

02:36:24.064 --> 02:36:27.004
problems we had in World War I." Except

02:36:28.154 --> 02:36:35.064
it quickly turns out that those
problems reemerge. Industrial warfare

02:36:37.084 --> 02:36:41.713
exposes people to seeing
things and doing things

02:36:42.484 --> 02:36:45.924
that all of us in normal
life would recoil from.

02:36:47.204 --> 02:36:51.784
And when we're forced to do
them as soldiers, many of us--

02:36:52.844 --> 02:36:58.544
Can't speak from experience, but from talking
to people who've gone through all this--

02:36:59.644 --> 02:37:03.564
Uh, many of them find the experiences and

02:37:03.604 --> 02:37:07.584
the memories those create intolerable.
And they emerge in symptoms

02:37:07.624 --> 02:37:08.424
and they break down.

02:37:09.464 --> 02:37:13.334
So it became a huge problem for the
American Army, as it did for all

02:37:13.364 --> 02:37:16.794
the armies. Actually, the
Nazis just shot people.

02:37:18.664 --> 02:37:22.344
Broke down, bang, you're dead.
We're not dealing with you.

02:37:23.344 --> 02:37:30.744
But, um, obviously the Allies, that was
not the response. Um, and they quickly had

02:37:30.864 --> 02:37:36.964
to try to treat these troops, maybe
get them back into the fighting

02:37:38.664 --> 02:37:42.424
lines, maybe get them
into support positions.

02:37:42.484 --> 02:37:46.224
Something to cope with the
problem, which was both, um,

02:37:47.004 --> 02:37:50.524
created extraordinary morale
problems among the troops

02:37:50.804 --> 02:37:54.764
and it invited malingering,
claiming you had these

02:37:54.804 --> 02:38:00.124
conditions when you didn't. It obviously
cost a lot of the fighting force.

02:38:00.964 --> 02:38:04.754
In combat conditions, as much
as 25% of the soldiers broke

02:38:04.844 --> 02:38:07.064
down. So it was a big, big problem.

02:38:09.284 --> 02:38:15.104
Before the war, there were about 2,000
psychiatrists in North America, in the US.

02:38:16.924 --> 02:38:20.684
At the end of the war, there were more
than 2,000 psychiatrists in the US

02:38:20.764 --> 02:38:25.904
military. Okay, so you had to
train people in a hurry, um,

02:38:26.964 --> 02:38:30.524
and even still, it was
hard to get enough people

02:38:30.624 --> 02:38:34.604
trained. And it turned
out that the head of the

02:38:34.684 --> 02:38:38.664
US military, not the first, but
the first one died, the second

02:38:38.744 --> 02:38:42.404
one uh was a man named
William Menninger from the

02:38:42.464 --> 02:38:46.064
Menninger Clinic in Kansas,
where he and his brother

02:38:46.624 --> 02:38:49.824
ran a psychoanalytic treatment facility.

02:38:51.024 --> 02:38:54.824
So Bill Menninger became the
head of Army psychiatry.

02:38:55.564 --> 02:38:59.434
He concluded that the best treatment
for these soldiers breaking

02:38:59.504 --> 02:39:03.264
down was psychotherapeutic in
nature. The origin of their

02:39:03.304 --> 02:39:09.204
condition was trauma, uh, and so
in a watered-down way, Freud's

02:39:09.304 --> 02:39:12.944
ideas, those were the ones
that the people that recruited

02:39:13.004 --> 02:39:19.464
quickly and retrained as psychiatrists
absorbed. So they formed after the war

02:39:20.304 --> 02:39:22.644
a fairly coherent group of people,

02:39:23.624 --> 02:39:27.604
separate from the psychiatrists in
the state hospitals who were still

02:39:27.644 --> 02:39:29.354
very biologically oriented.

02:39:31.984 --> 02:39:36.424
Another group emerged during the war
precisely because you couldn't produce enough

02:39:37.504 --> 02:39:43.084
psychotherapists who were MDs quickly
enough. Psychologists were drafted in

02:39:43.904 --> 02:39:49.484
and asked to treat. So what the
psychologists discovered was,

02:39:49.824 --> 02:39:54.354
"Hey, we could do this stuff too, and
we really like it." "It's interesting-"

02:39:54.414 --> 02:39:58.164
"...it's challenging.
We should do that." So

02:39:58.304 --> 02:40:00.424
after the war, similar kind of problem.

02:40:01.644 --> 02:40:05.634
Mental illness is a massive problem. The
returning soldiers, it's a big problem,

02:40:05.704 --> 02:40:09.804
but it's also a problem
in the community. Um,

02:40:10.784 --> 02:40:14.704
these guys don't... The psychiatrists
don't go and work in the

02:40:14.744 --> 02:40:20.684
mental hospital. They start
outpatient treatment. And by

02:40:20.704 --> 02:40:26.864
1958, about 80% of
psychiatrists are working in

02:40:26.924 --> 02:40:29.784
outpatient rather than
in the hospitals, right?

02:40:30.584 --> 02:40:34.244
So the psychologists organize
themselves and federal

02:40:34.304 --> 02:40:37.664
money -- Again, "We need you.
We need you. We need more of

02:40:37.744 --> 02:40:41.124
you." So the VA, the Veterans

02:40:41.144 --> 02:40:44.924
Administration, and then once
the National Institute of

02:40:45.004 --> 02:40:48.864
Mental Health is established, they
start providing a lot of money

02:40:48.904 --> 02:40:52.844
for training, and they train not
just psychiatrists, they train

02:40:52.964 --> 02:40:56.864
psychologists. So the question is:
How are the psychologists gonna set

02:40:56.964 --> 02:40:58.294
up clinical training?

02:40:59.124 --> 02:41:03.064
So what the psychologists come
up with, the clinically oriented

02:41:03.144 --> 02:41:11.104
ones, is a training program where would-be
clinical psychologists spend two years

02:41:12.684 --> 02:41:16.504
learning basic scientific psychology

02:41:16.604 --> 02:41:20.584
and research methods, and then
they have two or three years

02:41:20.644 --> 02:41:26.854
of clinical work and an experience.
And that's very clever because

02:41:27.844 --> 02:41:31.824
it means when they start looking
for grants, they know how to

02:41:31.864 --> 02:41:35.724
do that, and the psychoanalysts don't
have a clue how to apply for federal

02:41:35.764 --> 02:41:37.724
grant money, and they
don't get any almost.

02:41:38.704 --> 02:41:42.024
Beyond that, these people begin to work on

02:41:42.303 --> 02:41:46.224
treating symptoms. The psychoanalysts
think to treat a symptom is to

02:41:46.244 --> 02:41:49.624
play Whac-A-Mole. If you don't
deal with the underlying

02:41:49.744 --> 02:41:56.764
problems of the personality, their
interaction between your id ego and superego,

02:41:58.384 --> 02:42:01.464
you suppress that symptom, and
another one will pop up over here.

02:42:04.084 --> 02:42:08.044
The clinical psychologists go, "We're
gonna treat the symptoms of what

02:42:08.064 --> 02:42:12.984
are troubling people. We're gonna treat
the symptoms, and we're gonna develop

02:42:12.994 --> 02:42:19.824
techniques that allow people
to cope with those symptoms

02:42:19.904 --> 02:42:25.464
and eliminate them." And how are we
gonna do that? We're going to do it

02:42:26.004 --> 02:42:31.864
primarily through what initially is
cognitive behavioral therapy and

02:42:31.964 --> 02:42:37.064
later interpersonal therapy and some other
variants emerge. But basically, what

02:42:37.904 --> 02:42:44.384
they try to do with those techniques is
develop things that work relatively quickly,

02:42:45.264 --> 02:42:51.184
work specifically on one set of
problems or small subset of problems,

02:42:52.024 --> 02:42:55.884
and try to get those under
control. And how does

02:42:55.944 --> 02:42:59.124
it work? You've developed
really bad habits.

02:43:00.444 --> 02:43:04.404
You... The way you react
to being socially snubbed

02:43:04.504 --> 02:43:10.354
or being ignored or the way you
deal with your coworkers or the

02:43:11.754 --> 02:43:14.744
trouble you're having with
your disruptive child

02:43:16.464 --> 02:43:19.724
will help you see the patterns
that you've fallen into

02:43:20.444 --> 02:43:24.304
and will give you exercises
that will work to give

02:43:24.344 --> 02:43:28.284
you a better way of coping with those
things that don't produce these

02:43:28.324 --> 02:43:32.064
symptoms. I'm oversimplifying
obviously, but basically that's the

02:43:32.104 --> 02:43:33.544
fundamental thrust of this.

02:43:33.944 --> 02:43:38.614
- And one of the other contrasting things
about CBT, Cognitive Behavioral Therapy,

02:43:39.464 --> 02:43:45.924
is that it doesn't have necessarily that
heavy requirement of five hours a week.

02:43:46.064 --> 02:43:51.404
- Exactly. So these therapies could be
packaged. They could be reproduced.

02:43:51.464 --> 02:43:54.364
They could be tested to
see whether they worked,

02:43:55.384 --> 02:43:59.324
so they had an evidentiary foundation. The
psychoanalyst would say, "It's gonna take

02:43:59.384 --> 02:44:03.344
us years, and it's hard to measure."
These guys went, "Well, we'll

02:44:03.424 --> 02:44:07.214
measure what happens when
we treat people this way?"

02:44:07.464 --> 02:44:08.263
- Psychologists.

02:44:08.404 --> 02:44:12.384
- Uh, yeah. The symptoms are what matter,
not the underlie-- you know, the

02:44:12.464 --> 02:44:17.154
underlying more complex thing that
psychoanalysts claimed to be treating.

02:44:18.204 --> 02:44:24.364
And so it's shorter. It's reproducible

02:44:25.544 --> 02:44:29.374
whereas psychoanalytic-- You know, every
patient is a new patient, a new-- with a

02:44:29.444 --> 02:44:35.784
new set of complicated things going
on. Here we are. We're gonna do this.

02:44:35.984 --> 02:44:40.584
- And the key figures here are Albert
Bandura, Albert Ellis, Aaron Beck.

02:44:40.764 --> 02:44:44.884
- Yes. Um, Bandura and
some other psychologists

02:44:45.704 --> 02:44:51.604
launching this movement, and then
later on Aaron Beck, who went by

02:44:51.664 --> 02:44:58.594
Tim Beck who just recently died at... I
think he was ninety-nine. Extraordinary.

02:44:58.614 --> 02:44:59.734
- No, no, I think he crossed 100.

02:44:59.824 --> 02:45:01.944
- Maybe 100. Yeah. Maybe 100.

02:45:02.124 --> 02:45:06.044
- There's videos of him talking at that
age and he's still, he's still killing

02:45:06.104 --> 02:45:06.944
it, so wise.

02:45:07.144 --> 02:45:10.444
- Aaron Beck is a remarkable
figure because he was

02:45:10.544 --> 02:45:17.264
also one of the first in the 1960s
to look at psychiatric diagnosis

02:45:18.164 --> 02:45:22.024
and show how unreliable it
was, how-- how difficult

02:45:22.104 --> 02:45:25.024
it was to get psychiatrists
to agree on what was wrong.

02:45:25.224 --> 02:45:26.864
- So he was a psychoanalyst, right?

02:45:26.904 --> 02:45:30.184
- He had been trained as
a psychoanalyst, became

02:45:30.244 --> 02:45:33.904
disillusioned with it, and so there
were two strands, two important

02:45:34.024 --> 02:45:37.424
strands that stemmed from
his work. First, this...

02:45:38.124 --> 02:45:41.324
He was one of the ones
within the community itself

02:45:41.924 --> 02:45:45.764
who said to his fellow
psychiatrists, "You know, we're

02:45:45.804 --> 02:45:51.124
not very good at this diagnosis business--"
"... and maybe we need to work on that."

02:45:51.404 --> 02:45:55.504
Okay? And that was very important
for the movement towards DSM-III.

02:45:57.164 --> 02:45:58.944
The other side of the coin was

02:45:59.924 --> 02:46:03.974
he broke with the idea that treating
symptoms was a waste of time,

02:46:05.284 --> 02:46:09.074
and joined with the
psychologists who already

02:46:09.084 --> 02:46:15.444
embarked on that journey to say, "How
can we develop techniques to overcome

02:46:15.454 --> 02:46:21.804
these self-destructive ways in which
people are responding? How can we help

02:46:22.844 --> 02:46:27.884
with their ability to
interact?" So interpersonal

02:46:27.964 --> 02:46:31.804
therapy is, you know, much more
concerned with the way in which

02:46:32.224 --> 02:46:36.044
if your relations with other
people break down, that

02:46:36.084 --> 02:46:39.444
damages your emotional stability. If you

02:46:39.484 --> 02:46:42.974
repair those relationships,
that helps your emotional

02:46:43.044 --> 02:46:52.524
stability come back. So all of this
develops, and Americans are unusual. The--

02:46:52.584 --> 02:46:56.324
Most of the rest of the world has
what we call socialized medicine.

02:46:57.604 --> 02:47:01.124
Um, America doesn't have that, so
we're used to paying for medical care.

02:47:01.884 --> 02:47:08.684
And so Americans were more willing, I
think, to pay for clinical psychologists,

02:47:09.484 --> 02:47:14.704
and they attacked some of the
problems of everyday living, the more

02:47:15.564 --> 02:47:20.074
uh, I was gonna say minor, but these
are often quite distressing things. But

02:47:20.924 --> 02:47:24.804
those were-- those were the things they
were most successful at doing. If I

02:47:24.864 --> 02:47:28.724
look at the literature on
CBT, for example, and its

02:47:28.824 --> 02:47:32.464
uses in cases of schizophrenia,
the evidence is pretty

02:47:32.584 --> 02:47:40.204
poor that it works. And even the evidence
for the milder conditions is a bit more

02:47:40.244 --> 02:47:43.944
ambiguous than we like...
That they like to pretend.

02:47:44.594 --> 02:47:48.064
The Cochrane Reviews, which
are a systematic attempt to

02:47:48.144 --> 02:47:51.344
assess how strong the
evidence for particular

02:47:52.124 --> 02:47:55.984
approaches to disease
of all sorts are, say

02:47:56.044 --> 02:47:59.853
that at best, the evidence
for CBT is of low and

02:47:59.984 --> 02:48:03.584
medium confidence, you
know? But I think, again,

02:48:04.324 --> 02:48:07.454
there are patients for
whom this helps, and

02:48:07.504 --> 02:48:14.544
indeed, for the milder forms of
depression, I think CBT works better

02:48:15.564 --> 02:48:19.324
than the drugs without some of
the side effects that the, the

02:48:19.404 --> 02:48:22.884
drugs have. And indeed, NICE, which is the

02:48:23.024 --> 02:48:26.304
British organization that
passes on what treatments

02:48:26.504 --> 02:48:30.284
the National Health Service
will support and which

02:48:30.364 --> 02:48:36.344
ones are, are sufficiently evidence-based
has come to that same conclusion.

02:48:36.484 --> 02:48:39.524
- So I think one thing to just say about
cognitive behavioral therapy, I mean, it's--

02:48:40.764 --> 02:48:50.254
It has some elements of kind of surface
level intuitive kind of things like--

02:48:50.504 --> 02:48:54.244
... being aware of and adjusting
your thought patterns, being

02:48:54.304 --> 02:48:57.394
aware of triggers that get
you-- down a negative spiral--

02:48:57.944 --> 02:48:57.954
- Exactly

02:48:57.954 --> 02:48:59.853
- ... and then basic behavior changes,

02:49:00.684 --> 02:49:05.624
habit changes that lead to a healthier
life. So I think, I think this kinda,

02:49:07.024 --> 02:49:10.684
you know, getting your life
together kind of process-

02:49:10.764 --> 02:49:14.564
... is intuitive that that would
have a positive effect on some

02:49:14.644 --> 02:49:18.084
percent of the population. But as you
get more serious and serious into the

02:49:18.564 --> 02:49:24.184
land of mental disorders, it starts
to be a little bit less conclusive.

02:49:24.584 --> 02:49:28.384
- Yes. I think that's an accurate
statement of what-- the way

02:49:28.524 --> 02:49:32.513
I read the literature. And
it's not a cure-all, but

02:49:32.564 --> 02:49:36.244
for some patients, this really
does seem to improve things

02:49:36.684 --> 02:49:40.504
quite a lot. And it does
make sense that you

02:49:40.544 --> 02:49:45.884
have developed poor ways of responding
to signals from the outside world,

02:49:46.784 --> 02:49:51.904
and they've become habitual, and
they've -- habits, as we know, are very

02:49:51.924 --> 02:49:52.904
hard to break.

02:49:53.804 --> 02:49:57.684
You know, there's a lot of homework, there's
a lot of exercises you have to do to try

02:49:57.744 --> 02:50:02.544
to reconfigure the ways
you deal with the world.

02:50:03.763 --> 02:50:07.444
And it's not for everybody, and it doesn't
work for everybody, but there's some

02:50:07.524 --> 02:50:13.824
set, subset of people for whom this
seems to have positive effects.

02:50:14.444 --> 02:50:19.144
- So this, this is the lay of the land.
We talked about some of the darkness.

02:50:20.064 --> 02:50:23.924
Lobotomies and so on. There
is some talk therapy ideas of

02:50:23.944 --> 02:50:27.844
psychoanalysis, and then there
is, from the clinical psychology

02:50:27.964 --> 02:50:36.174
side, cognitive behavioral therapy. Then
starts to emerge the psychopharmacology-

02:50:36.224 --> 02:50:39.464
...that challenges this whole
shebang of talk therapy,

02:50:39.704 --> 02:50:46.664
period. And can you talk about the
accidental origins of psychopharmacology-

02:50:47.004 --> 02:50:48.544
...that challenges this whole thing?

02:50:49.144 --> 02:50:52.704
- Hardly anybody had conceived of
the idea that drugs could be

02:50:52.844 --> 02:50:54.584
used to treat mental illness.

02:50:55.404 --> 02:50:59.084
Drugs had been used in the mental
hospitals back in the 19th and

02:50:59.224 --> 02:51:03.124
20th centuries, but they were
usually things that were used

02:51:03.184 --> 02:51:06.784
to control patients, to calm
them down. Opiates, for

02:51:06.824 --> 02:51:10.704
example various hypnotics
that would put people

02:51:10.804 --> 02:51:18.564
to sleep when they were extremely agitated,
and so forth. Sometimes marijuana was

02:51:18.604 --> 02:51:22.564
experimented with, as was alcohol
in the 19th century interestingly

02:51:22.604 --> 02:51:29.464
enough- ... given the revival of
psychedelics. But the idea that a drug might

02:51:29.904 --> 02:51:33.634
be used to actually attack
the underlying problem,

02:51:35.064 --> 02:51:41.694
it emerged by accident in the following
kind of way. The drug industry in general

02:51:43.248 --> 02:51:44.028
Had emerged

02:51:44.868 --> 02:51:48.508
mostly in Germany, where I'd mentioned
the most advanced medicine was being

02:51:48.548 --> 02:51:52.458
practiced in the late
19th century. From the

02:51:52.488 --> 02:51:58.328
chemical industry from, for example,
refining coal tar into different substances,

02:51:59.508 --> 02:52:04.088
that's where we got aspirin. That's
where we got a whole bunch of drugs.

02:52:05.088 --> 02:52:08.848
And gradually, the drug companies
become more organized. They

02:52:08.928 --> 02:52:14.788
differentiate themselves from all the quack
remedies that are around. And the war,

02:52:15.808 --> 02:52:19.568
I think greatly accelerates--
Again World War II

02:52:19.608 --> 02:52:23.588
greatly accelerates things, not
least because we discover a

02:52:23.648 --> 02:52:27.138
real magic bullet called
penicillin and then the other

02:52:27.208 --> 02:52:30.618
antibiotics that we
subsequently come across.

02:52:31.508 --> 02:52:35.328
Penicillin had been observed
by Alexander Fleming in

02:52:35.388 --> 02:52:39.308
his, famously, in his
lab in some Petri tube

02:52:39.348 --> 02:52:43.268
Petri dishes where bacteria
had been killed. Later was one

02:52:43.308 --> 02:52:46.968
of the co-winners of the Nobel Prize
and made a lot of capital out of that

02:52:47.008 --> 02:52:50.298
discovery. It was really Howard
Florey and his team at Oxford

02:52:50.948 --> 02:52:56.108
who developed the therapeutic
potential of penicillin.

02:52:57.648 --> 02:53:01.268
And actually, Florey flew
some of it in his jacket

02:53:01.388 --> 02:53:08.288
across the Atlantic to America, and it was
Americans that solved the critical thing.

02:53:09.288 --> 02:53:12.708
It took Florey and his team weeks to

02:53:12.768 --> 02:53:19.148
develop enough penicillin to treat a single
mouse. What America learned how to do

02:53:19.988 --> 02:53:23.948
was mass produce penicillin. And that

02:53:24.008 --> 02:53:27.268
was critical because it was
vital to the war effort

02:53:27.708 --> 02:53:31.688
obviously. And after the war, it was vital

02:53:31.728 --> 02:53:36.148
to the civilian population because it
was such an important breakthrough.

02:53:37.268 --> 02:53:44.148
So drugs, drug companies
grew fatter on that. They

02:53:44.168 --> 02:53:48.868
embarked on research to try to find new
substances they could use to treat things.

02:53:49.848 --> 02:53:53.487
Um, and a French company
named Rhône-Poulenc

02:53:55.668 --> 02:53:59.568
came across a chemical that had
actually been synthesized back in the

02:53:59.628 --> 02:54:03.038
1880s in Germany. It was an antihistamine,

02:54:03.208 --> 02:54:07.028
chlorpromazine. They didn't know
what to do with it, but other

02:54:07.088 --> 02:54:10.078
kinds of antihistamines in the
war had been used effectively,

02:54:11.208 --> 02:54:14.728
and they thought "Well, let's look and see
if we can find a market for this drug."

02:54:15.708 --> 02:54:19.628
And so they... The interesting
thing to remember in

02:54:19.688 --> 02:54:23.668
those days is that investigating
the property of new drugs was

02:54:23.677 --> 02:54:27.538
a Wild West phenomenon. There
were no controls. You could

02:54:27.568 --> 02:54:31.438
do whatever you wanted, and
you handed stuff out and said,

02:54:31.438 --> 02:54:35.308
"Why don't you try this and see
if it works?" Right? So Rhône-

02:54:35.368 --> 02:54:39.208
Poulenc did that. They
thought this might work as an

02:54:39.348 --> 02:54:43.028
antiemetic. If you have a
child who's prone to car

02:54:43.068 --> 02:54:45.348
sickness, you give them
an antiemetic drug.

02:54:46.268 --> 02:54:49.858
So that was one possibility. Another
possibility, it might work for

02:54:49.908 --> 02:54:53.808
eczema so that people didn't scratch
themselves. Well, there were a

02:54:53.868 --> 02:54:57.818
number of possibilities and somebody
said, "Well maybe it will work as

02:54:57.868 --> 02:55:01.788
an anesthetic potentiator,"
meaning if you gave

02:55:01.868 --> 02:55:05.788
some of these you need to use less
anesthetic or would act like a

02:55:05.848 --> 02:55:09.828
catalyst because it tended
to make you sleepy. If you

02:55:09.888 --> 02:55:13.708
read the leaflets on Dramamine,
one of the cautions is 'it'll

02:55:13.768 --> 02:55:16.748
make you sleepy, don't drive,' right? So

02:55:17.668 --> 02:55:21.688
why don't we give it to some surgeons
and see if they can find a use for it?

02:55:22.068 --> 02:55:28.228
Literally it's like that. And one of
the people who gets that drug is a

02:55:28.288 --> 02:55:32.228
lieutenant in the French
Navy, Henri Laborit,

02:55:33.488 --> 02:55:37.448
and he tries it as an anesthetic
potentiator and he also gives it to some

02:55:37.488 --> 02:55:43.148
of the patients who are awaiting surgery.
Normally when you're awaiting surgery,

02:55:43.688 --> 02:55:48.448
you're a little bit anxious. These
patients stop being anxious.

02:55:48.528 --> 02:55:52.368
Um, this was the era of
lobotomies and he wrote to

02:55:52.448 --> 02:55:56.038
one of his relatives who worked in one of
the Paris mental hospitals. He said, "This

02:55:56.188 --> 02:55:59.998
like, this works like a chemical
lobotomy. These people don't care

02:55:59.998 --> 02:56:01.248
anymore about their surgery."

02:56:01.438 --> 02:56:02.608
- And that's supposed to
be a good thing, right?

02:56:02.828 --> 02:56:07.708
- Well, lobotomy hadn't become the nasty
word it would become in a few years.

02:56:08.668 --> 02:56:12.468
So one of his colleagues
contacts Delay and Deniker, who

02:56:12.548 --> 02:56:16.418
work at Saint-Anne's which is the biggest
mental hospital in Paris and says,

02:56:16.418 --> 02:56:19.428
"Hey guys this might work on your

02:56:19.908 --> 02:56:23.508
psychiatric patients. You should
give it a try." And they do.

02:56:24.388 --> 02:56:28.288
When it doesn't work, they give larger dose
and when it doesn't work they give a large

02:56:28.328 --> 02:56:32.108
dose and sure enough patients

02:56:32.208 --> 02:56:35.968
stopped acting out. They stopped
smashing furniture. They

02:56:35.988 --> 02:56:42.728
become-- They're still sorta conscious
but they're less mobile and they're

02:56:43.728 --> 02:56:49.088
much calmer. And this is
why in the early stages

02:56:49.188 --> 02:56:52.908
Chlorpromazine which becomes
known as Largactil or

02:56:52.988 --> 02:56:55.908
Mighty Drug in Europe and Thorazine here,

02:56:56.728 --> 02:56:59.868
it becomes known as a major
tranquilizer because it has this

02:56:59.878 --> 02:57:03.588
tranquilizing effect. The
hospitals see it as a boon

02:57:03.628 --> 02:57:07.428
because it'll help control the patients.
They're not yet thinking of it

02:57:07.528 --> 02:57:11.418
as more than that. So that's
the accidental way it, way it's

02:57:11.468 --> 02:57:15.858
discovered. It comes to
North America via Quebec,

02:57:16.228 --> 02:57:19.148
where there's a
psychiatrist up there Heinz

02:57:19.228 --> 02:57:23.118
Lehmann who actually works in the
Protestant Hospital. Québec in those

02:57:23.128 --> 02:57:28.148
days is dominated by its Anglo
speaking elite who oppressed

02:57:29.798 --> 02:57:32.468
the French Canadians pretty mightily

02:57:33.408 --> 02:57:37.568
But because of the French connection,
he gets amounts of the drug.

02:57:50.808 --> 02:57:57.068
Rhône-Poulenc has had to sell the
rights to an American company.

02:57:58.428 --> 02:58:01.938
In those days, American physicians
don't trust European science or

02:58:02.008 --> 02:58:06.888
European medicine-- especially
European medicine. So

02:58:06.928 --> 02:58:10.458
Rhône-Poulenc sells the rights.
The first two drug companies it

02:58:10.488 --> 02:58:14.348
approaches say, "No thank you. This
doesn't look very interesting to us. I

02:58:14.368 --> 02:58:18.188
don't think there's much of a market."
And then SmithKline &amp; French buys it

02:58:18.268 --> 02:58:25.678
up and within two years, two million
people are taking this drug. It's a

02:58:25.788 --> 02:58:29.697
bonanza for them right?
Not an accident 'cause

02:58:29.748 --> 02:58:33.588
they put their best salesman
on the job of selling this

02:58:33.668 --> 02:58:37.448
and they realize the hospital
psychiatrists mostly aren't

02:58:37.528 --> 02:58:41.268
interested. They haven't yet
gotten to this idea of a chemical

02:58:41.488 --> 02:58:42.908
cure or a chemical treatment.

02:58:43.288 --> 02:58:44.828
- So it's the companies
that wake up to this?

02:58:44.888 --> 02:58:47.688
- The companies wake up, they sell it to the

02:58:48.148 --> 02:58:51.508
politicians. They go to the
state legislatures. They have

02:58:52.488 --> 02:58:56.948
moving pictures of an agitated
patient who turns calm, you know.

02:58:57.168 --> 02:59:00.528
- So this is already the
mechanism of big pharma?

02:59:00.748 --> 02:59:04.708
- Yeah. So it's starting to be big
pharma. It transforms Smith Kline

02:59:04.728 --> 02:59:08.608
&amp; French from a small operator into a
big company, and then of course once

02:59:08.688 --> 02:59:12.588
that's successful, it turns
out that chlorpromazine can

02:59:12.628 --> 02:59:16.468
be easily tweaked as a molecule, and so

02:59:16.528 --> 02:59:20.408
copycat drugs emerge on the
scene. So this happens in

02:59:20.448 --> 02:59:25.068
America in 1954. The
following year in 1955

02:59:25.908 --> 02:59:31.548
we get the so-called minor tranquilizers
being invented and brought to market.

02:59:31.988 --> 02:59:33.088
- This is Miltown.

02:59:33.488 --> 02:59:36.728
- Yeah, this is Miltown heavily
promoted by the first

02:59:37.568 --> 02:59:41.248
major television star of
the mid-'50s Milton Berle,

02:59:41.888 --> 02:59:47.088
who calls himself Uncle Miltown and
promotes the drug heavily on his show.

02:59:47.428 --> 02:59:50.728
- So these are mostly like
tranquilizer type of effects?

02:59:51.048 --> 02:59:54.368
- Those work really-- They're muscle
relaxant types of things and

02:59:55.008 --> 02:59:58.748
later on in the '60s we
get Valium and Librium

02:59:58.788 --> 03:00:02.578
coming on adding to or
replacing those, those first

03:00:02.628 --> 03:00:05.668
generation drugs. So you have
major tranquilizers, minor

03:00:05.708 --> 03:00:08.428
tranquilizers. Minor tranquilizers

03:00:08.548 --> 03:00:13.508
and guess what? The Freudians don't want
really anything to do with these drugs.

03:00:13.728 --> 03:00:14.508
- Right. Of course.

03:00:14.668 --> 03:00:17.828
- Uh, the hospital psychiatrist
wants to wake up to the

03:00:18.588 --> 03:00:25.238
usefulness of Thorazine and its analogues
do start using the drugs a lot,

03:00:26.128 --> 03:00:30.108
And that's really important,
and in the early '60s,

03:00:31.368 --> 03:00:34.928
they changed their name. Instead
of being major tranquilizers, they

03:00:34.968 --> 03:00:36.728
become antipsychotics.

03:00:37.548 --> 03:00:37.557
- Right.

03:00:37.557 --> 03:00:42.168
- So that suggests they actually
attack the underlying psychosis.

03:00:42.348 --> 03:00:45.068
- Is there any evidence of that for them?

03:00:45.148 --> 03:00:50.508
- They do change the forms of psychosis
in important ways, some good

03:00:51.328 --> 03:01:00.648
and some they fail to attack. So, um,
they reduce the agitation, they reduce

03:01:02.808 --> 03:01:06.758
the delusions and the hallucinations, the
things psychiatrists call the positive

03:01:06.848 --> 03:01:10.708
symptoms of schizophrenia
those, those they help

03:01:10.808 --> 03:01:14.768
with for not every patient
but for a significant

03:01:14.788 --> 03:01:17.548
number of patients, and they're
very -- That's very important.

03:01:17.708 --> 03:01:18.908
- Why are they called the positive?

03:01:19.248 --> 03:01:22.628
- The positive symptoms activate
things. They activate

03:01:22.748 --> 03:01:26.688
the hallucinations and the
delusions. Those are changing,

03:01:26.788 --> 03:01:30.528
and they're visible usually because if
somebody's deluded or hallucinating,

03:01:31.148 --> 03:01:36.748
you know about it pretty quickly... right?
The negative symptoms are things like

03:01:36.828 --> 03:01:41.988
apathy, loss of ability to interact
with people, poverty of language,

03:01:42.648 --> 03:01:46.888
lack of initiative. All of
those things are devastating.

03:01:47.248 --> 03:01:52.288
- Just to state out loud and clear that both
positive and negative affects so-called

03:01:53.188 --> 03:01:56.948
by psychologists of schizophrenia are
both devastating and both negative.

03:01:57.268 --> 03:02:00.168
- Yes, yes. Uh, I think that's right.

03:02:00.268 --> 03:02:02.068
- In the common parlance
of what negative means.

03:02:02.208 --> 03:02:05.968
- It's a very strange choice of language,
but it's been there for a long time,

03:02:06.508 --> 03:02:12.568
so it's embedded. Now, to the extent
antipsychotics work, they work on the positive

03:02:12.728 --> 03:02:13.268
symptoms.

03:02:14.448 --> 03:02:18.328
They either dull them down so you're still
hallucinating a bit and you're still

03:02:18.388 --> 03:02:21.568
delusional, but it's not--
it's very much less,

03:02:22.288 --> 03:02:28.208
and you are you're somewhat pacified.
You're less overtly disturbed.

03:02:29.608 --> 03:02:33.848
But the negative side of things,
not so much or not at all.

03:02:35.888 --> 03:02:42.338
Then on top of that, the drugs work for
some people and they don't work for

03:02:42.368 --> 03:02:47.948
others, and a significant number of psychotic
patients that are non-drug responders

03:02:49.588 --> 03:02:53.488
just as a significant number of people
with depression are not responsive to

03:02:53.498 --> 03:02:59.378
antidepressants. Moreover, initially
the enthusiasm for these drugs

03:03:00.268 --> 03:03:06.128
everybody neglects the fact that
they have serious side effects or

03:03:07.248 --> 03:03:13.228
many of them argue the side effects
are an essential part of the

03:03:13.288 --> 03:03:17.168
treatment and you just have to put up
with it. So what are we talking about

03:03:17.208 --> 03:03:20.848
when I say there are nasty side effects?

03:03:21.928 --> 03:03:25.628
Well among others, you may
become incurably restless.

03:03:26.518 --> 03:03:30.418
So you're constantly in motion, you're
moving around, you're never, never

03:03:30.488 --> 03:03:32.698
still. If you're in the
presence of somebody

03:03:33.708 --> 03:03:37.698
like that, it becomes unbearable
after a fairly short while, and

03:03:37.728 --> 03:03:40.948
it's unbearable often to the
person who can't control it.

03:03:42.568 --> 03:03:46.028
Other patients develop
Parkinson's disease symptoms.

03:03:47.268 --> 03:03:51.248
Is an awful affliction which
unfortunately affects a

03:03:51.308 --> 03:03:54.768
significant number of people each
year, and we don't have-- We have

03:03:55.308 --> 03:03:58.948
through L-DOPA a means to delay its impact

03:03:59.308 --> 03:04:03.488
but eventually that loses its efficacy
and unfortunately we don't have a cure

03:04:04.920 --> 03:04:08.840
So you have those. And then
perhaps the nastiest is what's

03:04:08.900 --> 03:04:10.400
called tardive dyskinesia.

03:04:11.640 --> 03:04:14.980
Tardive because it's late developing,
it doesn't happen right away,

03:04:15.840 --> 03:04:19.480
and dyskinesia because what
involves is jerky uncontrolled

03:04:19.560 --> 03:04:22.700
movements of the body and particularly
of the facial muscles. So

03:04:23.520 --> 03:04:27.080
your tongue will protrude, you'll
make strange noises, you'll

03:04:27.120 --> 03:04:30.999
twitch, you'll stagger about walking. If

03:04:31.060 --> 03:04:35.420
somebody like that is walking down the
street towards you, you will think

03:04:36.500 --> 03:04:40.300
there's a mentally ill person and you'll
cross to the other side of the street. And

03:04:40.340 --> 03:04:44.180
yes, they probably are a mentally
ill person but what you're seeing

03:04:44.260 --> 03:04:47.560
is the iatrogenic effects
of the drugs they're on.

03:04:47.660 --> 03:04:51.410
Okay? So that problem was ignored for

03:04:51.500 --> 03:04:55.460
about 20 years. A paper appeared
in Science by George Crane,

03:04:55.900 --> 03:04:59.570
a Maryland psychiatrist, in which he said,

03:05:00.050 --> 03:05:03.900
"We as a profession have been
ignoring this terrible problem."

03:05:05.120 --> 03:05:09.240
Yes, the drugs do some good, but
they're also creating a lot of harm,

03:05:10.080 --> 03:05:15.140
and we need to focus more on that. And
it took a few years, but by the '80s,

03:05:16.220 --> 03:05:20.360
I think the American Psychiatric Association
was very worried by that problem.

03:05:21.900 --> 03:05:27.960
And um, the drug companies were kind of
worried about it, but didn't have any obvious

03:05:28.000 --> 03:05:35.540
solution till towards the end of the '80s
something happened. Nineteen fifty-seven

03:05:35.840 --> 03:05:39.520
when the people were developing
copycat versions of Thorazine,

03:05:40.280 --> 03:05:44.220
clozapine was developed by a small
company which was subsequently bought

03:05:44.300 --> 03:05:49.800
up by a bigger company and introduced
in Europe. It never came to America

03:05:49.860 --> 03:05:56.680
because of two things. First, it tended
not to produce tardive dyskinesia,

03:05:57.620 --> 03:06:01.540
and at that time many in the profession
thought if you didn't get those symptoms

03:06:01.660 --> 03:06:05.500
you weren't attacking the
problem. Bizarre, right?

03:06:05.600 --> 03:06:10.610
Secondly in a significant
number of patients

03:06:11.460 --> 03:06:14.860
it destroyed the patient's white
blood cells and they died.

03:06:15.360 --> 03:06:19.320
Okay? Not a good outcome.
So it was quickly withdrawn

03:06:19.380 --> 03:06:24.600
from the market, and it never made it
to this side of the Atlantic. However,

03:06:25.580 --> 03:06:33.120
as this tardive dyskinesia problem became
more acute, there was an attempt to revive

03:06:34.020 --> 03:06:41.520
clozapine, and it turns out
it tends to work better in

03:06:41.600 --> 03:06:45.100
treatment-resistant cases, meaning
cases that don't respond to the

03:06:46.020 --> 03:06:49.160
other antipsychotics that we have.

03:06:49.900 --> 03:06:54.960
And it could be revived but at
the cost of weekly blood checks

03:06:56.160 --> 03:06:59.680
'cause you had to be very
careful if your white blood

03:06:59.740 --> 03:07:03.280
cells count started to
drop, you had to stop.

03:07:03.580 --> 03:07:07.770
Right? So it came to
market in the late '80s,

03:07:10.460 --> 03:07:16.720
and very quickly other drug companies
tried to find other compounds

03:07:18.300 --> 03:07:21.970
actually chemically not related
to it, but that's hardly the

03:07:22.000 --> 03:07:27.680
point. The whole class of drugs became
known as second generation antipsychotics.

03:07:28.040 --> 03:07:30.800
- So Clozapine, Risperdal--

03:07:31.000 --> 03:07:32.520
- Yes, those kinds of ones.

03:07:32.530 --> 03:07:32.860
- Zyprexa.

03:07:33.020 --> 03:07:33.750
- Zyprexa among others.

03:07:33.750 --> 03:07:34.360
- Zyprexa.

03:07:34.520 --> 03:07:38.330
- Yes. And so there were a number
of these, and they have actually

03:07:38.700 --> 03:07:40.620
-- Because they are chemically quite

03:07:41.520 --> 03:07:45.650
different from each other, but they're
classed together in the public mind and -

03:07:45.650 --> 03:07:46.680
...in the professional mind.

03:07:47.580 --> 03:07:51.360
They tend to have different side
effect profiles and slightly different

03:07:51.420 --> 03:07:56.100
modes of action and differing
levels of efficacy, I think.

03:07:56.260 --> 03:07:57.200
- Of course, there's not a real,

03:07:58.120 --> 03:08:02.860
at that time or even now, a real science
of like when you have a human come to you,

03:08:03.760 --> 03:08:06.600
what is the actual protocol of
how you figure out which to give?

03:08:07.100 --> 03:08:10.960
- Right. So in 2005 most of the-- I should

03:08:11.080 --> 03:08:14.980
say, once the Drugs Revolution
came along, increasingly

03:08:15.040 --> 03:08:18.930
the studies were funded by the drug
companies who owned the data and

03:08:19.000 --> 03:08:19.960
controlled the data--

03:08:20.180 --> 03:08:20.360
- Right

03:08:20.440 --> 03:08:24.420
- ...and only released what they chose to
release, what was helpful for them and

03:08:24.480 --> 03:08:25.900
not what wasn't.

03:08:25.940 --> 03:08:27.820
- And they were heavily marketing stuff.

03:08:28.060 --> 03:08:34.760
- Oh yeah, very heavily mar-- And then, to
get FDA approval of efficacy and safety you

03:08:34.800 --> 03:08:36.920
needed two trials.

03:08:37.760 --> 03:08:41.730
You might have conducted 15 trials
and 13 of them had been failures,

03:08:41.840 --> 03:08:45.720
but if you had two that worked,
that was enough. That's what you

03:08:45.800 --> 03:08:52.480
needed. That was the gold standard. So
2005, National Institutes of Mental Health

03:08:52.820 --> 03:08:56.060
funds this drug study,
not an industry study,

03:08:57.280 --> 03:09:01.160
and what it wants to look at,
the first generation drugs are

03:09:01.200 --> 03:09:04.760
now out of patent and they're
cheap. The second generation are

03:09:04.820 --> 03:09:10.500
patented and are quite expensive, ten
times as expensive in some cases.

03:09:11.420 --> 03:09:15.280
So we do something called
the CATIE Study, and it's

03:09:15.340 --> 03:09:18.460
published in the New England Journal
of Medicine. And there are two

03:09:19.020 --> 03:09:22.280
interesting findings. So you
have one first generation

03:09:22.340 --> 03:09:26.200
antipsychotic, one of those
very early drugs, and you

03:09:26.240 --> 03:09:30.360
have four more recent drugs
that you're looking at.

03:09:31.460 --> 03:09:37.310
And you ask a number of
questions. Are the new drugs more

03:09:37.360 --> 03:09:40.980
efficacious than the old drug? No.

03:09:41.820 --> 03:09:47.580
Hmm. Were patients willing to
tolerate these drugs? And that was a

03:09:47.940 --> 03:09:54.220
finding that I thought was much
underreported at the time. Between 67% and

03:09:54.360 --> 03:09:57.610
82% of the patients, depending
on which drug they were on,

03:09:58.100 --> 03:10:02.040
dropped out of the trial because either the
drug wasn't working or they couldn't stand

03:10:02.080 --> 03:10:05.840
the side effects. So that
tells you, these are our

03:10:05.860 --> 03:10:09.709
best drugs, you know, there
are real complications here.

03:10:10.344 --> 03:10:16.284
And then as we delve further into it,
do they have nasty side effects? Well,

03:10:17.344 --> 03:10:25.384
less tardive dyskinesia somewhat, but a
whole new set of side effects. If you take

03:10:27.584 --> 03:10:32.764
these pills, you will gain, in
most cases, a lot of weight,

03:10:35.204 --> 03:10:39.014
10, 20, 30, 40 pounds, 50 pounds. What

03:10:39.044 --> 03:10:43.034
does that do? That gives
you metabolic syndrome;

03:10:43.164 --> 03:10:47.284
it gives you diabetes; it
gives you heart trouble. So

03:10:48.464 --> 03:10:53.304
important to state when
we're looking at this 'cause

03:10:54.684 --> 03:11:00.364
there is no-- There is no free lunch
with medicine. If you take an aspirin,

03:11:01.824 --> 03:11:05.564
it may cause your stomach
to bleed, sometimes enough

03:11:05.624 --> 03:11:09.844
to really put you in serious
jeopardy. Most of the time it's fine.

03:11:10.904 --> 03:11:17.244
Um, everything... Every drug that's
brought to market has the main

03:11:17.284 --> 03:11:21.464
effect we're looking for and
some side effects which may be

03:11:23.144 --> 03:11:29.184
minimal for most people but for some people
maybe worse than that. So we've got to--

03:11:30.684 --> 03:11:33.434
It's one of these cost-benefit
analyses, right? You--

03:11:33.524 --> 03:11:35.094
You're getting some relief from your

03:11:36.384 --> 03:11:40.374
psychiatric symptoms, but
you're courting these other

03:11:40.404 --> 03:11:44.064
dangers that may or may not
arise, and going in, we don't

03:11:44.124 --> 03:11:49.884
know where you're gonna fall.
We don't know; trial and error.

03:11:49.924 --> 03:11:53.264
- You mean every individual that walks into
the picture, they don't know where on

03:11:53.404 --> 03:11:54.404
distribution they fall?

03:11:54.684 --> 03:11:59.664
- Right. And we have no biological markers
at the moment that would tell us.

03:12:00.644 --> 03:12:04.924
One or two of my friends in the,
um, psychiatric genetics industry,

03:12:05.764 --> 03:12:09.224
uh, if that's the right term for it,
but, you know, in that research area

03:12:09.764 --> 03:12:13.324
are saying, "Well, we haven't done
very well to find the genetic

03:12:13.584 --> 03:12:16.924
roots of mental illness," but maybe we can

03:12:17.804 --> 03:12:21.504
learn to distinguish which patients are
gonna respond well to drugs and which

03:12:21.824 --> 03:12:26.284
will, which shouldn't be given them
because they're not going to. Um,

03:12:27.144 --> 03:12:31.064
that's a promissory note. It's not
something that they actually can do now.

03:12:31.184 --> 03:12:35.364
It's just we hope we'll be able to do this,
and I hope they're able to do it too. But

03:12:36.304 --> 03:12:40.104
You know, you can't have an enormous
amount of confidence in that. Maybe

03:12:40.144 --> 03:12:43.844
it'll happen, or maybe some
other mechanism will emerge that

03:12:43.944 --> 03:12:47.744
allows us to see who the
responders and nonresponders are.

03:12:47.804 --> 03:12:51.694
Who's gonna suffer the worst side effects
if they're put on these medications? And

03:12:51.724 --> 03:12:58.384
The side effects do vary, so, um,
though a lot of what I've described is

03:12:58.504 --> 03:13:00.204
common to this class of drugs.

03:13:00.444 --> 03:13:03.784
- And sometimes it's hard to describe the
side effect. You're talking about the human

03:13:03.864 --> 03:13:07.024
mind. Yeah, yeah. So describing the
side effects, it's not like ble-

03:13:07.264 --> 03:13:10.524
bleeding or like diarrhea or-

03:13:10.644 --> 03:13:11.224
- Well, yeah.

03:13:11.304 --> 03:13:15.364
- Weight gain, that's describable, but
like the effect on your personality...

03:13:15.824 --> 03:13:22.704
- Yep, that's much harder, much harder. People,
you know, some of the patients who drop

03:13:22.804 --> 03:13:25.994
out drop out because they find

03:13:26.264 --> 03:13:30.184
the richness of their mental life is
completely gone, and they don't want

03:13:30.224 --> 03:13:35.344
to tolerate that. They'd sooner tolerate
some hallucinations. That's a hard one.

03:13:35.384 --> 03:13:38.624
- So there's a that picture, the
SSRIs that also came to be

03:13:38.644 --> 03:13:41.604
Prozac, Zoloft, Paxil during
that time. Yes, Paxil, all of

03:13:41.704 --> 03:13:45.424
that. Well again, they're the second
generation of antidepressants.

03:13:45.804 --> 03:13:49.444
Mm-hmm. So that's a complicated
story. Again, it's an

03:13:49.504 --> 03:13:52.644
accident. They're at treating
patients with advanced

03:13:52.744 --> 03:13:57.944
tuberculosis in the 1950s. Now, that's a
very unpleasant thing that's also gonna kill

03:13:58.024 --> 03:14:00.514
you, so tuberculosis, very

03:14:00.524 --> 03:14:04.704
advanced cases. You're coughing your lungs
up. You're depressed as all get out.

03:14:05.744 --> 03:14:09.604
And here we have two new
drugs, Iprsoltanto we can

03:14:09.704 --> 03:14:15.094
use that maybe will treat the condition
when you give it to the patients, and

03:14:15.144 --> 03:14:22.184
these depressed tuberculosis patients
start acting happy, dancing about. Their

03:14:22.244 --> 03:14:28.973
mood changes. Bingo! We've got something
that maybe we can use over here to

03:14:29.104 --> 03:14:34.884
treat depression. However,
the drug companies in circa

03:14:34.924 --> 03:14:41.844
1960 think of depression as a small market
because what they're talking about is the

03:14:41.904 --> 03:14:45.894
kind of melancholic psychotic
depressions that leads people

03:14:45.944 --> 03:14:51.504
into the mental hospital. And that's not an
insignificant group, but it's not a huge

03:14:51.624 --> 03:14:58.844
group. So it's really much later on that things
begin to change. Right now, it's fair to say

03:14:59.544 --> 03:15:05.984
Depression is the common cold of psychiatry.
It's, you know, it's abundant there, the

03:15:06.144 --> 03:15:11.924
depressive diagnosis, probably the most
commonly given among psychiatrists, so part of

03:15:12.004 --> 03:15:15.824
that is a deeper understanding of the
human mind, and a big part of that is

03:15:15.864 --> 03:15:19.304
probably the drug companies
convincing the world.

03:15:19.824 --> 03:15:23.184
- Well, it's transforming, yes.
So, there were big disputes for

03:15:23.244 --> 03:15:28.964
example, when DSM-5 was being
contemplated about whether grief,

03:15:29.904 --> 03:15:37.464
bereavement would count as a mental disorder,
as count as a form of depression if you

03:15:39.304 --> 03:15:44.604
lose a parent, if you lose,
even worse, a child, um

03:15:45.304 --> 03:15:49.624
It's a deeply-- how can I put
it? Deeply upsetting, yes.

03:16:13.756 --> 03:16:19.136
Some psychiatrists say yes and some no,
but it's an example of what happens

03:16:20.176 --> 03:16:23.716
in psychiatry, repeatedly, what
I would call diagnostic creep,

03:16:24.056 --> 03:16:27.936
which is a term, actually, an
Australian named Nick Haslam came

03:16:27.996 --> 03:16:31.696
up with. But it's a phenomenon
I described before that. You

03:16:31.776 --> 03:16:34.496
start with a core of unambiguous

03:16:34.936 --> 03:16:38.696
deviations from the norm
that are so serious that any

03:16:38.756 --> 03:16:42.336
competent member of the
culture knows that's that. But

03:16:43.016 --> 03:16:46.756
then it-- you begin to say, "Well,
there's this penumbra," you see, and

03:16:46.816 --> 03:16:53.096
just outside that core, there are people
who are also disturbed, not perhaps as

03:16:53.166 --> 03:16:57.066
sufficiently disturbed that you
actually recognize it, but it's

03:16:57.076 --> 03:17:04.416
happening. And so that has tended to happen
over and over again. Parents with an

03:17:04.476 --> 03:17:08.076
autistic child won't
necessarily agree with what I'm

03:17:08.196 --> 03:17:12.416
about to say, but the
chief editor of DSM-IV,

03:17:13.416 --> 03:17:17.236
Allen Frances, is convinced the
huge increase in the number of

03:17:17.276 --> 03:17:21.236
diagnoses of autism is more driven by the

03:17:21.276 --> 03:17:24.716
fact he loosened the criteria
for the diagnosis than it is

03:17:24.836 --> 03:17:29.106
because there are more actual
cases. Autism used to be a fairly

03:17:29.936 --> 03:17:33.796
rare phenomenon, and now sadly it's
a very broad one. And I understand

03:17:33.816 --> 03:17:37.476
why parents react very
negatively to that because the

03:17:37.496 --> 03:17:41.206
diagnosis is the key to all
sorts of social supports and

03:17:41.416 --> 03:17:45.236
educational supports and all the
rest, and they're dealing with a

03:17:45.296 --> 03:17:50.836
child who is extremely difficult. But
there is a real difference between

03:17:51.736 --> 03:17:55.296
the most severe forms of
autism where people lose

03:17:55.436 --> 03:17:59.276
capacity to speak and
very often any ability to

03:17:59.336 --> 03:18:01.636
interact with other people and so forth,

03:18:02.576 --> 03:18:06.516
and the other cases that are of
a still serious but milder sort.

03:18:07.136 --> 03:18:10.246
Diagnostic creep is not driven just by

03:18:10.696 --> 03:18:14.916
psychiatric imperialism, the desire
for more territory, more patients.

03:18:16.256 --> 03:18:20.186
It's driven as well by
patients and people and,

03:18:20.236 --> 03:18:24.036
and they resist it being
pushed back very often.

03:18:24.436 --> 03:18:28.316
So families have formed
organizations like NAMI, but

03:18:28.496 --> 03:18:32.456
those aren't the mentally ill. Those are the
family members of the mentally ill, and there

03:18:32.466 --> 03:18:35.976
are differences in the perspectives
and the interests of the family

03:18:36.076 --> 03:18:39.996
members and the patients- ...
And that's easy to forget.

03:18:40.036 --> 03:18:42.496
- I think one of the things
you talk about is,

03:18:43.556 --> 03:18:48.096
uh, I mean, the quote you had
was use the word madness.

03:18:48.476 --> 03:18:53.406
But that madness or
mental health maladies,

03:18:54.356 --> 03:18:57.676
if you're suffering from them,
it's a deeply lonely experience.

03:18:58.516 --> 03:19:04.896
And then if you're around somebody suffering
from it, it's a very social experience.

03:19:05.776 --> 03:19:09.696
- Yeah. Yeah, this is actually --
that's a paraphrase of -- I was

03:19:09.776 --> 03:19:13.696
quoting one of my friends and fellow
historians of psychiatry, Michael

03:19:13.716 --> 03:19:17.476
MacDonald. The most solitary of
afflictions for the sufferer and the

03:19:17.536 --> 03:19:21.296
most social of maladies for those
around them. And there's lots of

03:19:21.356 --> 03:19:23.176
ways in which you can see

03:19:24.316 --> 03:19:28.276
that very powerfully, and that's why
I think there are a ton of people who

03:19:28.356 --> 03:19:32.976
suffer in various ways from, from mental
disturbances of one sort and another.

03:19:33.876 --> 03:19:37.776
But the effects aren't confined
to them. They extend out

03:19:37.816 --> 03:19:41.316
to everybody else around
them, and that's really

03:19:41.376 --> 03:19:45.186
powerful. So we had this
first generation there were

03:19:45.236 --> 03:19:47.716
actually two different
kinds of antidepressants,

03:19:48.616 --> 03:19:51.936
MAOIs as they're called in the trade, and

03:19:51.996 --> 03:19:55.676
tricyclics which were called
tricyclics 'cause they had a

03:19:55.756 --> 03:19:59.316
third ring of a certain
element in their molecule.

03:20:00.116 --> 03:20:03.506
Those had as I say, a limited market, but

03:20:03.596 --> 03:20:07.205
also there were complications
associated with

03:20:07.256 --> 03:20:10.596
them that it was easy to
overdose and die. And so

03:20:10.936 --> 03:20:14.876
giving a depressed patient a bunch
of pills that if they took too many

03:20:14.916 --> 03:20:18.736
of them would kill them was
tricky. They also could

03:20:18.796 --> 03:20:24.656
kill you another way because for
example, you couldn't -- certain dietary

03:20:25.476 --> 03:20:28.376
items, cheese, cured meats,

03:20:29.276 --> 03:20:34.636
in combination with them were very, very
health threatening, maybe even fatal.

03:20:36.456 --> 03:20:38.676
Now, in the late '80s,

03:20:40.596 --> 03:20:45.596
the drug companies came across
a new class of antidepressants

03:20:46.396 --> 03:20:50.136
that didn't have those side effects.
As we'll see, they had other side

03:20:50.176 --> 03:20:54.156
effects, but not those. And
the most famous of those

03:20:54.236 --> 03:20:57.896
was Prozac. There were
a class of drugs called

03:20:58.716 --> 03:21:02.496
SSRIs, and again forgive the
acronyms, but what it means is

03:21:02.516 --> 03:21:05.416
selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors.

03:21:06.776 --> 03:21:10.316
Serotonin is something manufactured
in our bodies, actually

03:21:10.776 --> 03:21:14.156
in our digestive system, and
it performs a variety of

03:21:14.216 --> 03:21:19.376
functions in the body. But it's also one
of the neurotransmitters in our brains.

03:21:20.496 --> 03:21:25.376
And the way these drugs worked was by

03:21:27.096 --> 03:21:32.576
slowing the reuptake of serotonin
in the brain, which was

03:21:33.436 --> 03:21:37.136
marketed by the drug companies as the

03:21:37.196 --> 03:21:40.206
solution to depression. That
when you got depressed,

03:21:41.156 --> 03:21:44.796
it was because you didn't have enough
serotonin in your brain, and these pills

03:21:45.116 --> 03:21:53.156
solved that problem. It was like, well,
Tipper Gore, Al Gore's ex-wife, was like many

03:21:53.196 --> 03:21:57.096
politicians' wives, depressed
for reasons I fully understand

03:21:57.596 --> 03:22:01.356
or at least partially understand. And she

03:22:01.396 --> 03:22:05.276
went to her psychiatrist and that
was one of the things she was given,

03:22:05.496 --> 03:22:12.516
and she became one of the big
public advocates for SSRIs which

03:22:12.596 --> 03:22:15.536
still are the most
prescribed antidepressants.

03:22:15.956 --> 03:22:17.856
- Do they work? And what
are the side effects?

03:22:18.076 --> 03:22:21.992
- Yeah. So here's where we get
into slippery territory. Every

03:22:22.032 --> 03:22:26.092
time you do a controlled study
of these antidepressants,

03:22:27.432 --> 03:22:34.032
they beat placebo in a
statistically significant margin,

03:22:35.012 --> 03:22:38.912
but not necessarily in a
clinically significant margin.

03:22:38.992 --> 03:22:42.972
And this is when you see drug
ads, be very careful. This one

03:22:43.072 --> 03:22:45.272
significantly improves X or Y.

03:22:46.492 --> 03:22:49.932
Does that mean clinically or does
it mean statistically? Because

03:22:51.012 --> 03:22:54.552
the fact-- if you -- when
we measure improvement with

03:22:54.632 --> 03:22:57.891
depression, we tend to use
rating scales of various sorts.

03:22:58.832 --> 03:23:06.332
And if you improve on one of the major
scales that's used by one or two points

03:23:07.012 --> 03:23:10.652
on a 60-point scale, it
may be enough to show

03:23:10.812 --> 03:23:14.592
statistical significance. These drugs
look a little better than placebo,

03:23:15.452 --> 03:23:20.332
but doesn't really affect your quality
of life much. And for most patients,

03:23:21.192 --> 03:23:26.332
antidepressants are marginally better

03:23:27.512 --> 03:23:32.512
than placebo, but a lot of the
effect is the placebo effect.

03:23:32.602 --> 03:23:39.432
And they come with very difficult side
effects. A lot of people describe them as

03:23:39.572 --> 03:23:42.412
numbing drugs. They--
...flatten everything out,

03:23:43.232 --> 03:23:46.272
So you can't experience the
highs and lows that normally

03:23:46.592 --> 03:23:50.782
we take as part of human
experience. And then, um,

03:23:51.672 --> 03:23:55.092
the numbing extends elsewhere in
your body. In particular, lots

03:23:55.152 --> 03:24:02.232
of patients find a complete
loss of libido. They...

03:24:03.492 --> 03:24:07.452
They can't get an erection.
They can't climax if they're a

03:24:07.492 --> 03:24:09.722
woman. Their sex life just goes away,

03:24:10.872 --> 03:24:14.452
and sometimes it doesn't come
back after you stop the drugs.

03:24:15.372 --> 03:24:19.342
Next layer of problems, getting off
the drugs for some people turns

03:24:19.432 --> 03:24:26.252
out to be hell on wheels. They get worse
depression than they had before. They

03:24:26.312 --> 03:24:30.932
get a feel-- uh, terrible feelings. Their
brain is sparking something's wrong.

03:24:31.832 --> 03:24:35.792
And so many patients find themselves
trapped on those drugs for

03:24:35.852 --> 03:24:39.432
a long time, and we don't know what
the effects of that are gonna be.

03:24:39.692 --> 03:24:43.582
So it's a very mixed picture,
you know. That's why I

03:24:43.612 --> 03:24:46.462
think groups like NICE
in England are saying,

03:24:47.972 --> 03:24:54.621
"Use CBT as the first line,
not drugs." But again,

03:24:55.932 --> 03:24:59.652
it's also fair to say, I think
what we find and there was a

03:24:59.692 --> 03:25:02.852
recent Lancet study that I think
was revealing on this point.

03:25:04.712 --> 03:25:08.612
It's like this with both antipsychotics
and antidepressants. You have

03:25:08.672 --> 03:25:12.512
a group of people who respond
pretty well, and the side

03:25:12.552 --> 03:25:16.892
effects for them are bearable or even
they don't experience them. That's great.

03:25:18.672 --> 03:25:22.412
You have a group of people, and it's
significant. With depressed patients,

03:25:22.532 --> 03:25:27.872
we're talking 40 or north of forty
percent who aren't responding. Okay?

03:25:29.632 --> 03:25:33.312
So drugs aren't doing anything for
them, and they're running a risk.

03:25:34.572 --> 03:25:38.592
And then in the middle, you have a
group of people who get some positive

03:25:39.552 --> 03:25:47.242
improvement, but they also get side
effects, and that's where, you know, this

03:25:47.272 --> 03:25:50.902
cost-benefit analysis, if we can call
it that, comes into play, and it's

03:25:51.012 --> 03:25:53.832
very difficult. And the
problem is going in,

03:25:54.832 --> 03:25:57.312
you don't know which group
you're gonna fall in.

03:25:57.652 --> 03:26:01.652
And your, and your doctor doesn't know
which group you're gonna fall in. And

03:26:02.992 --> 03:26:08.472
more importantly, one of the things
the Drugs Revolution did was it moved

03:26:09.452 --> 03:26:12.432
the diagnosis and the
prescribing of things

03:26:13.452 --> 03:26:17.332
away from psychiatry
alone, so many of these

03:26:17.372 --> 03:26:21.152
things, particularly antidepressants,
are dispensed by primary

03:26:21.252 --> 03:26:23.312
care docs, not psychiatrists.

03:26:23.892 --> 03:26:28.692
- Let's zoom out. We did say
that there's a real crisis.

03:26:30.052 --> 03:26:33.742
From an individual perspective suffering
from psychosis or suffering from

03:26:33.792 --> 03:26:38.792
depression- ... What are
you supposed to do? What

03:26:39.272 --> 03:26:43.452
works, and what is the hope for the
future in the next ten, twenty out?

03:26:43.952 --> 03:26:51.612
- So you probably need to try the drugs
because the suffering is very intense.

03:26:52.312 --> 03:26:55.782
But you need to be aware of things,
and you need a clinician who's

03:26:55.812 --> 03:27:03.292
monitoring very carefully. More generally,
psychiatric research needs to broaden.

03:27:04.312 --> 03:27:09.072
We have spent all our monies on drugs,

03:27:10.092 --> 03:27:13.952
on neuroscience, and on
genetics, but there are

03:27:13.992 --> 03:27:17.372
other things we could do
that would improve the lives

03:27:17.572 --> 03:27:24.552
of families and patients more immediately.
So we need to spend some time on the

03:27:24.572 --> 03:27:28.452
psychosocial dimensions of
mental illness and to allow

03:27:28.512 --> 03:27:35.552
psychiatrists to build careers in those
fields. One of the problems if you're an

03:27:35.612 --> 03:27:40.072
academic psychiatrist, your whole

03:27:40.892 --> 03:27:44.272
future is dependent on you
bringing in grant monies,

03:27:44.952 --> 03:27:48.752
and there aren't grant monies
available to study: Are there better

03:27:48.812 --> 03:27:52.712
ways we could cope with the problem
of homelessness? Are there better

03:27:52.812 --> 03:27:56.732
ways we could ease the problem for
families who are having to cope

03:27:56.772 --> 03:27:58.812
with somebody in their
midst who's hallucinating?

03:28:00.352 --> 03:28:03.192
That sort of thing, I think, would help.

03:28:03.792 --> 03:28:07.752
- What about talk therapy on both
the CBT, cognitive behavioral

03:28:07.812 --> 03:28:11.472
therapy, and psychoanalysis?
We left psychoanalysis in this

03:28:11.532 --> 03:28:14.072
place where society left it behind.

03:28:14.352 --> 03:28:17.312
- I think it's become a
niche product now. Only,

03:28:18.252 --> 03:28:22.192
only the very wealthy can afford
to do it, and some of them

03:28:22.252 --> 03:28:26.502
indeed do make use of it, and some
of them claim that it helps them.

03:28:27.004 --> 03:28:30.904
- Oh, do you think there's future
in it? I will add an extra-- I

03:28:30.924 --> 03:28:38.144
would add an extra bit to that. Carefully
is with the advent and the rapid

03:28:38.224 --> 03:28:41.834
improvement of artificial
intelligence systems-

03:28:41.904 --> 03:28:49.324
...that are able to communicate with
individual humans and learn a lot about them,

03:28:50.264 --> 03:28:53.964
and have a conversation about
the deepest secrets that you

03:28:54.024 --> 03:28:57.584
sometimes actually would even be
uncomfortable telling even a therapist.

03:28:58.764 --> 03:29:05.904
That starts to go into the realm of
Freud and Jung and psychoanalysis.

03:29:05.933 --> 03:29:09.424
- Well, yes, and we did see actually COVID

03:29:11.104 --> 03:29:14.904
with the isolation that it produced and
the fact people couldn't go to their

03:29:14.924 --> 03:29:21.404
therapists directly. We did see
the rise of some of this distant

03:29:23.184 --> 03:29:27.064
learning of this, and some of
it may indeed be mechanizable

03:29:27.124 --> 03:29:30.694
in the way you described.
It's very important

03:29:31.564 --> 03:29:35.464
that psychiatrists broaden
their perspective on

03:29:35.524 --> 03:29:39.204
these things, and some
already have. I think

03:29:39.244 --> 03:29:43.914
public policy is in a mess when it
comes to serious mental illness,

03:29:44.824 --> 03:29:49.484
but I'm pessimistic about fixing that
'cause it would cost bunches of money.

03:29:50.324 --> 03:29:53.374
If we were talking about something
where we could cure people,

03:29:53.944 --> 03:29:57.864
transform them from, as they used
to say, tax eaters to taxpayers,

03:29:58.704 --> 03:30:02.544
there'd be incentive to do it.
But the honest answer is right

03:30:02.584 --> 03:30:06.264
now that's not where we are. And so we

03:30:06.604 --> 03:30:12.294
face dilemmas. I think helping people as
much as we can with social supports --And,

03:30:13.984 --> 03:30:17.714
you know, social interaction
is tremendously important to

03:30:17.744 --> 03:30:21.654
people's mental health. If they lack it,
if they're lonely, if they're isolated,

03:30:22.484 --> 03:30:29.464
it does bad things to people. So somehow
providing that kind of support, providing

03:30:29.504 --> 03:30:33.444
some sense of agency to
people who often lack it

03:30:33.564 --> 03:30:37.503
would be very helpful.
Uh, I hope somebody has a

03:30:37.524 --> 03:30:39.824
breakthrough and produces
a better drug because

03:30:40.684 --> 03:30:44.484
unlike some people, I would be
astonished if the major forms

03:30:44.564 --> 03:30:48.144
of mental illness didn't have a
biological component to them.

03:30:48.564 --> 03:30:52.244
I don't think that's ever gonna be the
whole story, but it's going to be an

03:30:52.284 --> 03:30:56.024
important part of the story,
and therefore, you know, the

03:30:56.104 --> 03:30:59.724
fact that major drug companies have
abandoned research in this area,

03:31:00.504 --> 03:31:04.464
they've been bad actors in
many ways, but the drugs have

03:31:04.544 --> 03:31:07.853
had some positive effects, and
the fact that there's no research

03:31:08.484 --> 03:31:16.104
being done by the people with the most
money to develop better treatments is well,

03:31:16.304 --> 03:31:20.344
one word for it would be depressing.
You know? That's not what we wanna see.

03:31:20.584 --> 03:31:26.924
- So the path forward is a mix- ....of
continued research on drugs, but from a

03:31:26.964 --> 03:31:30.664
patient perspective, extreme
caution in use of those drugs.

03:31:31.184 --> 03:31:34.804
Uh, talk therapy, whether
it's CBT or psychoanalysis,

03:31:35.894 --> 03:31:39.864
further investigation research on
that front. Then the psychosocial

03:31:39.904 --> 03:31:42.084
component of social family-

03:31:42.094 --> 03:31:45.344
.....people around you, less
loneliness, investigating how

03:31:45.944 --> 03:31:51.504
from a cultural social perspective and
from a public policy perspective, can we

03:31:52.324 --> 03:31:55.904
increase the amount of social
connections that people who

03:31:56.044 --> 03:31:58.324
suffer have and all of that together?

03:31:58.844 --> 03:32:02.744
- Yes. And breakthrough with
people who have a very hard time

03:32:02.804 --> 03:32:06.524
making those connections or who've
lost the ability to make them.

03:32:07.484 --> 03:32:08.404
- If we can zoom out,

03:32:09.324 --> 03:32:13.314
looking back at this rich history of
human beings, and we did look at the

03:32:13.404 --> 03:32:17.204
darkness, but I think there's
a very large number of people

03:32:18.204 --> 03:32:22.744
that want to help those who suffer. So,

03:32:23.864 --> 03:32:27.564
looking at the history of
people trying to figure out-

03:32:27.573 --> 03:32:32.573
how to help those who suffer, what gives
you hope about our future? A real hope

03:32:33.724 --> 03:32:37.644
for the future of psychiatry that we
can actually help people who suffer.

03:32:39.644 --> 03:32:49.144
- Well, I think we have a profession
with many very well-meaning people

03:32:50.184 --> 03:32:54.804
who see the suffering on a
day-to-day, face-to-face basis.

03:32:56.124 --> 03:33:02.544
And I like to think that
among them will be people who

03:33:02.644 --> 03:33:06.324
move this thing forward.
And the fact is we

03:33:06.444 --> 03:33:10.064
have moved things forward. We
have tended to dwell a lot on

03:33:10.804 --> 03:33:14.464
some of the very unsavory
aspects of the past,

03:33:15.324 --> 03:33:19.844
and even some of the drawbacks of what
we have available to us in the present.

03:33:20.784 --> 03:33:23.724
But it is important to see at times

03:33:24.984 --> 03:33:28.344
highly motivated people have
been able to do very good

03:33:28.384 --> 03:33:33.814
things to help people with
these conditions. And there is,

03:33:34.744 --> 03:33:39.054
I think, hope in the future
that we will see more of that,

03:33:39.944 --> 03:33:47.594
Than has been the case. And that
involves, in some senses, a shift in the

03:33:47.664 --> 03:33:51.844
mentality of a whole
profession in, in a more

03:33:52.944 --> 03:33:57.424
caring kind of direction or less
technocratic kind of direction, less

03:33:58.464 --> 03:34:05.684
reliance on something as simple as giving
people pills, because that can help,

03:34:07.064 --> 03:34:10.484
but it's clearly not gonna be
the solution to the whole thing.

03:34:11.014 --> 03:34:17.864
We need a system that is
aware of and catering to the,

03:34:17.884 --> 03:34:21.784
suffering that people experience
and finds ways, if they

03:34:21.864 --> 03:34:27.504
can't eliminate it entirely, to
ameliorate it in ways that people will

03:34:28.548 --> 03:34:33.028
sense is actually of help
to them. It's a, it's a

03:34:34.308 --> 03:34:37.568
very difficult area,
this one. It's one that,

03:34:38.268 --> 03:34:44.408
touches some of the most profound
aspects of ourselves as human beings

03:34:45.208 --> 03:34:50.248
and I think as difficult
as this problem is

03:34:51.588 --> 03:34:55.048
it's very important not to be
cynical. Not to give up hope,

03:34:55.208 --> 03:34:59.108
not to deny the possibility of progress,

03:34:59.148 --> 03:35:03.128
because that's always
there. And has happened and

03:35:03.908 --> 03:35:08.008
I hope will happen with increasing
pace in the years ahead of us.

03:35:09.248 --> 03:35:11.008
- And if you're listening to these words

03:35:11.908 --> 03:35:16.488
and you're right now in this moment of your
life where you yourself are suffering,

03:35:18.668 --> 03:35:24.388
please know we're with you. We're
in this together. Stay strong.

03:35:25.028 --> 03:35:30.988
There's hope. Legitimately, as one
human to another, I love you. Brother,

03:35:31.108 --> 03:35:33.328
sister, if you're listening
to this, stay strong.

03:35:34.628 --> 03:35:37.928
Andrew, thank you for this incredible
work of history that you do.

03:35:39.108 --> 03:35:42.728
Incredible work of raising
awareness, stepping into a

03:35:42.808 --> 03:35:46.788
difficult topic, and trying to
find the wisdom, the insights

03:35:46.808 --> 03:35:49.768
in it. And thank you for this
incredible conversation today.

03:35:50.508 --> 03:35:52.768
- Lex, thank you for having me, and

03:35:54.008 --> 03:35:57.828
I felt you were remarkably
prepared to push me in various

03:35:57.908 --> 03:36:01.508
directions. So you'd
obviously done a lot of

03:36:01.518 --> 03:36:05.838
preparation to get this in
the right frame. And I hope,

03:36:06.928 --> 03:36:10.768
that people will get something positive

03:36:10.888 --> 03:36:14.848
from this conversation along with
some of the darkness we've inevitably

03:36:14.888 --> 03:36:16.208
had to talk about.

03:36:17.208 --> 03:36:21.168
- Thank you for listening to this conversation with
Andrew Scull. To support this podcast, please

03:36:21.208 --> 03:36:25.008
check out our sponsors in the description,
where you can also find links to contact me,

03:36:25.408 --> 03:36:28.958
ask questions, give
feedback, and so on. And

03:36:28.958 --> 03:36:35.448
now let me leave you with some words from
Friedrich Nietzsche: "To live is to suffer,

03:36:36.528 --> 03:36:39.868
To survive is to find
meaning in the suffering."

03:36:41.128 --> 03:36:44.148
Thank you for listening, and
I hope to see you next time.

